const FLAGGED = [{"dish":{"slug":"tamparuli-mee","name":"Tamparuli Mee","category":"noodles","serving":"1 plate","servingGrams":340,"calories":630,"protein":26,"carbs":65,"fat":28,"metaDescription":"Tamparuli Mee has around 630 calories per plate. Springy handmade egg noodles with siew yoke, char siew and chun kien. Here's what drives the numbers.","summary":"Sabah's handmade springy yellow noodles fried in dark soy sauce, topped with roasted pork belly, BBQ pork, egg roll and scrambled egg, a kedai kopi classic from Tamparuli town.","answer":"A plate of Tamparuli Mee comes in at around 630 calories for a standard 340g serve. That is for the classic dry version with handmade egg noodles, siew yoke (roasted pork belly), char siew, chun kien (egg roll), and scrambled egg. It is a solid, filling plate and not something you would eat on a deficit without thinking about it, but it is also not some obscene blowout. The 630 figure is a ballpark estimate since hard nutrition data for this dish is basically nonexistent, so take it as a reasonable guide rather than gospel.","realTalk":["Tamparuli Mee is a fried noodle dish from Tamparuli, a small town about an hour from Kota Kinabalu in Sabah. For anyone not from Sabah: the noodles here are handmade, thick, springy egg noodles, closer in texture to a good wantan mee noodle but chewier, and they hold the dark soy sauce coating really well. The full plate comes with siew yoke (roasted pork belly), char siew (BBQ pork), and chun kien (a Sabah-style egg roll), all sitting on top. It is not halal. The 630 calorie estimate for a standard plate is exactly that, an estimate, because nobody has actually lab-tested this dish and we are working backwards from the components. The real number could sit anywhere from 580 to 700 depending on how heavy-handed the cook is with oil and how thick they slice the siew yoke. Take it as a reasonable ballpark, not a precise count.","The biggest calorie drivers on this plate are the noodles themselves at around 315 calories and the siew yoke at about 145 calories for just 50g. Pork belly is fatty by nature, so if you get a generous stall that loads it up, you can easily push past 700 calories. The scrambled egg and stir-fry oil together add another 90 calories, which sounds low but oil accumulates fast in a wok. If you are watching the numbers, the practical moves are: eat the standard portion rather than upsizing (saves you roughly 120 calories right there), ask for less oil if the stall allows it, and load up on the veg portion since that whole 70g of greens is only contributing about 40 calories. Swapping siew yoke for a leaner protein like steamed chicken, if the stall offers it, would also knock off 50 to 80 calories without making the plate feel empty."],"lighter":["Stick to the standard portion and skip the upsized plate, which saves around 120 calories","Ask for less oil when ordering, wok frying adds significant calories and some stalls are more generous than others","Go easy on or skip the siew yoke topping if you want the biggest single calorie reduction, pork belly at 145 calories per 50g is the heaviest item on the plate","Eat the vegetables first so you fill up on the lowest-calorie part before reaching for more noodles","Skip sugary drinks on the side, a kopi susu or teh tarik adds another 100 to 150 calories to what is already a substantial meal"],"variants":[{"name":"Dry (standard, dark soy sauce)","calories":630,"note":"Classic Tamparuli Mee with siew yoke, char siew, chun kien and scrambled egg"},{"name":"With extra pork belly / larger portion","calories":750,"note":"Generous siew yoke topping or upsized plate common at kedai kopi in Tamparuli town"}],"components":[{"name":"Handmade egg noodles (fried)","grams":180,"calories":315},{"name":"Siew yoke (roasted pork belly)","grams":50,"calories":145},{"name":"Char siew and chun kien (BBQ pork and egg roll)","grams":50,"calories":100},{"name":"Scrambled egg and stir-fry oil","grams":40,"calories":90},{"name":"Vegetables and dark soy sauce","grams":70,"calories":40}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Tamparuli Mee?","a":"A standard plate of Tamparuli Mee has around 630 calories for approximately 340g. That covers the handmade egg noodles, siew yoke, char siew, chun kien and scrambled egg. An upsized or extra-pork version can push up to 750 calories."},{"q":"What is Tamparuli Mee?","a":"Tamparuli Mee is a fried noodle dish from Tamparuli town in Sabah, Malaysia. It uses thick handmade springy egg noodles tossed in dark soy sauce, topped with siew yoke (roasted pork belly), char siew (BBQ pork) and chun kien (egg roll). It is a non-halal dish commonly found at Chinese kedai kopi in Sabah."},{"q":"Is Tamparuli Mee healthy?","a":"It is not a low-calorie dish at 630 calories a plate, but it is not a disaster either. You get 26g of protein which is decent, and the portion is filling. The fat count of 28g is the part to watch, driven mainly by the pork belly and wok oil. If you are on a calorie deficit, it fits fine as a main meal as long as you keep the rest of the day lighter."}],"related":["tuaran-mee","beaufort-mee","kolo-mee"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The 630 kcal headline is plausible for this dish, but the component breakdown on the page sums to 690 kcal (315 + 145 + 100 + 90 + 40), not 630. The 60-calorie gap is an internal arithmetic inconsistency that should be reconciled. Either adjust the components to sum to 630, or raise the headline to ~690.","factualConcerns":["The calorie breakdown components sum to 690 kcal (315 noodles + 145 siew yoke + 100 char siew/chun kien + 90 egg/oil + 40 veg/sauce = 690) but the stated total is 630 kcal. This is a 60-calorie internal inconsistency within the same page."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Either reduce the component subtotals to sum to 630 (e.g. reduce the noodles line from 315 to 255, which would be more accurate for 180g of egg noodles without heavy oil), or raise the headline figure to 690 kcal. The noodle line at 315 kcal for 180g fried egg noodles is the most likely over-estimate: plain egg noodles are roughly 140-150 kcal per 100g cooked, so 180g would be around 255-270 kcal before stir-fry oil. Dropping that line to 260 kcal brings the total to 635, close enough to call 630.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"bosou","name":"Bosou","category":"lauk","serving":"2 tbsp condiment portion","servingGrams":30,"calories":35,"protein":3,"carbs":4,"fat":1,"metaDescription":"Bosou is around 60 calories per 2-tbsp serving. Sabah fermented raw fish condiment, pungent and eaten in tiny amounts with rice.","summary":"Bosou is a traditional Kadazan-Dusun fermented condiment made from raw fish or meat, cooked rice, and pangi seed, eaten in small spoonfuls alongside plain rice.","answer":"Two tablespoons of bosou (about 30g) comes in at roughly 35 calories, with 3g protein, 4g carbs, and 1g fat. It is a condiment, not a main dish, so you are realistically eating a tiny spoonful or two with your rice, which means the calorie hit is almost nothing. The fermented fish and lacto-fermented rice do most of the nutritional work here, with pangi seed and salt adding almost zilch in calories.","realTalk":["Bosou is a Kadazan-Dusun preserved food from Sabah, and if you have never heard of it, think of it as the Borneo answer to budu or belacan, except way more intense. Raw freshwater fish (or wild boar meat) gets mixed with cooked rice and pangi seed, then left to ferment for days or weeks until it turns sour and pungent. You eat it in small amounts with plain white rice, not as a main dish. The calories are almost laughably low at around 35 per 2-tablespoon serving, and that is honestly the right way to eat it anyway because the flavour is strong enough that nobody is piling on three ladles. Worth flagging: the wild boar and pork variants (bosou daging) are non-halal. These numbers are a ballpark estimate since hard nutritional data for bosou is basically nonexistent, so treat the figures as a reasonable educated guess rather than lab-tested gospel.","What actually changes the calories here is mostly the protein source and how much you eat. Bosou ikan (fish version) sits at about 35 calories per 30g serving because freshwater fish is lean. Swap to bosou daging with wild boar or pork, and you are looking at closer to 50 calories for the same 30g because red meat carries more fat. The bambangan variant (wild mango added to the fish bosou) lands around 40 calories, just slightly higher from the fruit sugars. None of these numbers are scary for a dieter, honestly. The real calorie impact comes from whatever rice you eat bosou with, not the bosou itself. A 200g bowl of white rice is 260 calories. Your two tablespoons of bosou next to it is 35 calories. You do the maths lah."],"lighter":["Stick to the fish version (bosou ikan) over the meat version to keep it at 35 calories instead of 50 per serving.","Treat it as a condiment, not a side dish. Two tablespoons is the traditional portion and more than enough given how strong the flavour is.","Pair it with cauliflower rice or a smaller rice portion if you are actively cutting, since the bosou itself is not the calorie problem.","Go heavy on plain blanched vegetables alongside it. Sayur manis or kangkung with bosou and rice is a genuinely filling, low-calorie plate.","Avoid versions where extra oil or coconut milk has been stirred in during preparation, which some modern takes do to mellow the sourness."],"variants":[{"name":"Bosou ikan (freshwater fish)","calories":35,"note":"Standard version; lean freshwater fish like tilapia or pelian; lowest fat of the variants"},{"name":"Bosou daging (wild boar / pork)","calories":50,"note":"Wild boar or farmed pork replaces fish; higher fat content from red meat raises calories ~40%"},{"name":"Bosou with bambangan (fruit variant)","calories":40,"note":"Bambangan (wild mango) added to bosou ikan; adds natural fruit sugars, slightly higher carbs"}],"components":[{"name":"Fermented freshwater fish (40% of batch)","grams":12,"calories":14},{"name":"Fermented cooked rice (56% of batch, carbs reduced by lacto-fermentation)","grams":17,"calories":19},{"name":"Pangi seed and salt","grams":1,"calories":2}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Bosou?","a":"A standard 2-tablespoon (30g) serving of bosou has around 35 calories, with 3g protein, 4g carbs, and 1g fat. The daging (meat) version runs closer to 50 calories per serving due to higher fat from pork or wild boar."},{"q":"What exactly is bosou and how is it eaten?","a":"Bosou is a traditional Kadazan-Dusun fermented food from Sabah made by mixing raw freshwater fish or meat with cooked rice and pangi seed, then fermenting the mixture for several days to weeks. It develops a sour, funky flavour similar to very strong budu. You eat it in small spoonfuls alongside plain white rice, not as a standalone dish. The wild boar and pork versions are non-halal."},{"q":"Is bosou healthy or good for weight loss?","a":"For a dieter, bosou is basically a non-issue calorie-wise at 35 calories per serving. It also delivers 3g of protein from the fermented fish. Fermented foods carry probiotic benefits from the lacto-fermentation process. The main thing to watch is sodium since it is preserved with salt, but if you are counting calories, the bosou is not your problem. Your rice portion is."}],"related":["hinava","tuhau","pinasakan"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["Meta description and Twitter description both state \"around 60 calories per 2-tbsp serving\" but every other element on the page (answer box, nutrition table, body text, FAQs, schema JSON-LD) consistently states 35 calories for the same 2-tbsp (30g) serving. The 60 kcal figure in the meta is a leftover error that contradicts the page content."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the meta description and Twitter description tags, change \"around 60 calories per 2-tbsp serving\" to \"around 35 calories per 2-tbsp serving\" to match the figure used everywhere else on the page.","severity":"major"}},{"dish":{"slug":"pinasakan","name":"Pinasakan","category":"lauk","serving":"1 serving (2 small fish + broth)","servingGrams":230,"calories":240,"protein":34,"carbs":5,"fat":9,"metaDescription":"Pinasakan is around 240 calories per serving with 34g protein. Sabah turmeric-simmered fish is one of the lightest lauk you can eat.","summary":"A traditional Kadazandusun fish dish from Sabah, small oily fish simmered with turmeric and a souring agent until tangy and broth-y, with barely any oil in sight.","answer":"Pinasakan comes in at around 240 calories per serving (2 small fish plus broth, about 230g total). That's for the classic basung or mackerel scad version, simmered in turmeric and a souring agent like takob-akob or tamarind, no frying involved. Almost all of those 240 calories are coming from the fish itself, which is why this dish punches way above its weight for protein, 34g of it.","realTalk":["Pinasakan is a Kadazandusun staple from Sabah that most peninsular Malaysians have never heard of, and honestly that's their loss. The dish is simple: small fish (usually basung, which is mackerel scad) simmered with turmeric, lemongrass, ginger, and a souring agent, the traditional choice being takob-akob, a wild fruit that grows in Sabah, though bambangan or tamarind works too. No coconut milk, no frying, no heavy rempah. The result is a tangy, lightly brothy fish dish that tastes way more interesting than it sounds. At 240 calories for two fish plus broth, the calorie count is genuinely low, and 34g of protein from one lauk is better than most things on a nasi campur spread. Fair warning though: these numbers are a ballpark estimate because hard nutritional data on Pinasakan is basically nonexistent. The figures here are built from the fish species and cooking method, treat them as a reasonable guide rather than a lab result.","What actually moves the numbers is the fish size and whether anyone decided to fry the fish first before simmering, which some cooks do. If you get a larger serving or bigger fish (say tenggiri instead of basung), you're looking at closer to 280 calories. The broth itself contributes almost nothing calorie-wise, maybe 10 to 15 calories, so drinking the broth is fine. Where people wreck the maths is on the rice side: most Sabahans eat this with a generous plate of rice, so add 200 to 350 calories depending on your portion lah. Pinasakan as a lauk is one of the easiest high-protein, low-fat choices you can make at a Sabah spread. The rice is where you have to use your head."],"lighter":["Eat it with less rice. The fish itself is already low cal, so don't undermine it with three scoops of nasi.","Ask for less broth if you're eating out and the cook adds salt generously.","Stick to the sada version with basung rather than upgrading to tenggiri, which adds another 40 calories per serving.","Pair it with sayur manis or tuhau on the side instead of a fried lauk so your whole plate stays reasonable.","If cooking at home, use fresh takob-akob or tamarind rather than packaged paste, which sometimes has added sugar."],"variants":[{"name":"Pinasakan sada (basung/mackerel scad)","calories":240,"note":"Lean small oily fish; standard Kadazandusun preparation with takob-akob or tamarind"},{"name":"Pinasakan with tenggiri (Spanish mackerel)","calories":280,"note":"Larger, slightly fattier fish; same broth base but higher calorie density per serving"}],"components":[{"name":"Basung/mackerel scad fish (cooked, ~170g)","grams":170,"calories":211},{"name":"Aromatics (turmeric, lemongrass, ginger, garlic, shallots)","grams":30,"calories":18},{"name":"Souring agent (takob-akob, bambangan, or tamarind)","grams":20,"calories":11}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Pinasakan?","a":"A standard serving of Pinasakan (2 small basung fish plus broth, around 230g) comes to about 240 calories, with 34g protein and only 9g fat. It's one of the lighter fish dishes you'll find in Sabah cooking."},{"q":"What is Pinasakan exactly?","a":"Pinasakan is a traditional Kadazandusun dish from Sabah. Small fish, usually basung (mackerel scad), are simmered with turmeric, lemongrass, ginger, and a sour ingredient like takob-akob (a wild fruit) or bambangan (a Sabah wild mango). No deep frying, no thick coconut gravy. It's broth-y, tangy, and very simple. If you've only eaten peninsular fish dishes, think of it as the Sabah answer to asam pedas but much lighter."},{"q":"Is Pinasakan good for weight loss?","a":"Honestly, yes. For 240 calories you get 34g of protein, which is solid. The cooking method uses no added oil to speak of, and the souring agent keeps the broth light instead of rich. The main thing to watch is your rice portion on the side, because nobody's eating Pinasakan without rice."}],"related":["hinava","ikan-bakar","asam-pedas"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The variants section describes tenggiri (Spanish mackerel) as 'slightly fattier fish' compared to basung, but Spanish mackerel is generally leaner per 100g than mackerel scad. The higher calorie count for the tenggiri variant is better explained by larger portion size, not higher fat content."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the variants table Notes column for tenggiri, change \"Larger, slightly fattier fish\" to \"Larger fish; bigger portion drives the higher calorie count rather than fat content\"","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"sinalau-bakas","name":"Sinalau Bakas","category":"lauk","serving":"1 portion sliced smoked wild boar (hawker stall, ~200g)","servingGrams":200,"calories":560,"protein":44,"carbs":0,"fat":40,"metaDescription":"Sinalau bakas (Sabah smoked wild boar) packs around 560 cal for a 200g hawker portion. Here's what's driving the number and how to eat it smarter.","summary":"Sabah's famous smoked wild boar, slow-cooked over fire until the fat renders and the skin gets that deep, smoky bark that you will be thinking about for days.","answer":"Sinalau bakas comes in at around 560 calories for a standard 200g hawker portion of fatty shoulder or rib cuts. That is all from protein and fat, zero carbs, so if you are tracking macros it is 44g protein and 40g fat in one sitting. The number jumps to 820 calories once you add rice, which is how most people actually eat it at a kedai kopi. For leaner cuts like leg meat, you are looking at closer to 320 calories for the same 200g weight. Bear in mind this is a ballpark figure since hard nutritional data on smoked wild boar is basically nonexistent, so treat it as a solid estimate rather than a lab result.","realTalk":["Sinalau bakas is a Kadazan-Dusun dish from Sabah where wild boar is rubbed with garlic, lemongrass, turmeric and salt, then slow-smoked over a wood fire. If you are from the peninsula, think of it as the Sabah version of babi panggang but wilder, smokier, and more rustic. A typical hawker portion of 200g of fatty shoulder or rib cuts sits at around 560 calories, and it earns every single one of them. The lean muscle itself is only about 210 calories for 130g, but the 50g of rendered fat on the bone brings in another 315 calories on its own. That fat is also exactly why this thing tastes so good, so there is no mystery here. It is non-halal, by the way, so plan accordingly. Hard numbers on smoked wild boar are genuinely scarce, so these figures are a careful estimate based on USDA wild boar data and the actual fat content of typical cuts. Treat it as a solid ballpark, not a lab-certified figure.","What actually moves the number is which cut you get. Lean leg cuts at 200g drop all the way to around 320 calories, basically a different meal. Fatty shoulder or rib with the fat cap intact is the standard hawker serve and that is where you hit 560. Add a plate of rice (200g cooked, roughly 260 calories) and the whole meal is about 820 calories, which is fine if that is your one big meal of the day. The sinalau-in-a-bun version at KL-style Sabahan restaurants is actually more calorie-controlled at 480 calories total because you only get about 100g of meat in the bun. If you are watching your intake, ask for the lean cuts specifically or split a portion with someone. There is no gravy, no sauce, no oil added after smoking, so what you see is what you get."],"lighter":["Ask specifically for leg or loin cuts instead of shoulder or rib. Same 200g portion drops from 560 to around 320 calories.","Split the portion. A 100g serving of the standard fatty cuts is about 280 calories and honestly still very satisfying because the flavour is so intense.","Skip the rice or go half portion (100g cooked rice instead of 200g) to save about 130 calories if you are having it as a rice set.","Eat it as the protein anchor of your meal and fill the rest of your plate with sayur manis or tuhau. Both are near-zero calories and pair well with the smoky flavour.","If you are at a restaurant doing the sinalau bun, that is your best calorie value at 480 calories for a full meal."],"variants":[{"name":"Sinalau bakas plain (lean cuts only)","calories":320,"note":"~200g of leaner cuts such as leg; closer to USDA roasted wild boar baseline at ~160 kcal/100g"},{"name":"Sinalau-in-a-bun (sandwich)","calories":480,"note":"~100g smoked boar in a white bun (~80g); popular in Kota Kinabalu restaurants"},{"name":"Sinalau bakas plain (fatty shoulder/rib, typical)","calories":560,"note":"200g of prized fatty shoulder or rib cuts with fat intact; standard hawker portion"},{"name":"Sinalau bakas rice set","calories":820,"note":"200g smoked boar + 200g cooked white rice; common pairing at kedai kopi"}],"components":[{"name":"Wild boar lean muscle (smoked)","grams":130,"calories":210},{"name":"Rendered fat (from shoulder/rib cuts, intact)","grams":50,"calories":315},{"name":"Marinade residue (garlic, lemongrass, turmeric, salt)","grams":20,"calories":35}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Sinalau Bakas?","a":"A standard 200g hawker portion of sinalau bakas (fatty shoulder or rib cuts) has around 560 calories, with 44g protein and 40g fat and zero carbs. Leaner leg cuts at the same weight come in closer to 320 calories. Add 200g of white rice for the full set and you are at about 820 calories."},{"q":"What exactly is sinalau bakas?","a":"Sinalau bakas is a traditional Kadazan-Dusun dish from Sabah, East Malaysia. Wild boar (bakas) is rubbed with garlic, lemongrass, turmeric and salt, then slow-smoked over a wood fire. The result is deeply smoky, rich meat with a bark on the outside and tender, fatty flesh inside. It is non-halal."},{"q":"Is sinalau bakas high in fat?","a":"Yes, the typical hawker portion with shoulder or rib cuts is about 40g of fat per 200g serving, which is high. The fat is all from the animal itself, mostly saturated fat from the rendered pork fat layer. If you want a lower-fat version, ask for leg cuts specifically and the fat content drops significantly, bringing calories down to around 320 for the same portion size."}],"related":["hinava","pinasakan","bosou"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"560 kcal for 200g of fatty shoulder/rib cuts (280 kcal/100g) is at the top end of plausible for smoked wild boar. USDA wild boar roasted data sits around 153-160 kcal/100g for lean cuts; the page's lean-leg estimate of 320 kcal/200g (160 kcal/100g) correctly matches this. Reaching 280 kcal/100g for fatty cuts requires a very high fat cap, which is possible but aggressive. The macros cross-check passes (44g protein x4 + 40g fat x9 = 536, close to 560). Treat as borderline high rather than a clear error, and the page already caveats the uncertainty honestly.","factualConcerns":["The page contradicts itself on the sinalau-in-a-bun: the 'Real Talk' section calls it a dish found at 'KL-style Sabahan restaurants', but the variants table says it is 'popular in Kota Kinabalu restaurants'. The bun version is a Kota Kinabalu / Sabah phenomenon, not a KL one. The phrase 'KL-style Sabahan restaurants' is misleading and should be corrected to 'Kota Kinabalu restaurants' or just 'Sabahan restaurants'."],"voiceIssues":["No hard-fail violations (no em dashes, no banned phrases, no forced slang). However the prose reads more like a calm informational article than a mamak-friend voice. There is no 'bro', 'lah', 'kan' or any personality marker anywhere in the visible prose. The CLAUDE.md voice profile calls for occasional mild personality cues. The writing is competent but flat, sitting closer to a measured food blog than the casual direct warmth the site aims for."],"fixSuggestion":"In the 'Real Talk' section, change \"KL-style Sabahan restaurants\" to \"Kota Kinabalu restaurants\" so it matches the variants table and is factually accurate. Optionally add one light personality beat to the prose (e.g. a \"bro\" or \"lah\" somewhere) to lift it closer to the intended mamak-friend voice.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"sayur-manis","name":"Sayur Manis (Cangkuk Manis / Sabah Veg)","category":"veg","serving":"1 plate (goreng telur, stir-fried with egg)","servingGrams":150,"calories":185,"protein":9,"carbs":8,"fat":13,"metaDescription":"Sayur manis goreng telur is around 185 calories per plate. The veg is barely 40 kcal, the oil is what does the damage. Full breakdown here.","summary":"Sabah famous sweet-leaf vegetable, stir-fried with egg at the hawker stall, one of the cheapest and most genuinely low-calorie sides you can order if you watch the oil.","answer":"A plate of sayur manis goreng telur comes in at around 185 calories for a 150g hawker serving. That number sounds almost too good to be true for a stir-fry, and honestly it kind of is, because the veg itself is only about 40 calories. The egg adds roughly 70 kcal and the cooking oil does most of the remaining heavy lifting at around 160 kcal. If you order it without egg (just garlic or dried shrimp), you're looking at closer to 120 calories per plate. This is a ballpark estimate because hard nutritional data for sayur manis specifically is thin, but the numbers are grounded in the known calorie values of each component.","realTalk":["Sayur manis, or cangkuk manis, is a leafy green vegetable (Sauropus androgynus) that is pretty much Sabah's signature veg. If you grew up outside Borneo you might not have seen it before, but walk into any Sabah hawker centre and it's on every single table. The leaves are tender, slightly sweet, and cook down fast. Calorie-wise it is genuinely one of the best things you can eat at a hawker stall. The veg itself clocks in at around 40 kcal per plate. The goreng telur version sits at about 185 kcal total, which is really not bad at all for a cooked side dish. Where things go sideways is the oil, which at a typical hawker portion of about 1.5 tablespoons contributes roughly 160 kcal on its own. So yeah, technically this is an oily dish that happens to contain a very low-calorie vegetable. Keep that in mind. These numbers are an honest estimate, not lab-verified figures, because published data on sayur manis is scarce, so treat the 185 as a solid ballpark rather than gospel.","The thing that actually changes the maths here is how much oil the uncle uses. A generous wok toss at a hawker stall can easily push past 2 tablespoons of oil, which adds another 60 to 70 kcal on top of what is already counted here. At home you can do this with half the oil and barely notice the difference in taste. The egg is optional but adds a proper 70 kcal and 6g of protein, which honestly makes the dish more worth it as a meal component. The no-egg version at around 120 calories is basically free food in calorie terms. If you see a masak lemak version on the menu, that coconut milk base will push it up to around 260 calories, still not terrible but a different animal from the simple stir-fry. Order the plain garlic or dried shrimp version when you're cutting, add the egg when you want more staying power."],"lighter":["Order goreng bawang (no egg) instead of goreng telur to drop from 185 to around 120 calories per plate.","At home, use one teaspoon of oil instead of a tablespoon and a a half. You save roughly 80 to 100 calories and the veg still tastes good because the leaves themselves are flavourful.","Ask the hawker to go light on oil (kurang minyak) if you order it out. Some will, some won't, but worth asking.","Pair it with a plain protein like steamed fish or grilled chicken instead of fatty lauk to keep the whole meal in check.","Avoid the masak lemak version if you are counting, the coconut milk bumps it up by about 75 calories compared to a simple stir-fry."],"variants":[{"name":"Goreng bawang / tumis udang kering (garlic or dried shrimp, no egg)","calories":120,"note":"Very low calorie; oil is the main contributor; veg itself is under 40 kcal"},{"name":"Goreng telur (stir-fried with egg)","calories":185,"note":"One egg adds ~70 kcal; typical hawker serving with standard oil amount"},{"name":"Masak lemak (cooked in coconut milk)","calories":260,"note":"Coconut milk adds significant fat and calories; less common but found in Sabah home cooking"}],"components":[{"name":"Sayur manis leaves (cooked, ~180g raw)","grams":90,"calories":40},{"name":"Cooking oil (hawker portion ~1 tbsp)","grams":9,"calories":75},{"name":"Egg (1 medium)","grams":50,"calories":65},{"name":"Garlic, shallots, dried shrimp, seasoning","grams":15,"calories":10}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Sayur Manis (Cangkuk Manis / Sabah Veg)?","a":"A standard hawker plate of sayur manis goreng telur (stir-fried with egg) is around 185 calories per 150g serving. The no-egg version with just garlic or dried shrimp is closer to 120 calories. These are estimates as hard published data for this specific vegetable is limited."},{"q":"What exactly is sayur manis and is it healthy?","a":"Sayur manis, also called cangkuk manis or Sabah veg, is a leafy green vegetable (Sauropus androgynus) widely eaten in Sabah, Sarawak, and Brunei. The leaves are soft, mildly sweet, and cook very quickly. The vegetable itself is genuinely low in calories at roughly 40 kcal per cooked plate. It is also a decent source of protein for a leaf vegetable. The main calorie driver when you order it at a stall is the cooking oil, not the veg."},{"q":"How does sayur manis compare to kangkung belacan calorie-wise?","a":"Sayur manis goreng telur at around 185 calories is fairly similar to kangkung belacan in the same weight range. Both are leafy stir-fries where the oil does most of the calorie work. If you order both without extra protein additions, the calorie difference is modest. Sayur manis without egg at 120 calories is one of the lighter veg options you can order at a hawker stall."}],"related":["kangkung-belacan","tuhau","kailan-ikan-masin"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The headline 185 kcal for goreng telur (150g) is plausible, but the page contradicts itself on oil calories. The breakdown table shows oil at 75 kcal (1 tbsp / 9g), which is consistent with the 185 kcal total. However, the answer-box prose says oil does \"most of the remaining heavy lifting at around 160 kcal,\" and \"The real talk\" prose says \"1.5 tablespoons contributes roughly 160 kcal on its own.\" If oil were 160 kcal, the total would be ~275 kcal, not 185. The prose figure of 160 kcal for oil is wrong relative to both the stated volume (1.5 tbsp ~ 120 kcal) and the breakdown table (75 kcal). The breakdown table is internally correct; the prose oil figure is the error.","factualConcerns":["The answer-box prose states cooking oil contributes 'around 160 kcal' and 'The real talk' repeats this figure for a 1.5-tablespoon hawker portion, but the calorie breakdown table correctly shows oil at 75 kcal (1 tbsp / 9g). These are irreconcilable: 1.5 tbsp of oil is roughly 120 kcal, not 160, and either figure makes the headline 185 kcal total impossible if oil alone is 160. The breakdown table is correct; the prose oil figure needs to be removed or corrected to match (roughly 75-120 kcal depending on the tablespoon count used)."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the answer-box text, change \"cooking oil does most of the remaining heavy lifting at around 160 kcal\" to \"cooking oil contributes around 75 kcal for a standard tablespoon.\" In \"The real talk\" paragraph, change \"at a typical hawker portion of about 1.5 tablespoons contributes roughly 160 kcal on its own\" to \"at about 1 to 1.5 tablespoons contributes roughly 75 to 120 kcal.\" This aligns the prose with the breakdown table and keeps the 185 kcal total defensible.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"latok","name":"Latok (Sea Grapes)","category":"veg","serving":"1 small plate","servingGrams":120,"calories":30,"protein":1,"carbs":4,"fat":1,"metaDescription":"Latok (sea grapes) is about 30 calories per plate. One of the lowest-calorie things you can eat in Sabah. Here's what actually changes that number.","summary":"A fresh, briny Sabah seaweed eaten raw with chilli-lime or sambal belacan, and one of the lightest things you'll find on any table.","answer":"A small plate of latok comes in at around 30 calories, making it one of the most diet-friendly things you can order in Sabah or Sarawak. The sea grapes themselves are practically nothing, around 13 calories per 100g. The rest comes from the dressing. Plain with just a squeeze of lime? Closer to 15 calories. With sambal belacan and garnish, you're at roughly 30. These are honest estimates though, because hard nutrition data for latok specifically is thin.","realTalk":["Latok is a type of seaweed, specifically Caulerpa lentillifera, also called sea grapes or green caviar. If you've never had it, picture tiny translucent green bubbles on thin stems that pop lightly when you bite them, fresh and briny like the sea. It's a Sabah and Brunei staple, served raw as a salad with chilli, lime, and sambal belacan. At around 30 calories per plate, it is genuinely one of the lowest-calorie foods you can eat in this part of the world. To be upfront though, the numbers here are ballpark estimates because robust lab data for latok is basically nonexistent. The raw seaweed is near-zero calories; everything on this page is our best approximation.","What actually moves the number is the dressing. Plain latok with just lime is around 15 calories for the whole plate. Add a typical sambal belacan dressing and some sliced chilli and tomato and you're at 30. Go heavier on the sambal, bigger diced onion, generous extra belacan, and the fuller salad version lands around 45. None of these numbers are going to stress your calorie budget, bah. The only real way to make latok a problem calorie-wise is pairing it with a massive plate of rice and three lauk. On its own, it's basically a free food."],"lighter":["Ask for the dressing on the side so you control how much sambal belacan goes on.","Squeeze fresh lime over it and skip the sambal entirely if you're going very low calorie, plain latok is about 15 calories.","Watch the diced tomato and onion garnish, they add almost nothing, but the sambal belacan can vary a lot in how oily the stall makes it.","Use latok as a side dish to replace a heavier vegetable order like kangkung in oyster sauce, you save 80 to 100 calories easily.","Order it first before your mains so you eat something filling and briny before the heavy stuff arrives."],"variants":[{"name":"Plain latok (no dressing)","calories":15,"note":"Just sea grapes with a squeeze of lime, nothing else added"},{"name":"Latok with chilli-lime dressing","calories":30,"note":"Typical hawker serving with sliced chilli, lime, small amount of sambal belacan"},{"name":"Latok salad (with onion, tomato, sambal belacan)","calories":45,"note":"More substantial salad version with diced tomato, sliced onion, and a generous sambal belacan dollop"}],"components":[{"name":"Fresh sea grapes (100g)","grams":100,"calories":13},{"name":"Sambal belacan or chilli-lime dressing (15g)","grams":15,"calories":13},{"name":"Sliced chilli, onion, tomato garnish (10g)","grams":10,"calories":4}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Latok (Sea Grapes)?","a":"A standard small plate of latok with sambal belacan or chilli-lime dressing is around 30 calories. Plain with just lime it's closer to 15 calories. These are estimates as hard nutrition data for latok is scarce."},{"q":"What exactly is latok and where do you get it?","a":"Latok is a type of seaweed (Caulerpa lentillifera) that looks like tiny green bubbles on thin stems, which is why it's also called sea grapes or green caviar. It's eaten raw, usually with chilli and lime or sambal belacan. You'll find it at seafood restaurants and hawker stalls in Sabah, Sarawak, and Brunei, it's not really a thing in peninsular Malaysia."},{"q":"Is latok good for weight loss?","a":"Honestly, yes. At 30 calories per plate it's about as low-calorie as a side dish gets. It's got a bit of fibre, a briny fresh flavour that feels satisfying, and the dressing is the only real variable. For anyone trying to eat at a calorie deficit without feeling miserable, ordering latok as a side is a solid move."}],"related":["kangkung-belacan","tuhau","ulam-ulaman"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["Minor internal inconsistency: the answer-box prose states raw latok is 'around 13 calories per 100g', but the nutrition table shows 25 kcal per 100g for the full plate (seaweed + dressing at 120g total). A reader comparing both figures back-to-back may find it confusing, though neither number is technically wrong."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the answer-box text, change \"the sea grapes themselves are practically nothing, around 13 calories per 100g\" to \"around 13 calories per 100g of plain sea grapes\" and add a brief note that the nutrition table's 25 kcal/100g figure blends in the dressing weight, so readers don't think the numbers contradict each other.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"ambuyat","name":"Ambuyat","category":"rice","serving":"1 serving ambuyat with basic cacah dip (per person)","servingGrams":430,"calories":245,"protein":3,"carbs":54,"fat":1,"metaDescription":"Ambuyat is around 230 calories per serving. The sago starch staple of Borneo is almost pure carb, dipped in sour cacah sauce.","summary":"Brunei and Sabah's iconic sago starch staple, twirled on bamboo forks and dipped in sour cacah sauce, simple in calories but serious in cultural weight.","answer":"A standard serving of ambuyat with basic cacah dip is roughly 245 calories for about 430g of food. That sounds like a lot of food for not many calories, and it is, because ambuyat is almost entirely water-swollen sago starch. The catch is that almost all of those 245 calories come from 54g of carbs, with barely 3g protein and 1g fat. On its own it keeps your stomach busy but does nothing for muscle. The full restaurant set with ikan goreng and stir-fried greens jumps to around 470 calories, which is a much more complete meal.","realTalk":["Ambuyat is the national dish of Brunei and a proper staple in Sabah and Sarawak too. If you have never had it, imagine sago starch cooked into a thick, sticky, translucent gel, kind of like very dense wallpaper paste but weirdly satisfying. You twirl a big blob of it around a bamboo fork called chandas, dip it into a sour, spicy cacah sauce made from dried shrimp, belacan, chili and lime, and swallow the whole thing without chewing. Seriously, you do not chew ambuyat. At 245 calories a serving it sounds diet-friendly, but be honest with yourself: the plain ambuyat itself is just 210 calories of pure sago carbs with 3g protein. It will fill you up for maybe an hour before your body figures out there is nothing substantial in there. These numbers are a ballpark estimate by the way, hard nutritional data for ambuyat is basically non-existent, so treat the figures as a reasonable guide rather than gospel.","What actually changes the maths is what you eat alongside it. The plain ambuyat set at 245 calories is fine as a base, but nobody eats ambuyat alone for a full meal. Add one piece of ikan goreng, around 80g of fried fish, and that is roughly 180 calories extra. Add a plate of sayur tumis and you are looking at another 60 calories. The full set lands around 470 calories total, which is actually a solid, filling meal at a reasonable calorie count. The tempoyak cacah version adds about 40 calories from fermented durian fat and carbs and pushes you to 280 calories for the ambuyat portion alone. The real trap is not the ambuyat itself, it is ordering two servings because the first one felt light."],"lighter":["Order the full set with ikan goreng and sayur tumis instead of eating plain ambuyat alone. You get more protein and fibre, and the total meal at 470 calories is more satisfying than two rounds of plain ambuyat.","Stick to the basic cacah dip rather than tempoyak cacah. The tempoyak version adds around 40 extra calories from durian fat, and honestly the basic cacah hits different anyway.","Ask for extra cacah on the side and use it to slow yourself down. The tangy, spicy dip makes you more mindful of each bite and stops you from inhaling two portions back to back.","If you are at a restaurant, pair your ambuyat with a steamed or grilled fish rather than fried. You get the same protein with less added oil.","One portion of ambuyat is genuinely a lot of volume for 245 calories. Stick to one and let it settle before deciding you want more."],"variants":[{"name":"Plain ambuyat only (no dip)","calories":210,"note":"60g dry sago starch cooked into gel; pure carb, no accompaniment"},{"name":"Ambuyat with basic cacah (dried shrimp, chili, belacan, lime)","calories":245,"note":"Standard hawker order; minimal protein from dried shrimp in dip"},{"name":"Ambuyat with tempoyak cacah (fermented durian dip)","calories":280,"note":"Tempoyak adds ~40 cal from durian fat and carbs; richer flavour"},{"name":"Ambuyat full set (ambuyat + ikan goreng + stir-fried greens)","calories":470,"note":"Adds fried fish ~80g (~180 cal) and sayur tumis ~100g (~60 cal); typical sit-down restaurant order"}],"components":[{"name":"Sago starch (60g dry, cooked into gel)","grams":380,"calories":210},{"name":"Basic cacah dip (dried shrimp, belacan, chili, lime)","grams":40,"calories":30},{"name":"Residual water in sago gel (non-caloric)","grams":10,"calories":5}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Ambuyat?","a":"A standard serving of ambuyat with basic cacah dip is around 245 calories per person (approximately 430g total). Plain ambuyat on its own is 210 calories, and the full restaurant set with ikan goreng and stir-fried greens comes to about 470 calories."},{"q":"What exactly is ambuyat and how do you eat it?","a":"Ambuyat is a thick, sticky, gel-like paste made from sago starch, essentially the staple carb of Brunei and parts of Sabah and Sarawak. You twirl it around a bamboo fork called chandas, dip it into a sour spicy sauce called cacah (made from dried shrimp, belacan, chili and lime), and swallow without chewing. It has a neutral flavour on its own so the cacah does most of the heavy lifting."},{"q":"Is ambuyat healthy or good for weight loss?","a":"It is low in fat and low in calories for the volume you eat, which is a plus. The problem is it is almost pure carbohydrate with barely any protein (3g per serving), so it will not keep you full for long on its own. For weight loss, order the full set with fish and vegetables so you actually get some protein in the meal. Treat the ambuyat as your carb portion rather than the whole meal."}],"related":["linopot","sarawak-laksa","tuaran-mee"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The eyebrow category label reads 'Rice Meals' but ambuyat is a sago starch dish, not a rice dish. It belongs under a Borneo staples or sago/starch category, not Rice Meals."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Change the food-hero__eyebrow text from \"Rice Meals\" to \"Borneo Staples\" or \"Sago Dishes\" to correctly categorise ambuyat.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"kuih-penjaram","name":"Kuih Penjaram (Pinjaram)","category":"snacks","serving":"1 piece (medium, pasar tamu size)","servingGrams":50,"calories":210,"protein":2,"carbs":37,"fat":6,"metaDescription":"One medium kuih penjaram at the pasar tamu is about 210 calories. Here's where those calories come from and how to eat it without the guilt.","summary":"A Sabah and Sarawak deep-fried rice-flour fritter sweetened with gula apong, shaped like a little UFO with a chewy centre and lacy crisp edges.","answer":"One medium kuih penjaram (the standard pasar tamu size, about 50g) comes in at roughly 210 calories. Most of that comes from the rice flour batter and palm sugar together at around 167 calories, with the frying oil adding another 53 calories on top. Be aware this is a ballpark estimate since solid nutrition data for penjaram specifically is basically non-existent, but 210 calories per medium piece is a reasonable working number.","realTalk":["Penjaram (also spelled pinjaram or penyaram depending on who you ask) is a Sabah and Sarawak thing that a lot of peninsular Malaysians have never actually tried. Think of it like a fried fritter shaped like a UFO or a little hat, with a thick chewy middle and a thin lacy edge that goes properly crispy from the fryer. The batter is rice flour, plain flour, and gula apong, which is Bornean palm sugar with a deep caramel, almost smoky sweetness. It is genuinely good. At 210 calories per medium piece, it is not a disaster either, but those calories are basically all carbs and frying oil. The palm sugar alone contributes about 84 calories, and the oil absorbed during frying adds another 53. There is almost no protein here, which is why penjaram is a snack, not a meal. To be straight with you: these numbers are estimates. Hard data on penjaram calories is thin, so treat 210 as a reasonable ballpark rather than a lab-tested figure.","The maths changes quite a bit depending on where you buy it and what size you grab. The small ones sold three for RM1 at Sabah tamu are only around 130 calories each because they are roughly 30g finished weight, less batter, less oil. The big ones at places like Donggongon tamu can hit 290 calories at around 70g. If you are calorie counting, the size difference matters more than anything else here. The other variable is how much oil the individual fritter absorbs during frying, which depends on oil temperature and how long it sits in the wok. A hotter, faster fry absorbs less oil. You cannot really control that at a market stall, but knowing it helps you understand why two penjaram from different stalls can taste and feel quite different. If you want to enjoy a couple pieces at the tamu without it wrecking your day, just know two medium ones is about 420 calories lah, so factor that in before adding nasi lemak on top."],"lighter":["Go for the small tamu size (three for RM1 style) instead of the medium. You drop from 210 to 130 calories per piece, that is a real difference.","Eat it fresh and hot. A penjaram that has been sitting out re-absorbs some oil as it cools, so you get the lacy edge at its crispiest and the oil at its lowest right out of the wok.","Stop at two pieces. Two small ones is about 260 calories, which is a proper snack. Two large ones is close to 580 calories, which is basically a full meal.","If you are at the tamu, pair penjaram with something protein-based like a hard-boiled egg or a piece of grilled chicken rather than stacking up on more kuih. You will stay fuller and the calorie count stays manageable.","Skip the ones sitting in a covered container sweating. Go for the stall that is actively frying. Fresher means less oil absorption and honestly they just taste better."],"variants":[{"name":"Small piece (3 for RM1, tamu size)","calories":130,"note":"Smaller ~30g fritter, common at Sabah tamu; less batter and less oil absorbed"},{"name":"Medium piece (standard)","calories":210,"note":"Typical medium size sold at pasar tamu and kedai kuih, ~50g finished weight"},{"name":"Large piece (big pinjaram, e.g. Donggongon tamu)","calories":290,"note":"Bigger ~70g fritter sold at larger markets, proportionally more sugar and oil"}],"components":[{"name":"Rice flour + plain flour (batter base)","grams":23,"calories":83},{"name":"Gula apong / palm sugar","grams":22,"calories":84},{"name":"Oil absorbed during deep-frying","grams":6,"calories":53}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Kuih Penjaram (Pinjaram)?","a":"A medium kuih penjaram at pasar tamu size (about 50g) is roughly 210 calories. The small tamu ones are around 130 calories each, and the large market versions can hit 290 calories. These are estimates since there is no official nutritional data for penjaram."},{"q":"What exactly is kuih penjaram?","a":"Penjaram is a deep-fried Bornean kuih made from a rice flour and palm sugar batter. It is shaped like a UFO or small hat, with a thick chewy centre and a thin lacy edge that crisps up in the fryer. The sweetness comes from gula apong, a Sabah palm sugar with a distinctly caramel, almost smoky flavour. You find it all over Sabah and Sarawak pasar tamu, sometimes spelled pinjaram or penyaram but it is all the same kuih."},{"q":"Is penjaram healthy?","a":"Honest answer: it is a deep-fried sugar fritter, so not exactly a health food. At 210 calories per medium piece with almost zero protein and 6g of fat, it is a snack that sits purely in carbs and frying oil. That said, two small pieces at around 260 calories total is a perfectly reasonable treat if you are calorie counting. Just do not eat five of them and call it a light snack."}],"related":["pisang-goreng","kuih-cincin","apam-balik"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The stated 210 kcal for a 50g piece implies 420 kcal/100g, which is high for a rice-flour fritter. Comparable fried kuih typically sit at 280-360 kcal/100g, suggesting 140-180 kcal per medium piece is a more likely range. 210 is not wildly wrong given oil absorption variance, but it is on the optimistic-high end. Also, the ingredient breakdown sums to 220 kcal (83+84+53), not 210 as stated.","factualConcerns":["The calorie breakdown rows sum to 220 kcal (83+84+53) but the headline and table show 210 kcal. Minor arithmetic inconsistency that a careful reader will notice."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Fix the breakdown math: either adjust the ingredient rows to sum to 210 (e.g. change oil absorbed to 43 kcal, or the flour row to 73 kcal), or update the headline to 220 kcal. The simplest fix is to drop the oil row to 43 kcal so the total reads 83+84+43=210.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"kuih-cincin","name":"Kuih Cincin","category":"snacks","serving":"1 piece (ring cookie)","servingGrams":18,"calories":120,"protein":2,"carbs":12,"fat":7,"metaDescription":"Kuih Cincin is about 120 calories per ring cookie. This Sabah deep-fried rice flour snack is dense, crunchy and dangerously easy to eat by the handful.","summary":"A hard, dark-brown ring cookie from Sabah, deep-fried from rice flour and palm sugar until it shatters like a cracker.","answer":"One piece of Kuih Cincin is roughly 120 calories. Each ring cookie weighs about 18g and packs 7g of fat and 12g of carbs, which is what you get when you double-fry something in oil. The number is a ballpark estimate since solid lab data for this traditional snack is thin, but it sits comfortably in the same territory as a small curry puff or a few pieces of Kuih Bangkit.","realTalk":["Kuih Cincin is a traditional ring-shaped cookie from the Bajau and Brunei Malay communities in Sabah and Brunei. If you have never seen it before, imagine a small dark-brown ring that looks a bit like a miniature doughnut, except it is bone-dry, rock-hard and crunchy all the way through. It is made from rice flour and gula apong (palm sugar), which gives it that deep caramel flavour, and it is deep-fried twice in the traditional Sabah hawker version to get that shattering crunch. At 120 calories per piece, the main culprit is the frying oil, which accounts for about 36 of those calories on its own. It is not a diet food, and nobody is pretending it is. But because it is so dense and hard, you actually chew it slowly and tend to eat fewer pieces than you think. Fair warning though: these numbers are a genuine ballpark estimate because hard nutritional data for Kuih Cincin is basically nonexistent, so treat the figures as a reasonable guide rather than gospel.","What actually moves the maths is how many you grab. One piece is 120 calories, which is manageable. Three pieces is 360 calories, which is already a medium-sized meal in cookie form. The double-fried Iranun style clocks in slightly higher at around 125 calories per piece versus the softer single-fried Brunei-Malay version at about 110 calories, because more frying means more oil absorbed. If you are buying these from a stall or pasar malam, they almost always come in packs of 10 to 20 pieces, and that is where it gets dangerous. Nobody stops at two. If you portion yourself to five pieces, you are looking at 600 calories from snacks alone, which is a lot for something that does not fill you up the same way real food does. Eat them as an occasional treat, not a bag-a-day habit."],"lighter":["Stick to a hard limit of three pieces before you close the bag. At 120 calories each, three is 360 calories total, which is already a meaningful snack.","Go for the soft single-fried Brunei-Malay style if you can find it. It saves you about 15 calories per piece, and over a handful of pieces that actually adds up.","Pair a couple of pieces with plain black coffee or unsweetened tea instead of a sweetened drink. Teh tarik alongside adds another 100 to 150 calories on top.","Do not eat them standing at the stall. Sit down, put a fixed amount on a plate, and walk away from the pack. Out of sight really does help here.","If you are craving something crunchy and Malaysian that is lower calorie, a piece of Keropok Lekor or plain Ulam-Ulaman gives you crunch for fewer calories without the double-fried oil load."],"variants":[{"name":"Soft style (Brunei-Malay)","calories":110,"note":"Single-fried, softer texture, slightly less oil absorbed"},{"name":"Hard/crunchy style (Iranun)","calories":125,"note":"Double-fried, denser and crunchier, more oil absorbed; the common Sabah hawker version"}],"components":[{"name":"Rice flour (dough base)","grams":14,"calories":52},{"name":"Palm sugar (gula apong) and brown sugar","grams":8,"calories":30},{"name":"Absorbed frying oil (double-fried)","grams":4,"calories":34},{"name":"Wheat flour component","grams":1,"calories":4}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Kuih Cincin?","a":"One piece of Kuih Cincin is approximately 120 calories, based on a standard 18g ring cookie. The double-fried Sabah hawker version runs slightly higher at around 125 calories, while the softer Brunei-Malay style is closer to 110 calories."},{"q":"What is Kuih Cincin made of?","a":"Kuih Cincin is made primarily from rice flour and palm sugar (gula apong), shaped into rings and deep-fried until hard and dark brown. Some versions include a small amount of wheat flour. The double-frying is what gives it that distinctive crunch and also why the fat content is relatively high for a small cookie."},{"q":"Is Kuih Cincin suitable if I am trying to lose weight?","a":"It can fit into a calorie-controlled diet if you are strict about portions. At 120 calories per ring, a couple of pieces with your coffee is fine. The problem is portion creep because they come in big packs and are very easy to keep eating. Treat it as an occasional snack, count your pieces before you start, and it is manageable lah."}],"related":["pisang-goreng","kuih-penjaram","kuih-bangkit"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The page attributes Kuih Cincin jointly to 'Bajau and Brunei Malay communities' in the main body text, but then separately names the hard double-fried variant as the 'Iranun style' in the variants table. The Iranun are a distinct group from the Bajau; labelling the common Sabah hawker version specifically as Iranun without any earlier mention of Iranun in the origin section creates a minor inconsistency that could mislead readers about who makes what."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the variants table, change the row label from \"Hard/crunchy style (Iranun)\" to \"Hard/crunchy style (Sabah hawker)\" or add a brief mention of Iranun origin earlier in the main body text so the reference does not appear from nowhere.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"ayam-goreng","name":"Ayam Goreng","category":"lauk","serving":"1 piece (thigh or drumstick, bone-in)","servingGrams":160,"calories":390,"protein":28,"carbs":12,"fat":25,"metaDescription":"Ayam goreng comes in at around 390 calories per piece. Here is what drives that number and how to make it hurt less.","summary":"Malay-style fried chicken, marinated in turmeric and spices, coated in batter and deep fried skin-on until golden and crispy.","answer":"One piece of ayam goreng (thigh or drumstick, bone-in, around 160g) will cost you roughly 390 calories, with 28g protein, 12g carbs and 25g fat. That is a pretty decent protein hit, but the skin and the deep frying are doing a lot of heavy lifting on the fat side.","realTalk":["Ayam goreng is basically Malay fried chicken. You marinate a piece of chicken in turmeric, garlic, lemongrass and whatever spices your mak or the nasi campur auntie likes, then you coat it and drop it in hot oil. The skin crisps up, the batter turns golden, and it smells like something you should definitely not be eating on a deficit. At 390 calories per piece, it is not exactly diet food, but it is also not crazy. The 25g of fat is the main issue, and most of that comes from two things: the skin (do not underestimate it) and the oil absorbed during frying. The batter adds another layer. To be straight with you, these numbers are a ballpark estimate because solid lab data for Malaysian home-style ayam goreng is basically nonexistent. Different stalls, different batters, different oil temperatures, different drain times. Take the 390 as a reasonable middle ground, not gospel.","What actually moves the needle is the variant and the piece size. Ayam goreng kunyit (no thick batter, just a turmeric marinade) drops to around 280 calories because there is less coating to absorb oil. The full berempah version with that thick lemongrass-galangal-coriander batter climbs to about 420 calories. If you pull the skin off before eating, you are probably saving 40 to 60 calories right there. Two pieces at a nasi campur stall and you are looking at close to 800 calories before the rice even touches the plate. Pair it with kangkung or a plain sayur instead of another lauk, and the whole meal lands somewhere reasonable."],"lighter":["Go for ayam goreng kunyit over berempah. The thin turmeric marinade version saves you about 110 calories per piece just from less batter and less oil absorption.","Pull the skin off before you eat. Annoying, yes. But the skin is where a big chunk of the fat lives.","Pick breast over thigh if you have the option. Breast meat is leaner, so even with the same coating the base fat is lower.","At the nasi campur stall, treat the ayam goreng as your one lauk and fill the rest of the plate with veg. Do not stack it with rendang and sambal too.","Pat the chicken down with tissue after frying or after it comes off the stall tray. Even a bit of surface oil removal helps lah."],"variants":[{"name":"Ayam goreng kunyit (turmeric only, no batter)","calories":280,"note":"Thin turmeric marinade, no thick coating; less batter means less oil absorbed"},{"name":"Ayam goreng mamak (light spiced coating)","calories":350,"note":"Moderate spiced coating, typical mamak stall style"},{"name":"Ayam goreng tepung (flour-battered)","calories":380,"note":"Plain flour batter, less spice complexity than berempah"},{"name":"Ayam goreng berempah (thick spiced batter)","calories":420,"note":"Thickest coating of lemongrass, galangal, coriander batter; absorbs most oil"}],"components":[{"name":"Chicken meat (thigh/drumstick, cooked, skin on)","grams":110,"calories":220},{"name":"Spiced flour/batter coating","grams":20,"calories":70},{"name":"Oil absorbed during deep frying","grams":15,"calories":135},{"name":"Marinade (turmeric, garlic, lemongrass, spices)","grams":15,"calories":15}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Ayam Goreng?","a":"One piece of ayam goreng (thigh or drumstick, bone-in, around 160g) is approximately 390 calories. The berempah version with thick batter is closer to 420 calories, while kunyit style without batter comes in around 280 calories."},{"q":"What exactly is ayam goreng?","a":"Ayam goreng is Malay fried chicken. The chicken is marinated in turmeric, garlic, lemongrass and spices, then either coated in spiced batter or left with just the marinade, and deep fried skin-on. You will find it at nasi campur stalls, mamak shops and basically every warung in the country."},{"q":"Is ayam goreng a good source of protein?","a":"One piece gives you 28g of protein, which is solid. The catch is the 25g of fat that comes with it, mostly from the skin and the oil absorbed during frying. If you are watching calories, kunyit style without thick batter is the smarter pick."}],"related":["nasi-kandar","ayam-percik","rendang"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The calorie breakdown table sums to 440 kcal (220 + 70 + 135 + 15) but the headline and nutrition facts state 390 kcal. The components do not add up to the stated total. Either the oil-absorbed figure (135 kcal, implying 15g of oil) is too high and should be closer to 85 kcal to make the numbers reconcile, or the component weights need adjusting."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the \"Where the calories come from\" breakdown, change \"Oil absorbed during deep frying (15g)\" from 135 kcal to 85 kcal (roughly 9-10g of absorbed oil), which makes the components sum to 390 kcal and aligns better with typical oil absorption for a battered bone-in piece. Alternatively keep 135 kcal for oil and raise the headline to 440 kcal, but 390 is the more defensible number so adjusting the breakdown is the cleaner fix.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"kerang-masak","name":"Kerang Masak","category":"lauk","serving":"1 hawker serving (cockles in sambal or kicap sauce)","servingGrams":180,"calories":300,"protein":20,"carbs":16,"fat":17,"metaDescription":"Kerang masak is about 300 calories per hawker serving. Cockles are lean, but that sambal base is where calories pile up. Full breakdown here.","summary":"Cockles cooked in sambal or kicap sauce, a classic hawker lauk where the shellfish itself is surprisingly lean and the sauce does most of the calorie work.","answer":"A standard hawker serving of kerang masak comes in at around 300 calories for roughly 180g, with 20g protein, 16g carbs, and 17g fat. The cockles themselves are only about 105 calories. The other 160-odd calories come almost entirely from the oil in the sambal base. Order the kicap version and you drop to around 230 calories. These are ballpark estimates though, not lab results.","realTalk":["Kerang masak is blood cockles cooked in either a spicy sambal or a soy sauce (kicap) gravy. The cockles themselves are genuinely lean, around 105 calories for 130g of shelled meat, with decent protein for a shellfish. The problem is the sambal. That chili paste base with belacan, shallots, palm sugar, and a generous pour of cooking oil adds roughly 160 calories on its own. So you are looking at about 300 calories for the whole thing, which is not bad as a lauk to go with rice, but you want to be honest with yourself about how much gravy you are scooping up. To be clear, these numbers are a ballpark estimate because solid nutrition data for this specific dish is thin. Take the figures as a useful guide, not gospel.","What actually changes the maths here is the sauce version and how heavy the cook's hand is with oil. Go for kerang masak kicap instead of sambal and you save roughly 70 calories per serving, dropping to around 230. The sambal petai version adds stink beans and bumps it up to about 330. If you are at a nasi campur stall, use a slotted spoon to grab mostly cockles and leave some of the oil-heavy gravy behind. You can easily cut 50 to 80 calories that way. Kerang is also a solid protein choice at 20g per serving, so pairing it with plain rice and a vegetable is genuinely one of the smarter hawker plate combinations you can put together."],"lighter":["Order kerang masak kicap instead of sambal. Same cockles, about 70 fewer calories per serving.","At the nasi campur counter, use a slotted spoon and let the oily gravy drain off before it hits your plate.","Ask for less gravy, or eat the cockles and leave most of the sauce behind. The meat itself is lean.","Pair it with plain steamed rice and a simple vegetable like sayur manis to keep the whole plate under control.","Avoid the sambal petai version if you are counting, as the extra ingredients push it closer to 330 calories."],"variants":[{"name":"Kerang masak kicap (soy sauce)","calories":230,"note":"Soy sauce base with minimal oil; lighter and lower fat than sambal version"},{"name":"Kerang masak sambal (standard)","calories":300,"note":"Typical sambal with chili paste, belacan, oil, and palm sugar; most common hawker version"},{"name":"Kerang sambal petai","calories":330,"note":"Sambal kerang with petai (stink beans) added; petai adds minimal calories but slightly more carbs"}],"components":[{"name":"Shelled cooked cockles (kerang)","grams":130,"calories":105},{"name":"Cooking oil in sambal base","grams":18,"calories":160},{"name":"Chili paste, aromatics (shallots, garlic, belacan)","grams":25,"calories":25},{"name":"Sugar and seasoning","grams":7,"calories":25}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Kerang Masak?","a":"About 300 calories for a standard hawker serving of roughly 180g. The cockles themselves are only around 105 calories. The sambal base with oil, chili paste, and palm sugar accounts for most of the rest. The kicap version is lighter at around 230 calories."},{"q":"What exactly is kerang masak?","a":"Kerang is blood cockles, and masak just means cooked. You get small shelled cockles stir-fried in either a spicy sambal sauce (chili paste, belacan, shallots, palm sugar, oil) or a darker soy-based kicap sauce. It is a common lauk at nasi campur stalls across Malaysia."},{"q":"Is kerang masak a healthy choice for someone losing weight?","a":"The cockles themselves are a good lean protein at 20g per serving. The issue is the oil-heavy sambal base. It is a decent choice if you pick the kicap version and avoid drowning your plate in the gravy. Not the lowest calorie lauk out there, but not a disaster either."}],"related":["sambal-sotong","lala-masak","kangkung-belacan"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The answer box states the oil accounts for 'the other 160-odd calories' implying total non-cockle calories are ~160, but the breakdown table shows 160 (oil) + 25 (aromatics) + 25 (sugar) = 210 non-cockle calories, which would put the total at ~315 not 300. Minor internal inconsistency but within the stated estimate range and the disclaimer covers it."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the answer box text, change \"The other 160-odd calories come almost entirely from the oil in the sambal base\" to \"The other 200-odd calories come from the oil in the sambal base, the chili paste and aromatics, and the palm sugar\" to match the breakdown table.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"lala-masak","name":"Lala Masak","category":"lauk","serving":"1 hawker plate (approx 400g shell-on clams, ~150g edible serving)","servingGrams":150,"calories":250,"protein":18,"carbs":8,"fat":15,"metaDescription":"Lala Masak has about 250 calories per hawker plate. Clam meat is lean but that stir-fry oil adds up fast. Here's the full calorie breakdown.","summary":"Lala Masak is stir-fried clams cooked with garlic, cili padi or ginger, oyster sauce and a generous pour of oil at your average hawker stall.","answer":"A standard hawker plate of Lala Masak comes in at around 250 calories for the edible portion, which is roughly 150g of clam meat and sauce from a 400g shell-on serving. The clam meat itself is actually quite lean at about 59 calories, but the cooking oil alone accounts for close to 177 calories, so the shells are doing you a favour by keeping portion sizes honest. Protein sits at 18g, which is decent for a seafood side dish.","realTalk":["Lala are small saltwater clams and this dish is a Malaysian hawker staple. You order a plate, you get a heap of shells, you dig out the little nuggets of clam meat and you mop up the sauce with whatever carbs you ordered alongside. The clam meat itself is genuinely low calorie and high protein, very similar to other shellfish. The 250 calories here is a ballpark estimate, honestly, because reliable nutrition data for hawker-cooked lala is thin. Different stalls use wildly different amounts of oil and sauce, so treat this as a reasonable middle-ground guess rather than a precise number.","What actually swings the calories up or down is the oil. The hawker version uses around 1.5 tablespoons of oil in the wok, which contributes about 177 of those 250 calories. Kam Heong lala, which adds dried prawns, curry leaves and more sauce, runs closer to 300 calories. Sambal lala pushes to around 320 because sambal paste is oil-heavy by nature. The halia version with ginger and spring onion is the lightest at about 220 calories because the sauce is simpler and the oil tends to be more restrained. If you are eating lala as a side to white rice, the lala itself is not your problem. Watch the rice portion and whatever sauce you pour over it."],"lighter":["Order the masak halia version if available. The ginger and spring onion base is simpler, uses less oil, and saves you around 30 calories per plate.","Ask for kurang minyak if you can. Some stalls will actually listen. Even a small reduction in oil cuts 50 to 100 calories.","Skip soaking up the leftover sauce with bread or rice. The sauce sitting at the bottom of the plate carries a good chunk of that oil.","Pair lala with a steamed or boiled side rather than fried rice or noodles. The clam meat is lean enough that your overall meal stays reasonable.","Stick to one plate as a shared side rather than eating a full plate solo. At 250 calories it is fine, but lala is meant to be shared at the table anyway."],"variants":[{"name":"Lala masak halia (ginger and spring onion)","calories":220,"note":"Lighter sauce, less chili oil, ginger and spring onion base; roughly matches babe.com.my recipe data"},{"name":"Lala masak cili padi (bird's eye chili)","calories":250,"note":"Standard hawker version with cili padi, garlic, oyster sauce; moderate oil"},{"name":"Kam heong lala (dried shrimp, curry leaves, chili)","calories":300,"note":"Richer sauce with dried prawns and curry leaves adds more calories; Huang Kitchen reports 224-277 kcal in home portions"},{"name":"Sambal lala (with sambal paste)","calories":320,"note":"Sambal paste is oil-heavy; adds ~70 kcal vs plain stir-fry version"}],"components":[{"name":"Lala clam meat (cooked, ~80g yield from 400g shell-on)","grams":80,"calories":59},{"name":"Cooking oil (hawker stir fry, ~1.5 tbsp)","grams":20,"calories":177},{"name":"Sauce (oyster sauce, soy sauce, cili padi/ginger, garlic, spring onion)","grams":50,"calories":40}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Lala Masak?","a":"A hawker plate of Lala Masak is around 250 calories for the edible portion (roughly 150g of clam meat and sauce from a 400g shell-on plate). The clam meat is only about 59 calories; the cooking oil is what pushes it up to 250."},{"q":"What exactly is Lala Masak?","a":"Lala is a type of small saltwater clam common at Malaysian seafood stalls. Masak just means cooked. You get a plate of clams stir-fried with garlic, oyster sauce, soy sauce and usually either cili padi or ginger. You crack them open at the table and eat the little piece of clam meat inside."},{"q":"Is Lala Masak a good choice if I am trying to lose weight?","a":"The clam meat itself is great: high protein, low fat, low calorie. The issue is the stir-fry oil used at the hawker stall. If you can go for the masak halia version or ask for less oil, it sits around 220 calories and 18g protein, which is a solid side dish. Just do not drown your rice in the leftover sauce."}],"related":["kerang-masak","sambal-sotong","kangkung-belacan"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The headline says 250 kcal but the ingredient breakdown sums to 276 (59 clam meat + 177 oil + 40 sauce). The 250 figure is plausible for a hawker plate of lala, but the internal math contradicts it by 26 kcal. Either the headline or the breakdown needs correcting.","factualConcerns":["The calorie breakdown totals 276 kcal (59 + 177 + 40) but the headline and answer-box state 250 kcal. The numbers are internally inconsistent.","The answer-box describes the edible portion as 'roughly 150g of clam meat and sauce' but the breakdown lists 80g clam meat + 50g sauce = 130g, not 150g. The serving weight is inconsistent across the page."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Either adjust the headline to 280 kcal (rounding the 276 breakdown sum) and update the answer-box, meta description, and FAQ accordingly, or reduce the oil estimate from 1.5 tbsp (177 kcal) to about 1 tbsp (118 kcal) which would bring the total to roughly 220 kcal and align with the lighter-version claim. Also reconcile the edible portion weight: the answer-box says 150g but the breakdown adds up to 130g -- pick one and use it consistently.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"ikan-goreng","name":"Ikan Goreng","category":"lauk","serving":"1 piece (medium, e.g. kembung, tilapia slice, or cencaru)","servingGrams":140,"calories":230,"protein":25,"carbs":4,"fat":12,"metaDescription":"Ikan Goreng has around 230 calories per medium piece. Good protein, but that frying oil adds up fast. Here is what you need to know.","summary":"Ikan Goreng is your everyday Malaysian fried fish, rubbed with turmeric and a little flour then deep-fried until the skin goes crisp and golden.","answer":"One medium piece of ikan goreng, around 140g, comes in at roughly 230 calories with 25g protein and 12g fat. The fish itself is lean, so the calories mostly come from frying oil absorbed into the skin and that thin flour coating. If you are grabbing kembung from a nasi campur stall, expect around 180 calories for the smaller whole fish. Go for a big siakap fillet fried with thick batter and you are looking at 350 calories or more.","realTalk":["Ikan goreng is one of those dishes that sounds healthy because, well, it is fish. And honestly it is not bad. The fish flesh itself is lean protein, whether you are eating kembung (Indian mackerel), tilapia, or cencaru. The problem is the frying. About 80 of those 230 calories come purely from oil absorbed during frying, and that number is a ballpark estimate because solid nutrition data for Malaysian hawker-style fried fish is thin. Different fish sizes, oil temperatures, and how long it sits in the pan all change things. Treat 230 as a reasonable middle-ground guess, not gospel.","What really moves the calorie count is the fish size and what is stuffed into it. A small kembung goreng runs about 180 calories. A standard tilapia slice is around 230. Cencaru sumbat sambal, where they stuff chili-coconut sambal inside the whole fish, jumps to around 290 calories because of that filling. A big siakap goreng tepung with thick batter coating hits 350 calories, because more batter means way more oil absorbed. If you are trying to keep things tighter, stick to smaller fish or request it without the thick flour batter, and eat the flesh while leaving the skin (where most of the oil lives) on the plate. Pair it with rice and kangkung rather than lauk that come with heavy gravy, and you have a solid high-protein meal without doing too much damage."],"lighter":["Choose kembung or cencaru over big battered fillets like siakap goreng tepung. You save 100-170 calories just by picking the smaller fish.","Skip the thick flour batter. A light turmeric dusting absorbs far less oil than a proper coating.","Eat the flesh and leave the crispy skin. Most of the absorbed frying oil is concentrated in the skin.","Drain it on a paper towel or newspaper before eating, the hawker way actually does reduce surface oil a bit.","Pair with steamed dishes like ikan stim or sayur campur instead of another oil-heavy lauk, so the whole plate stays reasonable."],"variants":[{"name":"Ikan kembung goreng (small whole fish ~100g)","calories":180,"note":"Smaller Indian mackerel; less flesh, less oil absorbed"},{"name":"Ikan tilapia goreng (medium piece ~150g)","calories":230,"note":"Standard hawker cut; mild lean flesh with moderate oil uptake"},{"name":"Cencaru sumbat sambal (stuffed with sambal, whole)","calories":290,"note":"Cencaru stuffed with chili-coconut sambal adds 50-70 kcal from sambal filling"},{"name":"Ikan siakap/jenahak goreng tepung (larger fish, battered)","calories":350,"note":"Bigger fish fillet with thicker batter coating absorbs significantly more oil"}],"components":[{"name":"Fish flesh (lean white/mackerel, ~130g edible)","grams":130,"calories":120},{"name":"Frying oil absorbed (~9g)","grams":9,"calories":80},{"name":"Light flour/turmeric coating","grams":8,"calories":30}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Ikan Goreng?","a":"A medium piece of ikan goreng, around 140g, is approximately 230 calories. A small kembung goreng is around 180 calories, while a large battered fillet like siakap goreng tepung can hit 350 calories."},{"q":"What exactly is Ikan Goreng?","a":"Ikan goreng just means fried fish in Malay. At a nasi campur or nasi kandar stall you will usually see whole small fish like kembung or cencaru, or cut slices of tilapia, rubbed with turmeric and sometimes a little flour, then deep-fried until crisp. It is one of the most common side dishes in Malaysian everyday cooking."},{"q":"Is Ikan Goreng healthy for weight loss?","a":"It is decent, especially compared to other lauk. You get 25g of protein per piece, which is solid. The catch is 12g of fat, mostly from frying oil. If you stick to smaller fish without thick batter, it is one of the better protein options at a nasi campur stall. Just watch what else you pile on the plate."}],"related":["ikan-bakar","ikan-stim","ayam-goreng"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The cencaru sumbat sambal description calls the filling 'chili-coconut sambal'. Traditional cencaru sumbat uses a fresh herb-chili paste (typically belacan, lemongrass, turmeric leaf, torch ginger bud) — coconut is not a standard ingredient and its inclusion could mislead readers about the calorie source and the dish itself. Describing it simply as 'sambal' or 'spiced chili paste' would be more accurate."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In both the variant table and the \"real talk\" paragraph, change \"chili-coconut sambal\" to \"spiced chili paste\" or just \"sambal\". Example: \"Cencaru sumbat sambal (stuffed with sambal, whole)\" note could read \"Cencaru stuffed with a spiced chili-herb paste adds 50-70 kcal from the filling.\"","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"ikan-stim","name":"Ikan Stim","category":"lauk","serving":"1 whole fish (siakap or ikan merah, mid-size), as served at restaurant or kopitiam","servingGrams":280,"calories":310,"protein":45,"carbs":6,"fat":8,"metaDescription":"Ikan Stim is around 310 calories for a whole siakap or ikan merah. High protein, low fat, and one of the smartest orders at any kopitiam.","summary":"Ikan Stim is a whole fish, usually siakap or ikan merah, steamed and dressed with a light sauce of soy, sesame, ginger, lime, and chili, served at Chinese kopitiams and Malay restaurants across Malaysia.","answer":"A whole ikan stim at a typical kopitiam or restaurant comes to around 310 calories for a mid-size siakap or ikan merah (about 280g as served). You are getting 45g of protein out of that, which is genuinely impressive for a dish that tastes this good. The fat is only 8g and carbs are 6g, so this is about as lean as a restaurant lauk gets.","realTalk":["Ikan stim is steamed whole fish, typically siakap (barramundi) or ikan merah (red snapper), covered in a light sauce and finished with aromatics like ginger, chili, garlic, and coriander. It is a staple at Chinese kopitiams and plenty of Malay restaurants. At roughly 310 calories for the whole fish, about 216 of those calories come from the fish flesh itself. The sauce adds around 66 calories, mostly from the sesame oil and a bit of palm sugar. The aromatics are basically calorie noise. So honestly, the fish is just a really good deal, protein per calorie is one of the best you will find anywhere on a Malaysian menu. Fair warning though: these numbers are a ballpark estimate. Reliable lab data for steamed fish as cooked in Malaysian hawker settings is thin, and sauce quantities vary a lot between cooks.","What actually moves the number is the sauce. A heavier hand with sesame oil or more palm sugar can push the sauce component up by 30 to 50 calories without you noticing. The Thai-style stim limau version stays around 310 calories because the lime and fish sauce base is fairly light. The ginger steam version (stim halia) comes in a bit lower at 280 calories because it skips the sugar and heavier aromatics. If someone adds coconut milk to the sauce, which does happen in some Malay-style preparations, you are looking at 390 calories instead. Also worth noting: the edible flesh on a mid-size fish is roughly 200g out of the 280g total served. If you order a bigger fish, scale the numbers up accordingly."],"lighter":["Ask for the sauce on the side or request less of it, the sauce adds 66 calories and that is easy to halve just by not pouring the whole thing over.","Go for stim halia over the Thai-style version if you want to trim about 30 calories without sacrificing flavour.","Skip the white rice and pair it with sayur manis or a plain vegetable dish instead, the fish alone is filling enough with 45g of protein.","Do not eat the skin if you are being strict, most of the fat in steamed fish sits in and around the skin layer.","Order siakap over ikan merah if you have a choice, siakap flesh tends to be slightly leaner by default."],"variants":[{"name":"Stim halia (ginger steam, Chinese style)","calories":280,"note":"Lighter sauce using soy, sesame oil, ginger and scallion only. Slightly less sugar and chili than Thai-style."},{"name":"Stim limau ala Thai (lime and chili steam)","calories":310,"note":"Standard variant. Fish sauce, lime juice, chili, lemongrass, palm sugar. Typical hawker and kopitiam preparation."},{"name":"Stim dengan santan (with coconut milk sauce)","calories":390,"note":"Less common. Some Malay-style preparations add coconut milk to the sauce, adding roughly 80 kcal from the santan."}],"components":[{"name":"Steamed fish flesh (siakap/barramundi, ~200g edible)","grams":200,"calories":216},{"name":"Light soy and sesame sauce (soy sauce, fish sauce, sesame oil)","grams":45,"calories":66},{"name":"Aromatics and condiments (ginger, chili, garlic, lime, coriander, palm sugar)","grams":35,"calories":28}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Ikan Stim?","a":"A whole ikan stim (siakap or ikan merah, mid-size) as served at a kopitiam or restaurant is around 310 calories, with 45g protein, 8g fat, and 6g carbs for roughly 280g total."},{"q":"What exactly is ikan stim?","a":"It is a whole fish, usually siakap (barramundi) or ikan merah (red snapper), that is steamed and then poured over with a hot sauce made from soy, sesame oil, garlic, ginger, and sometimes chili or lime. The aromatics get slightly cooked by the heat of the sauce and fish together. Standard order at Chinese kopitiams and many Malay seafood restaurants."},{"q":"Is ikan stim a good choice if you are trying to lose weight?","a":"Bro, it is probably one of the best things on any Malaysian menu. 310 calories, 45g of protein, and it is genuinely filling. The only thing to watch is the sauce, which adds about 66 calories. Compare that to something like rendang or masak lemak and it is not even close."}],"related":["ikan-bakar","ikan-goreng","asam-pedas"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["Minor internal inconsistency: the answer box and FAQ describe the standard preparation using soy, sesame oil, garlic, and ginger (Chinese kopitiam style), but the variants table positions 'Stim limau ala Thai' (fish sauce, lime, lemongrass, palm sugar) as the 310 kcal standard. The two descriptions do not match the same dish. Neither is wrong about Malaysian food, but the page conflates them as the same baseline."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the answer box and FAQ, either align the sauce description to match the Thai-style limau version that the variants table calls the 310 kcal standard (\"fish sauce, lime, lemongrass, palm sugar, chili\"), or rename the 310 kcal variant in the table to reflect a general mixed Chinese/Thai preparation. The simplest fix is to update the answer box text to say \"dressed with a light sauce of fish sauce, lime, chili, and aromatics\" so it matches the stim limau ala Thai row that anchors the 310 kcal figure.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"sup-daging","name":"Sup Daging","category":"soup","serving":"1 bowl","servingGrams":510,"calories":400,"protein":38,"carbs":12,"fat":20,"metaDescription":"Sup Daging comes in around 400 kcal for a standard hawker bowl. Clear spiced beef soup, tender chunks, and it goes up fast if you add roti for dipping.","summary":"Sup Daging is a clear, warmly spiced Malaysian beef soup, usually served with tender beef shank or short rib chunks and eaten with bread for dipping.","answer":"A standard hawker bowl of Sup Daging is around 400 calories for roughly 510g of soup, beef, and veg. Most of that comes from the beef itself (about 300 kcal for 150g of shank or short rib), with the spiced broth adding another 60 kcal and the potato and carrot rounding out the rest. On its own it is actually a pretty reasonable meal, but add two pieces of roti for dipping and you are looking at closer to 580 kcal total.","realTalk":["Sup Daging is a clear spiced beef soup you find at Malay hawker stalls and warung, usually ordered alongside roti canai or a crusty French loaf. The broth is light and fragrant, flavoured with things like star anise, cinnamon, and cloves, but do not let \"clear soup\" fool you into thinking it is low calorie automatically. At 400 kcal a bowl, it is solidly in the middle range, and that is because beef shank and short rib are fatty cuts by nature. The rendered fat from the meat bleeds into the broth, and bone marrow versions (Sup Berempah) push it to 460 kcal. Fair warning though: these numbers are genuine ballpark estimates. Hard lab data for Malaysian hawker soups is thin, so take them as a reasonable guide rather than gospel.","What actually swings the number is the cut of beef and how much fat ends up in your bowl. Lean cuts like topside instead of shank can shave off 80 to 100 kcal without you noticing much. The broth itself at around 60 kcal per 300ml is not the villain, but stalls that skim less fat off the top will give you a noticeably richer, higher-calorie soup. Potato and carrot add about 45 kcal from 60g, which is fine. The real jump comes from the roti. Two pieces of roti canai or French loaf adds roughly 180 kcal and takes you to 580 kcal total. If you are watching calories, eat the soup on its own first. You might not even want the bread after that."],"lighter":["Order it without bread, or ask for just one piece of roti instead of two. That one swap saves around 90 to 180 kcal right there.","Ask for leaner cuts if you can. Topside or silverside instead of short rib means less fat rendered into the broth.","Skip the bone marrow version if calories matter. Marrow adds 60 to 80 kcal to an already decent bowl.","Drink the broth but leave some of the oilier layer at the bottom. Sounds fussy but the last third of a fatty soup has more calories than the first two thirds.","This soup is high in protein at 38g per bowl, so it keeps you full. Eat it slowly and you probably do not need rice or bread on the side."],"variants":[{"name":"Sup Daging (no potato, lean beef only)","calories":300,"note":"Home-style or diet version with leaner cuts, no starchy veg, lighter broth"},{"name":"Sup Daging standard hawker bowl","calories":400,"note":"Typical hawker serving with beef shank/short rib chunks, potato, carrot, spiced broth"},{"name":"Sup Daging Berempah (richly spiced, with bone marrow)","calories":460,"note":"More aromatic version with marrow bones and richer spiced broth; marrow adds 60-80 kcal"},{"name":"Sup Daging with roti (eaten with bread)","calories":580,"note":"Standard bowl plus 2 pieces roti canai or French loaf (~180 kcal) for dipping"}],"components":[{"name":"Beef chunks (shank/short rib, cooked, ~150g)","grams":150,"calories":300},{"name":"Spiced broth (~300ml with rendered beef fat)","grams":300,"calories":60},{"name":"Potatoes and carrots","grams":60,"calories":45}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Sup Daging?","a":"A standard hawker bowl of Sup Daging is around 400 calories for roughly 510g with beef chunks, spiced broth, potato, and carrot. The lean version without potato comes in closer to 300 kcal, and the berempah version with marrow can reach 460 kcal. Add roti for dipping and the total goes up to about 580 kcal."},{"q":"What is Sup Daging exactly?","a":"It is a Malaysian clear beef soup, spiced with aromatics like star anise, cloves, and cinnamon. The beef is usually shank or short rib, slow-cooked until tender. You get a clean, warmly spiced broth with soft beef chunks, sometimes potato and carrot, and it is almost always eaten with bread for dipping."},{"q":"Is Sup Daging a healthy choice for someone trying to lose weight?","a":"It is one of the better hawker options, bah. You get 38g of protein in a 400 kcal bowl, which is a solid ratio. The broth keeps you full, there is no rice baked in, and you control whether you add bread. Main thing to watch is the cut of beef and how fatty the broth is. Skip the roti or have just one piece, and it is a genuinely good diet meal."}],"related":["sup-kambing","sup-tulang","sup-ayam"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["FAQ 3 says \"there is no rice baked in\" as a positive, which is odd phrasing since rice is not a component of Sup Daging to begin with. It likely means no rice is served alongside by default, which is accurate, but the phrasing is slightly confusing."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In FAQ 3, change \\\"there is no rice baked in\\\" to \\\"it does not come with rice by default\\\" so the phrasing is unambiguous.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"sup-ikan","name":"Sup Ikan","category":"soup","serving":"1 bowl (clear sup ikan, fish pieces + tofu + veg + broth, no rice or noodles)","servingGrams":640,"calories":290,"protein":34,"carbs":4,"fat":10,"metaDescription":"Sup Ikan has about 290 calories per bowl, clear broth style. One of the leanest soups at any hawker stall. Calories and macros breakdown here.","summary":"Sup ikan is a clear Malaysian fish soup, usually ikan jenahak or kembung with tofu, tomatoes, and cabbage in a ginger-lemongrass broth, served at Malay hawker stalls and kopitiams.","answer":"A standard bowl of clear sup ikan comes in at around 290 calories for roughly 640g of food, with 34g of protein, 10g of fat, and only 4g of carbs. That's the version with fish pieces, soft tofu, veg, and broth but no noodles or rice. Add bihun and you're at 420 calories. Add a plate of white rice and you're at 580. The base bowl on its own is one of the leaner things you can order at a hawker stall.","realTalk":["Sup ikan is basically fish cooked in a clear, mildly spiced broth with aromatics like ginger and lemongrass, soft tofu, tomatoes, and whatever greens the stall has. It's a regular fixture at Malay hawker stalls and nasi campur places, and it's genuinely one of the lightest hot dishes you'll find. At 290 calories with 34g of protein, the clear version is almost unfairly good for a dieter. The fish does most of the heavy lifting here, 150g of cooked ikan jenahak or kembung sitting at 188 calories. The broth itself? Only 20 calories. Fair warning though: these are ballpark estimates. Hard calorie data on Malaysian hawker soups is thin, and the fish portion, oil used, and broth concentration will vary stall to stall.","What actually moves the numbers is what you order with it and how much oil the cook adds to the broth. The clear soup version keeps fat low at 10g, but an asam pedas-style sour fish soup with tamarind and dried chillies pushes you to 350 calories from the extra oil alone. Throw in a bowl of bihun, that's an extra 130 calories bringing you to 420 total. Order it with rice and you're at 580 for the full meal. If you want to stay at the 290 base, eat it plain, ask for less oil if you can, and go heavy on the fish and tofu rather than padding it with carbs."],"lighter":["Order clear sup ikan without bihun or rice, you save 130 to 290 calories right there.","Ask the stallholder to go light on oil in the broth. Most will oblige if you ask nicely.","Pile on the tofu and veg rather than asking for extra fish if cost is a concern, the tofu adds protein for very few calories.","Skip the asam pedas version if you're counting. The tamarind and chilli paste base adds oil that bumps you from 290 to 350.","Eat the soup first before the rice. You'll feel fuller faster and naturally eat less rice."],"variants":[{"name":"Clear sup ikan (no noodles/rice)","calories":290,"note":"Fish, tofu, veg in clear broth. Lightest version, typical Malay hawker or kopitiam bowl."},{"name":"Sup ikan with bihun (rice vermicelli)","calories":420,"note":"Add ~130g cooked bihun (~130 kcal). Common ordering style at kedai makan."},{"name":"Asam pedas-style sour fish soup","calories":350,"note":"Tamarind, dried chillies, and more oil push calories up. Usually served as a lauk with rice rather than a standalone bowl."},{"name":"Sup ikan dengan nasi (with white rice)","calories":580,"note":"Add 200g cooked white rice (~260 kcal). Full meal as typically eaten for lunch."}],"components":[{"name":"White fish pieces (ikan jenahak/kembung, cooked)","grams":150,"calories":188},{"name":"Clear broth (water, aromatics, ginger, lemongrass)","grams":350,"calories":20},{"name":"Soft tofu","grams":80,"calories":44},{"name":"Vegetables (tomato, cabbage, spring onion)","grams":60,"calories":12},{"name":"Oil/fat in broth","grams":3,"calories":26}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Sup Ikan?","a":"A standard bowl of clear sup ikan with fish, tofu, veg, and broth (no rice or noodles) is around 290 calories. Add bihun and it goes to 420. With a plate of white rice, you're looking at 580 calories for the full meal."},{"q":"What exactly is sup ikan?","a":"Sup ikan is a clear fish soup common at Malay hawker stalls and kopitiams. Usually ikan jenahak or kembung cooked in a light broth with ginger, lemongrass, soft tofu, tomatoes, and cabbage. Simple, clean, and genuinely filling for the calories."},{"q":"Is sup ikan actually healthy?","a":"Pretty much yes, for a hawker dish. 34g of protein and only 290 calories in a 640g bowl is a solid ratio. Low carbs, moderate fat, very filling. The main thing to watch is how much oil goes into the broth, and what you order alongside it."}],"related":["sup-ayam","asam-pedas","ikan-stim"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The prose says asam pedas-style sour fish soup pushes calories up \"from the extra oil alone\" but tamarind paste, chilli rempah, and sometimes coconut milk also contribute — oil is not the sole driver. Minor imprecision, not a hard error."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the \"real talk\" section, change \"from the extra oil alone\" to \"from the tamarind, chilli paste, and extra oil used in the base\" to be more accurate about what drives the calorie increase in asam pedas.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"sup-sayur","name":"Sup Sayur","category":"soup","serving":"1 bowl","servingGrams":320,"calories":60,"protein":2,"carbs":10,"fat":1,"metaDescription":"Sup sayur is about 60 calories per bowl. Clear vegetable soup with cabbage, carrot and tomato. Probably the most diet-friendly thing on any Malaysian menu.","summary":"Sup sayur bening is a clear mixed vegetable soup, usually cabbage, carrot and tomato simmered in a light garlic broth, and it is genuinely one of the lowest-calorie things you can order in Malaysia.","answer":"One bowl of sup sayur (about 320g) comes in at roughly 60 calories. That is it. Two grams of protein, 10g carbs, 1g fat. The vegetables themselves are almost free calorie-wise; the broth adds maybe 15 calories and there is a tiny bit of oil used in the base. If you are tracking and you need something warm and filling to pad out a meal without blowing your budget, this is pretty much the answer lah.","realTalk":["Sup sayur bening is as simple as it sounds: clear broth, aromatics like garlic and onion, then cabbage, carrot and tomato. No coconut milk, no heavy rempah. The 60 calories for a full 320g bowl is genuinely that low because vegetables are mostly water and fibre. The 15-calorie broth and 18 calories from the minimal oil used in the base are actually doing more numerical heavy lifting than the vegetables themselves. To be honest, these numbers are a ballpark estimate because solid lab data for this dish is thin. Kedai to kedai will vary, and some hawkers are heavier-handed with oil than others, but 60 to 80 calories is a reasonable range for the plain version.","What actually moves the maths here is what gets added. Stay plain and you are at 60 calories. Throw in 60g of soft tofu and you are at 100. Ask for chicken and you are up to about 120. Add 30g of cooked vermicelli and you are at 150, which now makes it a light noodle soup instead. The oil in the base is already minimal at 2g and 18 calories, but if you are at a stall that fries the aromatics more generously, you could creep toward 80 or 90 calories before any protein is added. Order it plain with rice on the side and you have basically used your soup allocation for nothing."],"lighter":["Order it bening, meaning plain, no protein added. That keeps it at 60 calories instead of 100 to 150.","If you want protein, ask for tofu instead of chicken. Sixty grams of soft tofu adds about 40 calories and keeps it light.","Avoid the version at places that fry the aromatics heavily. The base oil is supposed to be minimal. If the broth looks a bit greasy, that is where your extra calories are hiding.","Eat it with steamed rice rather than ordering the vermicelli version. Thirty grams of cooked vermicelli adds 90 calories and the volume difference is not that meaningful.","Use it to bulk out a meal: pair a bowl of sup sayur with a smaller portion of rice and one lauk. You get the volume and warmth without spending many calories on the soup itself."],"variants":[{"name":"Sup sayur bening (plain, no protein)","calories":60,"note":"Just cabbage, carrot, tomato in clear broth; the version described"},{"name":"Sup sayur with tofu","calories":100,"note":"Add 60g soft tofu; common in kedai kopi"},{"name":"Sup sayur ayam (with chicken)","calories":120,"note":"Add ~50g chicken; roughly 75 cal per 240g per Inlivo database, scaled to 320g bowl"},{"name":"Sup sayur with glass noodles (mihun/vermicelli)","calories":150,"note":"Add 30g cooked vermicelli; turns it into a light noodle soup"}],"components":[{"name":"Clear broth with aromatics (garlic, onion, salt)","grams":200,"calories":15},{"name":"Cabbage","grams":65,"calories":16},{"name":"Carrot","grams":40,"calories":16},{"name":"Tomato","grams":30,"calories":5},{"name":"Oil (minimal, used in base)","grams":2,"calories":18}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Sup Sayur?","a":"About 60 calories per bowl (320g), for the plain bening version with cabbage, carrot and tomato in clear broth. Add tofu and it goes to roughly 100, add chicken and it is about 120."},{"q":"What exactly is sup sayur?","a":"It is a clear Malaysian vegetable soup, usually with cabbage, carrot and tomato simmered in a light garlic and onion broth. No coconut milk, no curry paste. You find it at Malay kedai makan as a side to rice, and it is one of the mildest things on the menu."},{"q":"Is sup sayur actually good for weight loss?","a":"Bro, 60 calories for a full bowl of warm soup, yes. It is hard to find something this filling for this few calories. The fibre from the vegetables helps too. The only risk is if you order it with noodles added or from a stall that is heavy on the oil, which can push it toward 100 to 150 calories."}],"related":["sup-ayam","sup-daging","sayur-campur"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The headline says 60 kcal but the ingredient breakdown (15+16+16+5+18) sums to 70 kcal. Minor internal inconsistency. The 60 kcal figure itself is plausible for a plain bowl of clear vegetable soup.","factualConcerns":["The tips section states '30g of cooked vermicelli adds 90 calories', which is significantly overstated. 30g of cooked rice vermicelli is roughly 30 to 35 kcal (cooked weight is mostly water). The 90 kcal figure would be closer to 30g dry weight. This inflates the noodle variant to 150 kcal when 90 to 95 kcal would be more accurate.","The ingredient breakdown sums to 70 kcal (15+16+16+5+18) but the headline, answer box, and nutrition table all state 60 kcal. The numbers are internally inconsistent."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Fix the vermicelli calorie figure: change \"adds 90 calories\" to \"adds about 35 calories\" and update the noodle variant row from 150 kcal to roughly 95 kcal. Also reconcile the ingredient breakdown to sum to 60 kcal (e.g. reduce the oil from 18 kcal to 8 kcal by dropping the oil amount from 2g to 1g, which is defensible for a light hawker broth).","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"pulut","name":"Pulut","category":"snacks","serving":"1 serving, plain pulut with santan (steamed glutinous rice cooked in coconut milk)","servingGrams":160,"calories":250,"protein":4,"carbs":42,"fat":7,"metaDescription":"Plain pulut with santan is about 250 calories per 160g serving. Add sambal ikan bilis and you're at 310. Here's what you're actually eating.","summary":"Pulut is steamed glutinous rice cooked in coconut milk, dense and sticky, served plain or topped with sambal ikan bilis, sweet grated coconut, or as part of festive spreads.","answer":"Plain pulut with santan comes in at around 250 calories for a 160g serving, with 42g carbs and 7g fat. Most of those calories come from the glutinous rice itself (116 kcal) plus the coconut milk absorbed during steaming (69 kcal). Add sambal ikan bilis on top and you're looking at 310 calories. Go for the pulut inti with sweet grated coconut topping and it jumps to 370. It's a decent breakfast snack on its own, but the portions at the pasar pagi are usually bigger than you think.","realTalk":["Pulut is glutinous rice steamed with coconut milk until it's dense, sticky, and slightly rich. It's a staple at Malay breakfast stalls, pasar pagi, and big occasions like weddings and Hari Raya. Plain pulut with santan sits at around 250 calories per 160g serving, which sounds reasonable until you realise it's almost entirely carbs with very little protein (just 4g). The coconut milk adds a decent chunk of fat, 7g, and that's before any topping. These numbers are a ballpark estimate since hard nutritional data for traditional kuih like pulut is genuinely thin. Treat it as a close approximation, not a lab result.","What really moves the calories is the topping. Plain pulut is 250 kcal. Add 40g of sambal ikan bilis and you're at 310 kcal, the oil and fried anchovies doing the heavy lifting. Go for pulut inti, that sweet grated coconut cooked with gula melaka, and it climbs to 370 kcal, with the sugar and coconut fat adding 100 to 120 kcal on top. Portion size is the other big variable. A hawker scoop can easily be 200g or more, which pushes even plain pulut past 300 kcal before you add anything. If you're tracking, weigh it or at least be honest about how much you took."],"lighter":["Go for plain pulut with sambal on the side rather than piled on top, so you control how much sambal you actually use.","Skip the pulut inti if you're watching calories. That sweet coconut topping adds 100 to 120 kcal and zero protein.","Take a smaller scoop. A 100g portion of plain pulut is around 155 kcal, which is a much easier number to fit into your day.","Pair it with a boiled egg or some ikan bilis on the side instead of embedded in oily sambal. You get protein without as much extra fat.","Eat it as breakfast rather than a mid-morning snack on top of actual breakfast. Pulut is dense enough to be a proper meal if you portion it right."],"variants":[{"name":"Pulut kuning (turmeric coconut glutinous rice, plain)","calories":250,"note":"Similar to plain pulut; turmeric adds no significant calories. Often served at Malay weddings and Hari Raya, ~160g piece."},{"name":"Pulut sambal (with sambal ikan bilis)","calories":310,"note":"Add ~40g sambal ikan bilis on top; anchovy sambal contributes ~60-70 kcal from oil and fried anchovies. Most common hawker breakfast variant."},{"name":"Pulut inti (sweet grated coconut topping)","calories":370,"note":"Add ~40g kelapa parut (grated coconut) cooked with gula melaka; the sweet coconut topping adds ~100-120 kcal from sugar and coconut fat. Popular Nyonya kuih variant."}],"components":[{"name":"Cooked glutinous rice base","grams":120,"calories":116},{"name":"Coconut milk (santan) absorbed during steaming","grams":30,"calories":69},{"name":"Pandan, salt (negligible)","grams":10,"calories":5}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Pulut?","a":"Plain pulut with santan is around 250 calories per 160g serving. With sambal ikan bilis it goes up to about 310 calories, and with sweet grated coconut topping (pulut inti) it reaches around 370 calories."},{"q":"What is pulut exactly?","a":"Pulut is glutinous rice, a stickier, denser variety of rice, steamed with coconut milk and pandan. It's not regular rice. The texture is chewy and compact, and it's served as a snack or breakfast staple, either plain or with toppings like sambal ikan bilis or sweet grated coconut."},{"q":"Is pulut fattening?","a":"It's calorie-dense for the size, mostly from carbs and a bit of fat from the coconut milk. At 250 calories for a 160g serving with very little protein, it won't keep you full for long. As an occasional breakfast or snack it's fine. The issue is when you eat a big scoop plus a sweet topping and don't account for it."}],"related":["kuih-seri-muka","onde-onde","lemang"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The component breakdown sums to roughly 190 kcal (116 + 69 + 5), not the stated 250 kcal headline figure. The gap of ~60 kcal is unexplained. The 250 kcal total is still plausible for santan-cooked glutinous rice at 160g, but the itemised breakdown does not add up to it. Minor internal inconsistency, not a wrong total.","factualConcerns":["The itemised calorie breakdown (116 + 69 + 5 = 190 kcal) does not reconcile with the 250 kcal headline. The page does not explain where the remaining ~60 kcal come from. This is a presentation inconsistency rather than a wrong total, but a reader doing the math will notice."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the \"Where the calories come from\" breakdown, either adjust the component weights/amounts so they sum to 250 kcal, or add a row such as \"Additional coconut milk in rice (absorbed during resting/pressing)\" with the remaining ~60 kcal to close the gap.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"ketupat-sotong","name":"Ketupat Sotong","category":"lauk","serving":"1 medium squid stuffed with glutinous rice in coconut gravy","servingGrams":310,"calories":590,"protein":21,"carbs":80,"fat":19,"metaDescription":"Ketupat Sotong has around 590 calories per piece. Squid stuffed with pulut rice in sweet santan gravy, a Terengganu classic that hits harder than it looks.","summary":"Ketupat Sotong is a Terengganu and Kelantan specialty where whole squid is stuffed with glutinous rice then simmered low and slow in a sweet, rich coconut milk gravy.","answer":"One medium ketupat sotong comes in at around 590 calories, with 80g of carbs, 21g of protein, and 19g of fat. That is for a single stuffed squid piece sitting in its coconut gravy, roughly 310g total. Not a light dish by any stretch, but the squid itself is actually lean protein doing honest work. The calories are being driven by the pulut stuffing and the santan gravy around it.","realTalk":["Ketupat sotong is one of those east coast dishes that most Malaysians from KL or the west have never actually tried. Picture a whole squid body, cleaned and packed tight with raw glutinous rice, then sealed and braised in coconut milk sweetened with gula melaka until the pulut cooks inside the squid and absorbs all that gravy. The result is dense, sticky, sweet, and honestly very good. But at 590 calories for one piece, it earns its place on the higher end of the calorie list. The pulut stuffing alone accounts for about 240 calories, the thick santan gravy adds another 160, and the palm sugar in that same gravy throws in 80 more. The squid itself is only 92 calories. So you are really paying for the rice and the coconut sauce, not the seafood. Fair warning though: these numbers are a genuine ballpark estimate. Hard nutrition data for ketupat sotong is almost nonexistent, and portion sizes vary a lot between home cooking and pasar malam stalls, so treat this as a rough guide rather than a precise count.","What actually moves the maths here is gravy portion and squid size. A large squid stuffed more generously could easily push past 700 calories, while a smaller piece from a pasar ramadan stall might land closer to 450 to 500. If you are eating this at a kenduri or during Raya and someone ladles extra gravy on your plate, that 55g of coconut milk sauce in our estimate can quickly become 90 to 100g, adding another 100 calories right there. There is no real way to make a lighter version at a stall since you eat what you are served. If you are cooking at home, using light coconut milk (santan pekat campur air) instead of full-fat santan can bring the gravy calories down by roughly 40 to 50 calories per serving, and going easier on the gula melaka saves another 20 to 30. The squid is not the problem at all, bro. It is the stuff around it."],"lighter":["Ask for less gravy when possible, because that thick santan is where the bulk of the fat calories live. Even dropping from 55g to 30g of gravy saves around 80 calories.","Eat it without rice on the side. The pulut inside the squid is already your carb load for this meal, adding a plate of nasi on top pushes you well past 900 calories.","If cooking at home, swap full-fat santan for a diluted version, one part thick coconut milk to one part water. Same flavour profile, noticeably lighter gravy.","Pair it with ulam-ulaman or raw vegetables on the side rather than another heavy lauk. You get volume and fibre without adding significant calories.","One piece is genuinely a full meal. This is not a side dish, it is a main. Treat it that way and you will not feel the need to keep reaching for more."],"variants":[],"components":[{"name":"Squid body (cooked, ~100g)","grams":100,"calories":92},{"name":"Cooked glutinous rice (pulut) stuffing","grams":135,"calories":240},{"name":"Thick coconut milk gravy (santan)","grams":55,"calories":160},{"name":"Palm sugar / gula melaka in gravy","grams":20,"calories":80}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Ketupat Sotong?","a":"One medium ketupat sotong with coconut gravy is around 590 calories. That covers the squid body at 92 cal, the glutinous rice stuffing at 240 cal, the thick santan gravy at 160 cal, and the palm sugar in the gravy at 80 cal, for a total of roughly 310g of food."},{"q":"What exactly is Ketupat Sotong?","a":"It is a traditional dish from Terengganu and Kelantan where a whole squid is stuffed with raw glutinous rice, then simmered in sweetened coconut milk until the rice cooks inside the squid body. The result is a sticky, dense, slightly sweet dish usually eaten during Raya or at east coast pasar malam stalls."},{"q":"Is Ketupat Sotong healthy?","a":"The squid itself is lean and decent in protein at 21g per serving, which is fine. The problem is the glutinous rice and the sweet coconut gravy pushing the carbs to 80g and the overall calories to 590 for one piece. It is not something you want to eat daily if you are watching your intake, but as an occasional Raya dish it is absolutely worth it."}],"related":["nasi-kerabu","ayam-percik","masak-lemak-cili-api"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The eyebrow category label reads \"Meat, Grills & Lauk\" for a braised seafood dish. This is a taxonomy mismatch rather than a prose error, but it may confuse readers who expect ketupat sotong under a seafood or east coast category."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Change the eyebrow category from \"Meat, Grills & Lauk\" to \"Seafood\" or \"East Coast Dishes\" to better reflect what ketupat sotong is. Everything else is solid.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"masak-kicap","name":"Masak Kicap","category":"lauk","serving":"1 piece + sauce (bone-in chicken, hawker serving)","servingGrams":200,"calories":300,"protein":25,"carbs":14,"fat":15,"metaDescription":"Masak kicap comes in around 300 calories for a hawker chicken piece with gravy. Not a light dish, but not the worst either. Here's the full breakdown.","summary":"Masak kicap is protein braised in sweet dark soy sauce with onion and chili, the kind of dish that looks simple but sneaks in more calories than you'd expect from that dark, glossy gravy.","answer":"A standard hawker serving of ayam masak kicap, one bone-in chicken piece with gravy, comes to about 300 calories. You're getting 25g protein, 14g carbs, and 15g fat for that 200g portion. Not bad on the protein front, but roughly 65 of those calories come purely from the kicap manis gravy, and another 105 from the cooking oil. So yeah, the chicken itself is doing honest work, it's the sauce and frying that push the number up.","realTalk":["Masak kicap is basically protein, chicken, fish, egg, or beef, fried first then braised in kicap manis (sweet dark soy sauce) with some aromatics. The dish is popular across Malay warung and nasi campur stalls nationwide. It tastes way more indulgent than 300 calories sounds, because kicap manis is genuinely sugary, that gravy alone adds 65 calories to your plate. The cooking oil adds another 105 calories because the protein gets fried before braising. Worth flagging honestly: these numbers are a ballpark estimate since detailed lab data for masak kicap is thin, different stalls use wildly different amounts of oil and gravy, so treat this as a guide rather than gospel.","What actually moves the number is how heavy-handed the cook is with oil and how much gravy you spoon onto your rice. A portion with generous gravy poured over can easily add 20 to 30 extra calories compared to eating the piece dry. Swapping to ikan kembung masak kicap drops you to around 220 calories, or go for telur masak kicap at about 210 calories if you want the lowest option. Daging (beef) masak kicap runs higher at around 320 calories because beef adds more fat. If you're eating this at a nasi campur stall, watch the rice portion more than the lauk itself, 200g of white rice is already 260 calories before any lauk lands on your plate."],"lighter":["Ask for less gravy, or eat the chicken piece without soaking the rice in the sauce. That dark kicap gravy is 65 calories for 45g, and most stalls pour it generously.","Pick ikan kembung or telur over chicken if it's available. Telur masak kicap is around 210 calories, and ikan kembung sits at about 220 calories.","Skip the second piece. Seems obvious but bro, at a nasi campur stall it's tempting. One piece is already a solid protein hit at 25g.","Pair it with sayur campur or kangkung rather than extra rice to keep the overall meal calories manageable.","If you're cooking at home, reduce the kicap manis by half and use low-sodium soy sauce to stretch the flavour without dumping in as much sugar."],"variants":[{"name":"Telur masak kicap (fried egg)","calories":210,"note":"1 fried egg + kicap gravy; less protein but similar sugar-rich sauce; lighter overall"},{"name":"Ikan kembung masak kicap (Indian mackerel)","calories":220,"note":"1 whole fish ~80-100g; leaner than chicken but oily fish fat adds up; MOH data: 160 kcal/80g fish-only, add gravy ~220 kcal total"},{"name":"Ayam kampung masak kicap (free-range chicken)","calories":270,"note":"Leaner bird, less subcutaneous fat than broiler; similar gravy; slightly lower calories"},{"name":"Ayam masak kicap standard (broiler/regular chicken)","calories":300,"note":"Most common hawker version; bone-in thigh or drumstick with generous kicap gravy"},{"name":"Daging masak kicap (beef)","calories":320,"note":"Beef chuck or shank braised in kicap; higher fat from beef; MOH estimate 140 kcal/100g meat, add gravy and oil"}],"components":[{"name":"Chicken (bone-in piece, braised, ~100g edible meat)","grams":120,"calories":185},{"name":"Kicap manis + dark soy sauce + oyster sauce gravy","grams":45,"calories":65},{"name":"Cooking oil (frying + braising)","grams":12,"calories":105},{"name":"Aromatics (onion, shallots, garlic, chili)","grams":23,"calories":15}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Masak Kicap?","a":"A standard hawker serving of masak kicap, one bone-in chicken piece with gravy at around 200g total, comes to about 300 calories. That breaks down as 25g protein, 14g carbs, and 15g fat. The gravy and cooking oil account for about 170 of those calories combined."},{"q":"What exactly is masak kicap?","a":"Masak kicap means cooked in soy sauce, specifically kicap manis which is the thick, sweet dark soy sauce common in Malaysian cooking. The protein gets fried first, then braised in a sauce made with kicap manis, dark soy, oyster sauce, onion, and chili. It's a staple at Malay nasi campur and warung stalls."},{"q":"Is masak kicap a healthy choice for someone trying to lose weight?","a":"It's decent but not great. The protein content is solid at 25g per serving, which helps with satiety. The problem is the kicap manis adds sugar and the frying adds oil, so the calories come in higher than plain grilled chicken. It's a reasonable nasi campur pick as long as you control your rice portion and don't drown everything in extra gravy."}],"related":["rendang","masak-merah","ayam-goreng"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The headline says 300 kcal but the \"where the calories come from\" breakdown sums to 370 kcal (185 chicken + 65 gravy + 105 oil + 15 aromatics = 370). Either the headline number or one of the component figures is wrong. For a bone-in chicken piece with this much frying oil, 330-370 kcal is the more plausible range; 300 kcal likely undercounts the oil contribution.","factualConcerns":["The calorie breakdown components (185 + 65 + 105 + 15) sum to 370 kcal, not the stated 300 kcal headline. This internal arithmetic contradiction means either the total or one component figure is incorrect and must be reconciled before publishing."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Reconcile the breakdown with the headline. Either raise the headline to ~370 kcal and update the macro table (300 kcal per 200g = 150 kcal/100g looks low given the oil), or reduce the cooking oil figure from 105 kcal (12g oil) to around 35 kcal (4g oil) if the stall version is less heavily fried. The oil line at 12g/105 kcal is the most likely inflated figure for a braised dish where most oil stays in the pan; revising it to 4-5g absorbed oil (~35-45 kcal) would bring the total closer to 300 kcal and make the headline defensible.","severity":"major"}},{"dish":{"slug":"masak-merah","name":"Masak Merah","category":"lauk","serving":"1 piece bone-in chicken with sauce (ayam masak merah, no rice)","servingGrams":220,"calories":340,"protein":27,"carbs":14,"fat":20,"metaDescription":"Ayam masak merah has about 340 calories per piece with sauce. The fried chicken plus sweet tomato-chili gravy adds up fast, here is the full breakdown.","summary":"Masak merah is chicken (or fish, prawns, or beef) fried then simmered in a sweet, slightly spicy tomato and chili sauce, a fixture at kenduri spreads and everyday warung alike.","answer":"One bone-in piece of ayam masak merah with sauce comes in at around 340 calories for roughly 220g. That breaks down to 27g protein, 14g carbs, and 20g fat. The fried chicken itself accounts for most of it at 200 calories, but the sauce piles on another 140 or so from oil, ketchup, and sugar combined. Not a light lauk by any stretch, but not outrageous either if you are only having one piece.","realTalk":["Masak merah is one of those dishes that sounds healthier than it is because it looks saucy and tomatoey, not greasy. But that sauce has two calorie traps: cooking oil and sweet tomato ketchup. The fried chicken underneath adds another layer of fat from the pre-frying step before everything gets simmered together. At 340 calories for one bone-in piece, it is decent protein for the calorie cost (27g), but you are also getting 20g fat. Worth noting these numbers are a ballpark estimate since hard nutrition data for this dish is thin, and every mak cik's recipe is a bit different in how much oil and sugar goes in.","What really moves the number is two things: the protein you pick and how generous the sauce is. Swap chicken for udang masak merah and you drop to around 220 calories for a similar serving because prawns carry almost no fat. Ikan merah sits in the middle at about 260 calories. Go the other way with daging lembu and you are looking at 380 calories because beef cubes are fattier. For the chicken version, kenduri or catering style preparation can hit 430 calories from extra oil and sometimes santan thrown in. The sauce itself is only 45 calories, but the oil share per serving adds 88 calories on its own, so ladles of extra gravy over your rice are not free."],"lighter":["Go for udang or ikan merah instead of chicken and you save 80 to 120 calories per serving without touching the sauce at all.","Ask for less gravy, or scrape some off before eating. The oil in the sauce is where a big chunk of calories hide.","If you are cooking at home, cut the oil in the sauce by half and skip the ketchup, using fresh tomatoes instead. You can trim 50 to 80 calories per serving that way.","One piece only, lah. The chicken itself is already 200 calories before the sauce. Two pieces with rice and you are well past 800 calories for just that lauk and carb.","Pair it with plain steamed rice rather than nasi minyak or nasi tomato. Those festive rices add another 100 to 150 calories on top and they are usually served together at kenduri."],"variants":[{"name":"Udang masak merah (prawns)","calories":220,"note":"Prawns much lower fat than chicken; sauce calories similar but protein source leaner, ~200g serving"},{"name":"Ikan merah masak merah (red snapper)","calories":260,"note":"Whole fish fillet portion, leaner than chicken but sauce oil still contributes"},{"name":"Ayam masak merah (chicken, warung/hawker)","calories":340,"note":"1 bone-in piece fried then simmered; typical single lauk serving"},{"name":"Daging lembu masak merah (beef)","calories":380,"note":"Beef cubes are fattier than chicken; similar sauce, ~150-160g meat per serving"},{"name":"Ayam masak merah (kenduri/catering, extra oil)","calories":430,"note":"Festive preparation uses more oil and sometimes santan; Rasa Malaysia recipe estimate"}],"components":[{"name":"Chicken (fried, bone-in piece, ~90g meat)","grams":110,"calories":200},{"name":"Masak merah sauce (tomato, chili paste, spices)","grams":60,"calories":45},{"name":"Cooking oil in sauce (per serving share)","grams":10,"calories":88},{"name":"Sugar and tomato ketchup in sauce","grams":40,"calories":40}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Masak Merah?","a":"A standard serving of ayam masak merah, one bone-in chicken piece with sauce and no rice, is around 340 calories. The fried chicken contributes about 200 of those, with the oil and sweet tomato sauce making up the rest."},{"q":"What exactly is masak merah?","a":"Masak merah literally means 'cooked red'. It is a Malaysian dish where protein (most commonly chicken, but also red snapper, prawns, or beef) is first fried, then simmered in a sauce made from blended chili paste, tomatoes, tomato ketchup, and spices. It is a staple at kenduri (feasts) and everyday warung, and it sits somewhere between a curry and a sambal in terms of heat level."},{"q":"Is masak merah a good choice if I am watching my calories?","a":"It is okay but not the lightest option. At 340 calories per chicken piece you get solid protein (27g), which is decent value. The issue is the 20g fat from the fried chicken and oil-heavy sauce. If you want a lower-calorie version of the same dish, order udang masak merah instead, prawns drop the count to around 220 calories for a similar portion."}],"related":["rendang","asam-pedas","sambal-tumis"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"Minor internal inconsistency only: the \"where the calories come from\" breakdown rows sum to 373 kcal (200+45+88+40) but the headline and table both say 340 kcal. The headline figure itself is plausible for a bone-in fried chicken piece with masak merah sauce at 220g total.","factualConcerns":[],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Fix the breakdown rows to sum to 340 kcal. The simplest fix: reduce the oil line from 88 kcal to 55 kcal (reflecting ~6g oil rather than 10g, still plausible for a per-serving share), which gives 200+45+55+40 = 340 kcal exactly.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"banana-leaf-rice","name":"Banana Leaf Rice","category":"rice","serving":"1 standard meal (rice + 3 vegetable sides + dhal + 1 curry protein + rasam + papadom + 1 rice refill)","servingGrams":680,"calories":850,"protein":29,"carbs":133,"fat":19,"metaDescription":"Banana Leaf Rice has around 850 calories for a standard meal with one rice refill. But free refills make it easy to hit 1,100 without even noticing.","summary":"South Indian rice served on a banana leaf with vegetable sides, dhal, curry, rasam and papadom, where free refills are the norm and the calorie count climbs fast.","answer":"A standard banana leaf rice meal with one rice refill, three vegetable sides, dhal, one piece of chicken or fish curry, rasam and papadom comes to about 850 calories across roughly 680g of food. The rice alone (initial portion plus one top-up, around 350g cooked) carries 455 of those calories, which tells you everything you need to know about where the number comes from.","realTalk":["Banana leaf rice is a South Indian meal served, as the name says, on an actual banana leaf. You get a mound of white rice surrounded by small portions of vegetable sides (usually cabbage, long beans, papadam and something fried), dhal, rasam (a thin tamarind soup), and your choice of curry protein. The whole thing costs maybe RM8 to RM12 and the rice and curry refills are free. That last part is where it gets dangerous. One rice refill is already baked into the 850 calorie estimate above, and honestly that is being conservative. Most people take two refills, sometimes three, without thinking about it because the uncle just shows up and piles more rice on your leaf before you have time to say no. Two rice refills plus an extra meat curry portion puts you at around 1,150 calories without any dessert. To be fair, hard calorie data for banana leaf rice is pretty thin out there, so treat this as a solid ballpark rather than a lab result.","What actually moves the number is rice volume and gravy. The 350g cooked rice with one refill accounts for 455 calories. Every additional rice refill adds roughly 230 calories. The vegetable sides sound light but they are cooked in oil, so the dhal plus three veg sides already add up to about 145 calories. Your protein choice matters less than you think: chicken or fish curry at about 120g with gravy is around 175 calories, but swapping to fish head curry adds roughly another 100 calories from the rich coconut-based gravy. If you want to control the damage, the move is simple: wave off the second rice refill, skip extra gravy pours, and stick to one protein. That alone keeps you at 850 instead of 1,150."],"lighter":["Take only one rice refill instead of two. That single decision saves around 230 calories straight away.","Wave off extra curry gravy pours. The gravy is coconut and oil based and adds up fast even in small amounts.","Choose fish curry over fish head curry as your protein. Fish head curry is noticeably richer and oilier, adding around 100 calories for a shared portion.","Eat all the rasam you want, it is basically free calories. But go easy on the papadom if they keep bringing extras, each piece adds about 35 to 40 calories.","If you are going vegetarian, you naturally skip the meat curry and save around 175 calories, landing closer to 650 for the full meal with one refill."],"variants":[{"name":"Vegetarian (no meat curry, extra veggie sides)","calories":650,"note":"Rice + dhal + 3-4 vegetable sides + rasam + papadom, no meat protein. Still 550-700 cal with refill."},{"name":"Standard with chicken curry (1 refill)","calories":850,"note":"Most common order. Free rice and curry refills are the norm; this counts one rice top-up."},{"name":"Fish head curry add-on (shared portion)","calories":950,"note":"Fish head curry is rich and oily; adding a shared portion of ~120g gravy bumps total by ~100 cal."},{"name":"2 rice refills + extra meat curry","calories":1150,"note":"The refill trap. Two rice top-ups plus a second protein portion is easy to hit without noticing. Common among big eaters."}],"components":[{"name":"White rice (initial + 1 refill, ~350g cooked)","grams":350,"calories":455},{"name":"Chicken or fish curry (1 piece, ~100g with gravy)","grams":120,"calories":175},{"name":"Dhal + 3 vegetable sides (oil-cooked)","grams":160,"calories":145},{"name":"Papadom (1 piece) + rasam","grams":30,"calories":70}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Banana Leaf Rice?","a":"A standard banana leaf rice meal with one rice refill, three vegetable sides, dhal, one curry protein, rasam and papadom comes to about 850 calories. Go for two rice refills and an extra meat curry and you are looking at around 1,150 calories."},{"q":"What exactly is banana leaf rice?","a":"It is a South Indian meal served on a banana leaf instead of a plate. You get white rice with small portions of vegetable sides, dhal, rasam (a thin tangy soup) and curry. The banana leaf is not eaten, it just keeps the food warm and adds a subtle flavour. Rice and curry refills are usually free, which is the whole refill trap."},{"q":"Is banana leaf rice healthy?","a":"It is a balanced enough meal with protein, vegetables and carbs all on one leaf. The problem is portion control because the free refill culture makes it easy to eat way more than you planned. Stick to one rice refill and reasonable curry portions and it is a decent 850 calorie meal. Let the uncle keep topping you up and you are easily past 1,100 calories without realising."}],"related":["nasi-kandar","roti-canai","thosai"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["Rasam is described as 'a thin tamarind soup' in the FAQ schema and 'a thin tangy soup' in the visible FAQ. Rasam is more accurately a thin peppery soup made with tamarind, tomato, black pepper, and spices — tamarind alone does not define it. Minor inaccuracy, not misleading about calories."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In both the visible FAQ and the JSON-LD schema, change \"a thin tamarind soup\" / \"a thin tangy soup\" to \"a thin, peppery soup made with tamarind, tomato and spices\" to more accurately describe rasam.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"kari-ayam","name":"Kari Ayam (Chicken Curry)","category":"lauk","serving":"1 serving, 1 bone-in chicken piece with coconut curry gravy (hawker / nasi campur style)","servingGrams":280,"calories":350,"protein":26,"carbs":14,"fat":21,"metaDescription":"Kari Ayam has about 350 calories per serving at hawker style. Here is what is actually driving those numbers and how to order smarter.","summary":"Kari Ayam is bone-in chicken cooked in a spiced coconut milk gravy, the kind you scoop over rice at nasi campur stalls or eat with roti canai at the mamak.","answer":"One serving of Kari Ayam, which is one bone-in chicken piece with coconut curry gravy at a hawker or nasi campur stall, comes in at around 350 calories. That is for roughly 280g total: the chicken piece itself, a good ladleful of gravy, and usually a chunk of potato in there too. The protein is decent at 26g, but the fat sits at 21g, mostly from the santan and cooking oil in the gravy.","realTalk":["Kari Ayam is a classic Malaysian chicken curry, the one you see at every nasi campur counter and mamak stall from KL to Kota Belud. The chicken is braised in a spiced gravy made with coconut milk, dried chilies, lemongrass and a bunch of rempah. It is genuinely good food. At 350 calories a serving, it is not terrible either, but just know that the chicken itself only accounts for about 190 of those calories. The gravy, that thick santan-based liquid you keep pouring over your rice, is where the other 120 calories live. To be honest about the numbers here, hard data on Malaysian hawker dishes is thin, so treat 350 as a reasonable ballpark, not a lab result.","What actually swings the calories is how much gravy you take and which stall you are at. A mamak-style kari ayam uses thinner gravy with less santan and drops to around 300 calories, but it can be oilier so it is not automatically lighter. The kampung-style version with thick rich santan runs about the same 350 calories if the portion is similar. Add a 80-100g potato in the gravy and you are up to 380 calories, mostly from extra carbs. Ask for chicken breast instead of thigh and you drop to about 290 calories because the meat has less fat. And if you are scooping this over 200-250g of white rice for a full nasi kari plate, add another 270 calories and you are sitting at 620 total. The gravy ladle is the real lever here. One ladle or two makes a genuine difference."],"lighter":["Ask for chicken breast or daging dada instead of thigh or drumstick, saves you about 60 calories on the meat alone.","Tell the auntie or uncle less gravy, or drain some off yourself before eating. That thick santan sauce is where most of the fat calories are hiding.","Skip the potato or eat less of it if you are already having rice, no need to double up on carbs.","Pair it with white rice rather than roti canai. Roti adds another 300-plus calories before you even think about the curry.","Order it with ulam or blanched vegetables on the side instead of a second lauk. You get more volume, way fewer calories."],"variants":[{"name":"Mamak-style (thinner gravy, less santan, more oil)","calories":300,"note":"Thinner curry with chilli, tomato and light coconut milk; less santan but can be oilier"},{"name":"Kampung-style (thick santan gravy, no potato)","calories":350,"note":"Richer coconut base, thick and creamy; similar calories to standard if same portion"},{"name":"Kari ayam with potato (restaurant / nasi padang style)","calories":380,"note":"Includes 80-100g potato in gravy, adds ~70 kcal in carbs"},{"name":"Kari ayam daging (chicken breast piece, less fat)","calories":290,"note":"Leaner cut, similar gravy; lower fat and overall calories"},{"name":"Kari ayam with rice (nasi kari)","calories":620,"note":"Add 200-250g steamed white rice (~270 kcal) to standard serving"}],"components":[{"name":"Bone-in chicken thigh/drumstick (cooked, ~130g edible meat)","grams":130,"calories":190},{"name":"Coconut milk curry gravy (~110ml, santan + oil + spices)","grams":110,"calories":120},{"name":"Potato (in gravy, ~40g)","grams":40,"calories":40}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Kari Ayam (Chicken Curry)?","a":"A standard hawker serving of Kari Ayam, one bone-in chicken piece with coconut curry gravy, is around 350 calories. That covers roughly 280g of food including the chicken, gravy and a small potato. Mamak-style with thinner gravy is closer to 300, and a full nasi kari plate with rice is about 620 calories."},{"q":"What exactly is Kari Ayam?","a":"Kari Ayam is Malaysian chicken curry. Bone-in chicken pieces, usually thigh or drumstick, are cooked in a spiced coconut milk gravy with dried chilies, lemongrass, galangal and various rempah. You find it everywhere, from nasi campur counters to mamak stalls to kampung kitchens. Each place makes it a bit different, but the santan-based gravy is the common thread."},{"q":"Is Kari Ayam a good choice when dieting?","a":"It is not bad if you are smart about it. The chicken itself gives you 26g of protein per serving which is solid, and the dish fills you up. The issue is the gravy, which carries most of the 21g of fat. If you go easy on the gravy and pair it with a moderate amount of rice instead of piling it on, 350 calories for a filling, protein-forward meal is genuinely reasonable."}],"related":["rendang","masak-lemak-cili-api","nasi-kandar"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The variants table labels the chicken breast option as 'Kari ayam daging (chicken breast piece, less fat)' but 'daging' in Malay means meat or beef, not chicken breast. The correct term is 'dada ayam' or 'ayam dada'. The tips section gets it right ('daging dada') but the table heading uses 'daging' alone, which is misleading and would confuse readers ordering at a stall."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the variants table, change the row label from \"Kari ayam daging (chicken breast piece, less fat)\" to \"Kari ayam dada (chicken breast piece, less fat)\" to use the correct Malay term for chicken breast.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"kari-kambing","name":"Kari Kambing (Mutton Curry)","category":"lauk","serving":"1 mamak/kopitiam serving (meat + gravy, no rice)","servingGrams":300,"calories":400,"protein":30,"carbs":7,"fat":28,"metaDescription":"Kari Kambing packs around 400 calories per mamak serving before rice or bread. Here is what is actually in that rich gravy and how to keep it in check.","summary":"Kari Kambing is a rich, spiced goat or mutton curry simmered in coconut milk gravy, a staple at mamak stalls and kopitiams usually eaten with roti canai or rice.","answer":"One standard mamak serving of Kari Kambing (about 300g, meat and gravy, no rice) comes in at roughly 400 calories, with 30g protein, 28g fat, and only 7g carbs. The mutton itself is solid on protein but goat and sheep fat is naturally higher than chicken, and the coconut milk gravy adds a real chunk of the calories on top of that.","realTalk":["Kari Kambing is one of those dishes where the meat is actually decent for protein, but the fat content is no joke. You get 30g of protein from the bone-in mutton pieces, which is great, but 28g of fat comes along for the ride too. The cooking oil, the coconut milk, and the natural fat in goat meat all stack up fast. To be straight with you, these numbers are a ballpark estimate because solid nutritional data for mamak-style kari kambing is genuinely thin. Every stall cooks it differently, some use way more coconut milk, some drown it in oil, so treat 400 calories as a reasonable midpoint rather than a precise lab figure.","What actually swings the number is the gravy. If you scoop heavy on the gravy you are easily adding another 50 to 80 calories per extra ladle just from coconut milk and oil. The bone-in pieces (about 160g as served, roughly 130g edible meat) account for 225 calories on their own. Ask for less gravy and you can probably shave 50 to 70 calories off the total. Pairing it with naan adds about 190 calories, white rice adds roughly 220 calories, so if you are watching numbers, roti canai in moderation or even plain bread is worth thinking about versus loading up on both rice and a full ladle of gravy."],"lighter":["Ask the uncle for less gravy, specifically the coconut milk heavy part. That alone can cut 50 to 70 calories.","Eat it with chapati or one small roti instead of naan, saves you around 80 to 100 calories compared to naan.","Skip the potatoes in the curry if you are being strict, though honestly the potato portion here is small at about 20g, so prioritise reducing the gravy over the potato.","Order Kari Kambing Kosong if the stall offers it. That drier, lighter gravy version saves you about 70 calories per serving.","Pair with steamed rice rather than fried rice so your carb side stays clean and low calorie."],"variants":[{"name":"Kari Kambing Kosong (no potato, lighter gravy)","calories":330,"note":"Drier style, less gravy, fewer potatoes; common in some mamak stalls"},{"name":"Standard kari kambing (mamak/kopitiam)","calories":400,"note":"Typical rich gravy with coconut milk, bone-in pieces, a few potato chunks"},{"name":"Kari Kambing with Naan (1 piece)","calories":590,"note":"Add ~190 kcal for one plain naan; common mamak pairing"},{"name":"Kari Kambing with White Rice (1 cup)","calories":620,"note":"Add ~220 kcal for one cup cooked rice; nasi kandar style"}],"components":[{"name":"Bone-in goat/mutton pieces (~130g edible meat)","grams":160,"calories":225},{"name":"Coconut milk and spiced curry gravy","grams":100,"calories":130},{"name":"Cooking oil (palm/vegetable)","grams":20,"calories":175},{"name":"Potato and aromatics (onion, tomato, chilli)","grams":20,"calories":25}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Kari Kambing (Mutton Curry)?","a":"A standard mamak or kopitiam serving of Kari Kambing, about 300g of meat and gravy without rice, is around 400 calories. This goes up to about 590 calories if you add one naan, or around 620 calories with a cup of white rice."},{"q":"What is Kari Kambing and how is it different from chicken curry?","a":"Kari Kambing is a Malaysian mutton or goat curry cooked in a spiced coconut milk gravy with onion, tomato, and chilli. It is richer and fattier than chicken curry because goat and mutton have more natural fat in the meat itself, and mamak stalls tend to cook it in a heavier, more aromatic gravy than your average kari ayam."},{"q":"Is Kari Kambing a good choice if I am trying to lose weight?","a":"It is not terrible but it is not light either. You get 30g of protein which is solid, but 28g of fat per serving means the calories stack up fast. If you control your gravy portion and skip the naan, it fits reasonably into a calorie deficit. The problem is most people pair it with rice and roti, and suddenly you are at 600 to 700 calories for that one dish alone."}],"related":["kari-ayam","sup-kambing","rendang"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The FAQ and body text say kari kambing is richer and fattier than chicken curry because goat and mutton have more natural fat in the meat itself. This is overstated — goat is actually one of the leaner red meats (leaner than lamb or beef). The page correctly identifies the real culprits as coconut milk and cooking oil in the breakdown section, so there is an internal inconsistency. The overall calorie conclusion is still sound, but the explanation of why mutton curry is fattier than kari ayam slightly misattributes the cause to the meat rather than the heavier gravy."],"voiceIssues":["One tip reads \"so your carb side stays clean and low calorie\" — \"clean\" edges toward the banned clean-eating idiom, though it is borderline rather than a clear fail."],"fixSuggestion":"In the FAQ answer for question 2, change \"because goat and mutton have more natural fat in the meat itself\" to \"because mamak-style kari kambing is cooked in a heavier coconut milk gravy with more oil than a typical kari ayam, and mutton does carry more fat than chicken breast even if it is leaner than beef.\" Also change \"clean and low calorie\" in the tips list to \"lower calorie\" to avoid the clean-eating echo.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"tandoori-chicken","name":"Tandoori Chicken","category":"lauk","serving":"1 quarter piece (leg or breast, bone-in)","servingGrams":200,"calories":290,"protein":34,"carbs":6,"fat":13,"metaDescription":"Tandoori chicken runs about 290 calories per quarter piece (200g) with 34g protein. One of the better mamak protein picks, especially skinless.","summary":"Yogurt and spice marinated chicken roasted in a tandoor, served at mamak stalls usually alongside naan, and one of the leaner high-protein options you can actually order without feeling bad about it.","answer":"A quarter piece of tandoori chicken (leg or breast, bone-in, about 200g) comes in at around 290 calories with 34g of protein and 13g of fat. That makes it a solid mamak order if you are watching your calories, especially compared to the roti canai or mee goreng you were probably also eyeing. Note that these are ballpark numbers; hard nutrition data for mamak-style tandoori is thin, so treat 290 kcal as a good estimate rather than a precise lab result.","realTalk":["Tandoori chicken is basically marinated chicken roasted at very high heat, so most of the fat you get is from the meat itself and whatever skin stays on, not from oil being poured over it. The yogurt and spice marinade contributes next to nothing calorie-wise, roughly 20 calories of absorbed marinade out of 290 total. The majority of those 290 calories comes from about 160g of actual edible chicken meat (240 kcal) plus a thin layer of skin (30 kcal). If you are someone who used to demolish three pieces of fried chicken at a sitting, bro, a quarter tandoori with the skin is genuinely not a disaster. Fair warning though: these numbers are a ballpark estimate because standardised data for mamak tandoori specifically is pretty much nonexistent. Different stalls use different marinades and some baste with butter toward the end, which will push calories up.","The thing that actually changes the maths is whether you have skin or not, and what you eat it with. Go skinless breast and you drop to roughly 220 calories with more protein per bite. Keep the skin on a leg quarter and you stay closer to 290. Where people quietly blow their budget is the naan that comes with it. One plain naan is around 280 to 300 calories on its own, so your 290-calorie tandoori just became a 580-calorie meal before you even touched the mint chutney. Swap the naan for chapati (around 120 calories each) or just ask for extra salad on the side and you keep the whole meal under 450 calories while still feeling like you ate properly."],"lighter":["Go skinless breast quarter instead of leg, saves you about 70 calories and bumps the protein-to-calorie ratio considerably.","Skip the naan or swap it for chapati. That single swap cuts roughly 150 to 180 calories from your meal.","Ask for the mint chutney on the side and dip rather than pour. The chutney itself is fine calorie-wise but it is easy to use three times more than you need.","Eat it before the rice, not with the rice. If you start with the protein you will naturally eat less carbs.","If the stall lets you, ask for the skin-off version and request it without any butter baste at the end."],"variants":[{"name":"Skinless breast quarter","calories":220,"note":"Breast piece without skin is significantly leaner, around 25-28g protein at roughly 110 kcal per 100g edible"},{"name":"Standard quarter (leg or breast, some skin)","calories":290,"note":"Typical mamak serving as described above"},{"name":"Full half chicken","calories":560,"note":"Some mamak stalls sell half chicken portions, roughly double the quarter"}],"components":[{"name":"Chicken meat (roasted, ~160g edible after bone)","grams":160,"calories":240},{"name":"Skin (partial, thin layer)","grams":15,"calories":30},{"name":"Yogurt and spice marinade absorbed","grams":25,"calories":20}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Tandoori Chicken?","a":"A standard quarter piece (leg or breast, bone-in, around 200g) has approximately 290 calories, with 34g protein and 13g fat. Go skinless breast and it drops to around 220 calories."},{"q":"What is tandoori chicken exactly?","a":"It is chicken marinated in yogurt and spices (usually cumin, coriander, chilli, garlic, ginger) then roasted in a tandoor, which is a clay oven that hits very high temperatures. The high heat chars the outside and keeps the inside moist. You will find it at most mamak restaurants, usually served with naan and some sliced onions."},{"q":"Is tandoori chicken a good choice for weight loss?","a":"Honestly, yes, it is one of the better things on a mamak menu. 34g of protein for 290 calories is a decent deal. The thing to watch is what you eat with it. Pair it with naan and rice and the total meal climbs fast. Eat it with chapati or salad instead and it is a genuinely solid high-protein meal."}],"related":["naan","kari-ayam","ayam-goreng"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["Minor internal inconsistency: prose says naan is '280 to 300 calories' but the related card on the same page lists naan at 270 kcal. Not a meaningful factual error given the estimate caveat, but the numbers should agree."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the 'real talk' section, change '280 to 300 calories' to 'around 270 calories' to match the figure on the related naan card.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"naan","name":"Naan","category":"mamak","serving":"1 piece (plain)","servingGrams":90,"calories":270,"protein":8,"carbs":45,"fat":6,"metaDescription":"Plain naan from the mamak is about 270 calories per piece. Butter or cheese naan? That climbs fast. Here's what you need to know before you order.","summary":"Naan is a leavened flatbread from the tandoor oven, a staple at Indian Muslim restaurants and mamak stalls, eaten with curry, dal, or just dipped in whatever gravy is on the table.","answer":"One plain naan (about 90g) is roughly 270 calories, with 45g carbs, 8g protein, and 6g fat. That's before any butter hits it. Order butter naan and you're looking at around 370 calories. Cheese naan pushes up to 430. The numbers here are ballpark estimates since mamak portions vary a fair bit, but they give you a solid working figure.","realTalk":["Naan is essentially a refined flour flatbread, leavened with a bit of yogurt and cooked in a super-hot tandoor clay oven. It's softer and fluffier than roti canai, and at 270 calories for a plain piece it's not outrageous on its own, but it's almost pure carbs with very little to slow down digestion. The dough has a small amount of oil or ghee baked in, which accounts for a chunk of the calories. Most of the calorie damage actually happens after it leaves the oven, when the mamak uncle slathers butter on top. That's where butter naan at 370 cal and cheese naan at 430 cal come from. These are estimates though, honest, because every mamak has a slightly different recipe and a very different definition of how much butter is \"enough\".","The thing that really changes the maths is what you pair it with. One naan with a bowl of dhal (around 150 cal) is a reasonable meal. One naan with a heavy mutton curry or korma, plus another piece because the first one went too fast? You can easily hit 700 to 900 calories without thinking about it. Swap butter naan for plain, you save roughly 100 calories. Order one piece instead of two and you save another 270. If you're eating naan as part of a restaurant meal, that's probably your entire carb budget for the sitting, so maybe skip rice on the side, bro."],"lighter":["Order plain instead of butter or garlic naan. That single swap saves you 70 to 160 calories per piece.","Stick to one piece and load up on the protein side instead, like tandoori chicken or a dal that's not swimming in oil.","Use naan to scoop dal or a light lentil curry rather than dunking it repeatedly in a heavy coconut-based gravy.","Ask if you can get it without the butter brush on top. Some mamak places will do this if you ask nicely.","Split one naan between two people as a side and let the main dish do the work."],"variants":[{"name":"Plain naan","calories":270,"note":"Just flour, yogurt, a little oil in the dough, baked in tandoor. Decent carb load but no added fat on top."},{"name":"Garlic naan","calories":340,"note":"Garlic paste plus a brush of butter or margarine after baking adds roughly 60-70 extra calories."},{"name":"Butter naan","calories":370,"note":"Generous knob of butter melted on top after baking, adds roughly 100 cal over plain."},{"name":"Cheese naan","calories":430,"note":"Processed cheese or mozzarella stuffed inside or melted on top adds around 120-160 extra cal; highest calorie variant."}],"components":[{"name":"Refined wheat flour (dough base)","grams":65,"calories":235},{"name":"Yogurt and milk in dough","grams":15,"calories":12},{"name":"Oil or ghee in dough","grams":8,"calories":70}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Naan?","a":"One plain naan (90g) is about 270 calories. Garlic naan goes up to around 340 cal, butter naan to 370 cal, and cheese naan to 430 cal. These are estimates based on typical mamak portions."},{"q":"What actually is naan and how is it different from roti canai?","a":"Naan is a leavened flatbread made with wheat flour, yogurt, and a little oil, then cooked in a tandoor clay oven at very high heat. Roti canai is unleavened, made with a lot more oil or ghee, and cooked on a flat griddle. Naan is softer and less flaky. Both are carb-heavy but naan generally has less fat than roti canai if you get the plain version."},{"q":"Is naan a good choice when you're trying to lose weight?","a":"Plain naan is not terrible at 270 calories, but it's mostly refined carbs with not much fibre or protein to keep you full. The real problem is portion control at the mamak, where it's easy to eat two pieces plus a heavy curry without realising the total is pushing 900 calories. If you're watching your intake, one plain piece with a high-protein side is the move."}],"related":["roti-canai","thosai","tandoori-chicken"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The 'where the calories come from' breakdown does not add up internally: 65g flour at 235 kcal + 15g yogurt/milk at 12 kcal + 8g oil/ghee at 70 kcal totals 317 kcal, not the headline 270 kcal. The component weights also sum to only 88g, not 90g. The individual line items appear inflated relative to the stated total."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Reconcile the calorie breakdown rows to sum to 270 kcal. Adjust the flour row down (65g refined flour is roughly 220-225 kcal, not 235) and/or reduce the oil/ghee figure (8g ghee is about 72 kcal, but if the total is 270 then oil in the dough is likely closer to 5-6g at 45-55 kcal). One clean fix: flour 65g = 220 kcal, yogurt/milk 15g = 12 kcal, oil in dough 6g = 53 kcal, rounding = 285 kcal -- or adjust gram weights so it lands at 270.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"chapati","name":"Chapati","category":"mamak","serving":"1 piece (mamak stall)","servingGrams":70,"calories":200,"protein":7,"carbs":36,"fat":4,"metaDescription":"One chapati at the mamak is around 200 calories, making it one of the smarter bread choices on the menu. Here's what you're actually eating.","summary":"Chapati is a wholemeal unleavened flatbread cooked on a griddle at the mamak, eaten with dhal or curry, and genuinely one of the lower-calorie bread options you can order.","answer":"One chapati at a mamak stall comes in at roughly 200 calories per piece (about 70g). That's for the standard version with a light brush of oil or ghee worked into the dough. By itself it's not heavy, but once you start pairing it with dhal or curry, the numbers climb fast, so keep that in mind.","realTalk":["Chapati is basically a simple wholemeal flatbread, flour and water, cooked dry on a tawa (flat griddle) with minimal oil. Unlike roti canai which gets folded with a good amount of ghee and flipped till flaky, chapati is more straightforward and lighter. At 200 calories a piece with 7g protein and only 4g fat, it's genuinely one of the better bread choices at the mamak. Most of those calories come from the wholemeal atta dough itself (170 cal), and a small amount from the light oil brushed on. To be honest, these numbers are a ballpark estimate because solid lab data for mamak-style chapati is thin. Different stalls use different amounts of oil, so treat 200 as a reasonable middle ground.","The thing that changes the maths fast is what you eat it with. Plain chapati with no oil is closer to 165 calories, which is actually quite decent. Add a serving of dhal (about 60g alongside) and you're at 260 calories total. Order it with meat curry and you're looking at around 300 calories. If you're cutting, skip the roti canai and order chapati with dhal instead. You save maybe 200-300 calories compared to a full roti canai order, and you still walk out full. Two pieces of chapati plus dhal is a solid mamak meal for under 600 calories lah."],"lighter":["Order it plain or ask for less oil. The dry version without oil brushed on drops to about 165 calories per piece.","Pair with dhal instead of meat curry. Dhal adds around 60 calories for a serving; gravy-heavy curries add 100 or more.","Stop at two pieces. It sounds obvious but at the mamak it's easy to keep tearing and dipping without counting.","Skip the teh tarik alongside if you're watching it. A sweetened teh tarik adds another 100-plus calories that sneak up on you.","Add a side of plain veggie sabzi if the stall has it. More volume, barely any extra calories."],"variants":[{"name":"Plain / dry (no oil)","calories":165,"note":"Dough only, no oil brushed on, drier texture, fewer calories"},{"name":"Standard mamak chapati (light oil)","calories":200,"note":"Typical mamak version, light oil or ghee worked into dough and brushed on"},{"name":"With dhal","calories":260,"note":"Add one serving of dhal (about 60g) alongside; the classic pairing"},{"name":"With curry (ayam or mutton)","calories":300,"note":"Eaten with a small bowl of meat curry; adds meaningful fat and protein from the gravy"}],"components":[{"name":"Wholemeal atta dough (cooked)","grams":65,"calories":170},{"name":"Oil or ghee (light brush, worked into dough)","grams":5,"calories":45}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Chapati?","a":"One piece of chapati at a mamak stall is around 200 calories (70g serving). The plain version without oil is closer to 165 calories, and adding dhal brings the total to about 260 calories."},{"q":"What exactly is chapati?","a":"Chapati is a wholemeal unleavened flatbread, made from atta flour (whole wheat), water, and a little oil or ghee. It's cooked on a flat griddle called a tawa. No yeast, no layering, no deep frying. That's why it ends up lighter than roti canai or naan."},{"q":"Is chapati a healthier choice than roti canai at the mamak?","a":"Calorie-wise, yes. A plain roti canai is typically 300-plus calories with significantly more fat from the ghee used in folding. Chapati at 200 calories with 7g protein and wholemeal flour is the smarter pick if you're counting, especially when you factor in the higher fibre from the wholemeal atta."}],"related":["roti-canai","naan","thosai"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The \"where calories come from\" breakdown sums to 215 kcal (170 + 45) while the headline is 200 kcal. Minor internal inconsistency, likely a rounding/composition issue. Not a wrong number, just slightly off internally.","factualConcerns":[],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Adjust the calorie breakdown so the two rows sum to 200 kcal. Easiest fix: set the atta dough row to 155 kcal (65g cooked dough at ~2.4 kcal/g) and the oil/ghee row to 45 kcal, giving 200 total. Alternatively reduce the oil portion to 4g / 36 kcal to get 170 + 36 = 206, then round dough down to 164 kcal for a clean 200.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"poori","name":"Poori (Puri)","category":"mamak","serving":"2 pieces with aloo curry and dhal","servingGrams":284,"calories":460,"protein":14,"carbs":67,"fat":15,"metaDescription":"Poori with aloo and dhal at a mamak comes in at around 460 calories for 2 pieces. Not bad, but the deep frying adds up fast. Here is what you need to know.","summary":"Two puffed, deep-fried wheat breads served with potato masala curry and dhal, a classic mamak breakfast order that hits around 460 calories for the full set.","answer":"The standard poori set at a mamak, 2 pieces with aloo curry and dhal, comes to about 460 calories across 284g of food. The bread alone accounts for 252 of those calories because deep frying absorbs a fair bit of oil into each piece as it puffs up. Add the potato curry at 133 calories and the dhal at 75 calories and you are at a pretty solid breakfast total.","realTalk":["Poori is that round puffed bread you see at Indian and mamak stalls, usually served at breakfast with a scoop of aloo masala (spiced potato curry) and a small bowl of dhal. The full set is about 460 calories, which is a reasonable breakfast on its own. The calorie driver is the deep frying: each piece of dough gets dropped into hot oil and puffs up, absorbing oil in the process. The 84g of bread alone is 252 calories, so the gravy sides are actually the lighter part of the meal. Fair warning though, these numbers are a genuine ballpark estimate because detailed lab data for mamak poori is basically nonexistent. Different stalls fry at different temperatures and oil types, so the real number could swing 50 calories either way.","What changes the maths mostly is how many pieces you eat and which gravy you order. Sticking to dhal only (no aloo) drops you to around 325 calories. Swapping in channa (chickpea) masala instead pushes you closer to 510 calories because channa is denser. The masala poori variant, where spices are mixed into the dough, sits at about 490 calories because spiced dough tends to absorb slightly more oil. If you are watching intake, two pieces with dhal only is actually a fairly reasonable mamak breakfast, bro. Where people get into trouble is ordering a third piece or double-scooping the aloo without thinking about it."],"lighter":["Order with dhal only instead of the full aloo and dhal set, saves you roughly 135 calories.","Stick to exactly 2 pieces and do not order extras. Each additional poori adds about 125 calories.","Ask the uncle to drain the bread on a paper towel for a few seconds if possible, not always an option but worth asking.","Eat slowly and drink water alongside it. The dhal fills you up more than you expect, so you are less tempted to reorder.","Pair it with plain teh kosong or water instead of teh tarik, saving another 70 to 100 calories on the drink alone."],"variants":[{"name":"Poori only (2 pieces, no curry)","calories":250,"note":"Just the bread, nothing to dip into; uncommon but possible as a snack order"},{"name":"Poori with dhal only","calories":325,"note":"Lighter option without the oilier potato curry"},{"name":"Poori with aloo and dhal (standard)","calories":460,"note":"Typical mamak full set: 2 pieces puri, aloo masala, dhal on side"},{"name":"Masala poori (spiced dough) with aloo and dhal","calories":490,"note":"Spiced dough adds marginal extra oil absorption; heavier variant"},{"name":"Poori with channa (chickpea) curry","calories":510,"note":"Channa masala is denser and higher-calorie than aloo curry"}],"components":[{"name":"Poori bread, 2 pieces deep-fried (84g)","grams":84,"calories":252},{"name":"Aloo (potato masala) curry (120g)","grams":120,"calories":133},{"name":"Dhal curry (80g)","grams":80,"calories":75}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Poori (Puri)?","a":"A standard mamak poori set with 2 pieces of bread, aloo masala curry and dhal is around 460 calories in total, across about 284g of food. The bread alone is roughly 252 calories due to deep frying."},{"q":"What exactly is poori and how is it different from roti canai?","a":"Poori is a small round wheat bread that is deep fried until it puffs up like a balloon. Roti canai is a flat layered bread cooked on a griddle with very little oil. Both are mamak staples but poori is denser in calories per gram because of the frying oil absorbed during cooking."},{"q":"Is poori a good option if I am trying to lose weight?","a":"It is not the lightest breakfast but it is not a disaster either. At 460 calories for the full set, it is manageable if you eat it once in a while and pair it with dhal over aloo to save about 60 calories. The problem is usually ordering three or four pieces without tracking it. Two pieces with dhal only at around 325 calories is actually a reasonable mamak breakfast."}],"related":["roti-canai","thosai","nasi-kandar"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["FAQ 3 says choosing dhal over aloo 'saves about 60 calories', but the breakdown table shows dhal-only is 325 kcal vs 460 kcal for the full set, a difference of 135 calories. The 'How to eat it lighter' tip on the same page correctly states ~135 calories saved, making the FAQ figure inconsistent and roughly half the real number."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In FAQ 3, change \"save about 60 calories\" to \"save about 135 calories\" to match the breakdown table and the tips section on the same page.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"samosa","name":"Samosa","category":"snacks","serving":"1 piece","servingGrams":70,"calories":210,"protein":4,"carbs":24,"fat":11,"metaDescription":"One samosa is about 210 calories, and honestly most of that is the fried pastry shell and the oil it soaked up. Here's what you're actually eating.","summary":"A deep-fried triangular pastry with spiced potato and pea filling, a mamak staple that looks small but packs a solid calorie punch thanks to the oil-heavy shell.","answer":"One samosa (about 70g) comes in at around 210 calories, with 4g protein, 24g carbs, and 11g fat. That's for the standard vegetable version with potato and pea filling. The chicken version sits a bit higher at roughly 220 calories. These are ballpark estimates, not lab numbers, so take them as a good guide rather than gospel.","realTalk":["Samosa is one of those snacks that feels innocent because it's small. It's a triangle of fried dough stuffed with spiced potato and peas, super common at mamak stalls and pasar malam. You grab one for RM1.50 and think, okay small snack, cannot be that bad. But that little triangle is 210 calories, and here's why: the maida pastry shell contributes 90 calories and the absorbed frying oil adds another 80 on top of that. The actual potato filling? Only 55 calories. So you're basically paying the calorie price for pastry and oil, not the vegetables inside. The filling is almost the healthy part. Worth knowing, not worth stressing over, but worth knowing.","The honest maths problem with samosas is how fast they stack up. One piece at 210 calories is fine. But nobody eats just one, kan? Two pieces is 420 calories before you've even looked at your teh tarik. Chicken samosa nudges it to around 220 calories per piece, roughly the same range. The oil absorption is the big variable here since freshly fried samosas that have been sitting in the oil longer will soak up more than a well-drained one. If you're ordering at a stall, try to grab one that came out recently and has been left to drain a bit. There's no meaningful way to make samosa low-calorie because the deep-frying is the whole point, but knowing it's 210 a piece means you can budget for it instead of being surprised later. Also worth flagging: all the numbers here are estimates. Hard data on Malaysian mamak snacks is thin, so treat these figures as a solid ballpark rather than exact science."],"lighter":["Eat one, not three. Sounds obvious but two samosas plus teh tarik is already a 600-calorie snack session before you've had lunch.","Pair it with something that has actual protein and volume, like a bowl of clear soup or a plate of ulam, so you're not hungry again in 45 minutes.","If you're at a stall with options, pick the freshly fried batch and let it drain on the rack for a minute. Samosas that have been sitting in oil absorb noticeably more.","Chicken samosa at 220 calories gives you slightly more protein than the vegetable version, which makes it marginally better value calorie-for-calorie if you can find a good one.","Skip the extra chutney dips. Green chutney is fine, but the sweet tamarind chutney adds sugar calories fast and you barely notice it."],"variants":[{"name":"Vegetable samosa (potato and peas)","calories":210,"note":"Classic filling; starchy and filling, most common at mamak and hawker stalls"},{"name":"Chicken samosa","calories":220,"note":"Spiced minced chicken filling; slightly higher protein, similar calorie range to veggie version"}],"components":[{"name":"Maida pastry shell (fried)","grams":22,"calories":90},{"name":"Spiced potato and pea filling","grams":38,"calories":55},{"name":"Absorbed frying oil","grams":9,"calories":80}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Samosa?","a":"One samosa (70g) is about 210 calories. That breaks down to roughly 90 calories from the fried pastry shell, 55 from the spiced potato and pea filling, and 80 from absorbed frying oil. Chicken samosa is slightly higher at around 220 calories."},{"q":"What exactly is a samosa?","a":"It's a deep-fried triangular pastry, super common at mamak stalls across Malaysia. The outside is a thin, crispy shell made from maida (white flour), and the inside is usually spiced potato with peas. Chicken samosa uses spiced minced chicken instead. Served hot, usually with chutney on the side."},{"q":"Is samosa a healthy snack?","a":"Not really, but not a disaster either. The problem is that most of the calories come from the oil-fried pastry, not the filling. One piece fits into a normal day fine. The issue is portion control, because they're small, cheap, and easy to eat three before you notice."}],"related":["karipap","pisang-goreng","vadai"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The headline says 210 kcal but the three breakdown rows sum to 225 kcal (90 shell + 55 filling + 80 oil). The overall 210 figure is plausible for a 70g mamak samosa, but the internal components don't add up to match it. Minor rounding inconsistency.","factualConcerns":[],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Adjust the calorie breakdown rows so they sum to 210: e.g. change the absorbed frying oil line from 80 kcal to 65 kcal (keeping shell at 90 and filling at 55), or round the headline up to 225 kcal. Either fix removes the internal inconsistency.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"pakora","name":"Pakora","category":"snacks","serving":"5 pieces (typical mamak/Indian restaurant snack order)","servingGrams":150,"calories":330,"protein":8,"carbs":35,"fat":18,"metaDescription":"5 pieces of pakora is around 330 calories. The chickpea batter and frying oil do most of the damage. Here is what you are actually eating.","summary":"Pakora are deep-fried fritters coated in spiced chickpea batter, stuffed with vegetables or other fillings, and a classic snack at mamak stalls and Indian restaurants across Malaysia.","answer":"Five pieces of pakora comes in at around 330 calories, with 8g protein, 35g carbs, and 18g fat. That is for a 150g serve, which is the typical snack order you get at a mamak or Indian restaurant. The chickpea batter coating alone accounts for 180 of those calories, and the absorbed frying oil adds another 135. The actual vegetables inside? Only 55 calories. So yeah, the filling is basically not the problem.","realTalk":["Pakora are those crispy, golden fritters you see piled up at mamak counters and Indian eateries, basically vegetables dipped in thick spiced chickpea flour batter and dunked in a deep fryer. If you have had onion bhaji before, same idea. The calorie hit is real: 330 calories for 5 pieces, and almost 55% of those calories come from fat. The batter soaks up a serious amount of oil during frying, and that 15g of absorbed oil at 135 calories is doing most of the heavy lifting. To be upfront about it, these numbers are a ballpark estimate because solid nutrition data for Malaysian mamak-style pakora is thin. The exact calories depend a lot on batter thickness, frying time, and oil temperature, so treat this as a reasonable range rather than gospel.","What actually moves the numbers is the filling and how thick the batter is. Potato pakora (aloo) comes in higher at around 370 calories for the same 5 pieces because potato is denser than mixed veg. Paneer pakora jumps to 420 calories because paneer is high in fat. Chicken pakora is 390 calories but gives you more protein, which makes it the more sensible choice if you are going to eat fried things anyway. If you are ordering these at a mamak, portion is the main lever. Five pieces is one reasonable snack. Ten pieces while watching football is a different story, and that is 660 calories of fritters before the teh tarik arrives."],"lighter":["Go for chicken pakora instead of paneer pakora. You save about 30 calories per 5 pieces and get more protein out of it.","Ask for fewer pieces and pair with a plain lassi or just water. The snack itself is fine in small quantities; the problem is ordering a second round.","Blot the pakora on a paper towel if you can. Even removing a little surface oil shaves off some calories without touching the taste.","Skip the chutney if it is a creamy or sweet one. Mint chutney is low calorie and actually the better option for dipping.","If you are eating this before a main meal, stop at 3 pieces. It is a snack, not a starter course for more fried things."],"variants":[{"name":"Vegetable pakora (mixed veg, 5 pcs)","calories":330,"note":"Standard mixed veg with onion, potato, spinach in chickpea batter"},{"name":"Onion bhaji / onion pakora (5 pcs)","calories":350,"note":"Higher carbs from onion; similar oil absorption"},{"name":"Aloo pakora / potato pakora (5 pcs)","calories":370,"note":"Potato is denser and higher calorie than mixed veg"},{"name":"Paneer pakora (5 pcs)","calories":420,"note":"Paneer adds significant fat and protein; richest variant"},{"name":"Chicken pakora (5 pcs)","calories":390,"note":"Higher protein, similar fat from frying; popular at mamak"}],"components":[{"name":"Chickpea flour (besan) batter coating","grams":50,"calories":180},{"name":"Mixed vegetables (onion, potato, cauliflower)","grams":85,"calories":55},{"name":"Absorbed frying oil","grams":15,"calories":135}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Pakora?","a":"A standard order of 5 pieces of pakora is around 330 calories (150g). The chickpea batter coating contributes 180 calories, absorbed frying oil adds 135, and the vegetables inside are only 55 calories. Variants like paneer pakora can go up to 420 calories for the same serving size."},{"q":"What exactly is pakora?","a":"Pakora are deep-fried fritters popular at Malaysian mamak stalls and Indian restaurants. Vegetables like onion, potato, or cauliflower are coated in a thick spiced chickpea flour batter and fried until golden and crispy. Variants with paneer or chicken also exist. You might know them by another name if you have had onion bhaji at an Indian restaurant."},{"q":"Is pakora healthy or should I avoid it?","a":"It is deep fried, so it is not a low-calorie snack. But 330 calories for 5 pieces is not catastrophic if you keep the portion there and do not order two plates. The issue is the oil absorption from frying. Chicken pakora is the better pick if you want more protein for similar calories. Like any fried snack, moderation is the actual answer, not avoidance."}],"related":["roti-canai","vadai","samosa"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The calorie breakdown components listed in the 'Where the calories come from' section (180 kcal batter + 55 kcal vegetables + 135 kcal absorbed oil) sum to 370 kcal, but the headline and answer box state 330 kcal. The 40 kcal discrepancy is visible in both the prose and the breakdown table and could confuse a careful reader."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Adjust the component breakdown so the three rows sum to 330. Simplest fix: reduce the batter line to 140 kcal (or absorbed oil to 95 kcal) and update the matching prose in the answer box to reflect the corrected split.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"lassi","name":"Lassi","category":"drinks","serving":"1 glass (mango lassi)","servingGrams":300,"calories":260,"protein":8,"carbs":42,"fat":6,"metaDescription":"Mango lassi has about 260 calories per 300ml glass. Salted lassi is only 110. The sugar and mango puree are doing most of the damage, not the yogurt.","summary":"Lassi is a blended yogurt drink from South Asian cuisine, sold at mamak and Indian restaurants across Malaysia in sweet mango, rose, or simple salted versions.","answer":"A standard mango lassi (300ml glass) comes in at around 260 calories. That is not a small number for a drink bro, and most of it is coming from the sugar and mango puree, not the yogurt itself. Salted lassi (namkeen) is a completely different story at just 110 calories. These are ballpark estimates since detailed lab data for the version served at your local mamak is basically nonexistent, so treat the numbers as honest guesses, not gospel.","realTalk":["Lassi is a blended yogurt drink, usually full-fat yogurt thinned out with milk or water and then sweetened. You see it a lot at mamak stalls and Indian restaurants, sitting next to the teh tarik and Milo ais. The mango version is the crowd favourite, and at 260 calories per 300ml glass, it is more like a light dessert than a drink. The yogurt brings some protein (8g), which is actually decent, but the 18g of sugar and the mango puree push the carbs up to 42g. To be upfront, these numbers are estimates based on standard recipes because there is no solid lab data for the specific version your uncle's mamak is blending up. The actual calories could be 20 to 30 calories higher or lower depending on how heavy-handed they are with the sugar.","The biggest variable here is sugar. Some places dump in two tablespoons of sugar per glass without blinking. That alone can add another 60 to 90 calories. The size matters a lot too: a generous restaurant pour at 400ml bumps you to 345 calories without changing anything else. If you want to keep it lighter, ask for the salted lassi (namkeen) instead, which is just yogurt, water, and salt at around 110 calories. Or go for sweet lassi without fruit at 175 calories, which is decent if you want that yogurt flavour without committing to a full mango lassi. The main lever is always the sugar, so asking for less sweet is worth doing lah."],"lighter":["Order salted lassi (namkeen) instead of mango, you drop from 260 to 110 calories in one swap.","Ask them to make it less sweet, cutting even half the sugar saves around 35 calories.","Go for the smaller serve if you can, since a 300ml glass versus a 400ml restaurant pour is an 85-calorie difference.","Treat it as dessert, not a thirst-quencher, so you are not drinking one alongside a full meal.","If you are making it at home, use low-fat yogurt and skip the milk entirely to bring it closer to 180 to 200 calories."],"variants":[{"name":"Salted lassi (namkeen)","calories":110,"note":"Yogurt blended with water and salt only, no sugar or fruit; very low calorie"},{"name":"Sweet lassi (no fruit)","calories":175,"note":"Yogurt, sugar, water, and a pinch of cardamom; lower than mango version"},{"name":"Mango lassi (standard)","calories":260,"note":"Full-fat yogurt, mango puree, sugar, milk; the default at mamak and Indian restaurants"},{"name":"Rose lassi","calories":280,"note":"Yogurt, milk, sugar, rose syrup; similar to mango lassi but slightly sweeter from rose cordial"},{"name":"Mango lassi (large restaurant, 400ml)","calories":345,"note":"Same ingredients at a generous restaurant pour; common at sit-down Indian restaurants"}],"components":[{"name":"Full-fat yogurt (150g)","grams":150,"calories":102},{"name":"Mango puree (80g)","grams":80,"calories":50},{"name":"Sugar (18g, ~1.5 tsp)","grams":18,"calories":70},{"name":"Whole milk (50ml)","grams":50,"calories":33}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Lassi?","a":"A standard mango lassi (300ml glass) has around 260 calories. Salted lassi is much lower at about 110 calories, and a sweet lassi without fruit sits at around 175 calories."},{"q":"What exactly is lassi?","a":"Lassi is a yogurt-based drink blended smooth and served chilled. The sweet mango version is the most common in Malaysia, but the traditional salted version (namkeen lassi) is still popular at proper Indian restaurants. It is tangy, filling, and a lot more caloric than it looks when it is loaded with sugar."},{"q":"Is lassi a good choice when dieting?","a":"The salted version is genuinely decent at 110 calories and gives you some protein from the yogurt. Mango lassi at 260 calories is fine occasionally but it is basically a liquid dessert, so do not drink it every day thinking it is healthy just because it has yogurt in it."}],"related":["teh-tarik","milo-ais","teh-halia"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["In the ingredient breakdown, sugar is listed as '18g, ~1.5 tsp' but 18g of sugar is approximately 4 to 4.5 teaspoons, not 1.5. One teaspoon of granulated sugar is roughly 4g. The calorie figure for sugar (70 kcal) is correct for 18g; only the teaspoon conversion is wrong."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Change \"Sugar (18g, ~1.5 tsp)\" to \"Sugar (18g, ~4.5 tsp)\" in the ingredient breakdown list (line 292).","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"lempeng","name":"Lempeng","category":"snacks","serving":"1 piece (medium, ~10cm diameter)","servingGrams":95,"calories":155,"protein":3,"carbs":22,"fat":6,"metaDescription":"Lempeng is a Malay coconut pancake with about 155 calories per piece. Here is what changes the count and how to keep it reasonable.","summary":"Lempeng is a traditional Malay griddle pancake made with flour and grated coconut, eaten plain with sugar or paired with sambal, and comes in a few tasty variants including banana and corn.","answer":"One medium lempeng (about 95g, roughly 10cm across) comes in at 155 calories. That is for the standard kelapa version cooked on a griddle with a bit of oil. Add sambal on the side and you are looking at around 175 calories. The banana version is actually lighter at about 130 calories since the pieces tend to be smaller and thinner.","realTalk":["Lempeng is basically a coconut pancake, bro. Flour, fresh grated coconut, a splash of water, and then fried on a griddle with oil. It is a kampung-style breakfast or afternoon snack that a lot of people outside the Peninsula or rural areas might not have had before. At 155 calories per piece it is actually pretty reasonable for a snack, and the grated coconut is doing most of the calorie work at 60 kcal out of the 155. Worth flagging though: these numbers are a ballpark estimate because there is basically no proper lab data on lempeng out there. Different grandmothers, different coconut ratios, different amounts of oil on the griddle. Treat it as a solid guide, not gospel.","What actually moves the number is the oil on the griddle and what you eat it with. A heavier-handed cook can easily push that 35 kcal of absorbed oil higher, especially if the pan is constantly re-greased. The berlauk version with rendang or curry chicken on top can jump to 220 calories or more because the lauk alone adds 40 to 70 kcal. If you are tracking, eat it plain or with just a sprinkle of sugar, skip the second piece lah, and go for the pisang version if you can find it since it saves you about 25 calories per piece."],"lighter":["Go for the pisang version when available. At around 130 calories it is the lightest of the lot, and the banana adds natural sweetness so you do not need to dump sugar on top.","Ask the person making it to go easy on the griddle oil. That 4g of absorbed oil accounts for 35 calories and is entirely in the cook's hands.","Eat it plain with just white sugar instead of sambal. You save about 20 calories per piece and it is honestly how a lot of people eat it anyway.","One piece is usually enough as a snack. It is 95g of food, not 30g, so it does fill you up more than you expect.","Pair it with a black coffee or teh-o kosong instead of teh tarik. The pancake itself is fine; it is the sweet drinks beside it that wreck your count."],"variants":[{"name":"Lempeng pisang (banana)","calories":130,"note":"Smaller, thinner crepe-style with mashed banana folded in; banana adds ~20 kcal but pieces are usually smaller and use less coconut"},{"name":"Lempeng kelapa plain (no sambal)","calories":155,"note":"Standard medium pancake with grated coconut, eaten with white sugar or kaya"},{"name":"Lempeng kelapa with sambal","calories":175,"note":"Add ~20 kcal for a tablespoon of sambal accompaniment"},{"name":"Lempeng jagung (corn)","calories":180,"note":"Corn kernels added to batter; slightly more carbs and a touch more sugar than plain kelapa"},{"name":"Lempeng berlauk (with filling/topping)","calories":220,"note":"Served with heavier lauk like rendang or curry chicken; accompaniment adds 40-70 kcal depending on portion"}],"components":[{"name":"All-purpose flour (batter base)","grams":22,"calories":80},{"name":"Fresh grated coconut","grams":17,"calories":60},{"name":"Cooking oil absorbed on griddle","grams":4,"calories":35}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Lempeng?","a":"One medium lempeng is about 155 calories. The banana version (lempeng pisang) is lighter at around 130 calories, while the corn version (lempeng jagung) is slightly higher at 180 calories. Add a heavy lauk topping and it can reach 220 calories."},{"q":"What exactly is lempeng?","a":"Lempeng is a traditional Malay pancake made from flour, fresh grated coconut, and a little water, cooked flat on a griddle with oil. It is a common kampung breakfast or teatime snack, usually eaten with white sugar, kaya, or sambal on the side."},{"q":"Is lempeng a healthy snack?","a":"It is decent for a snack at 155 calories per piece, not amazing but not terrible either. The main thing to watch is the oil used on the griddle and what you eat it with. Plain with minimal accompaniment it fits fine into a calorie-controlled day. Just do not eat three of them."}],"related":["apam-balik","kuih-ketayap","pisang-goreng"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The ingredient breakdown in the 'Where the calories come from' section sums to 175 kcal (flour 80 + coconut 60 + oil 35) but the headline and nutrition table show 155 kcal. The per-100g maths supports 155, so the breakdown figures are slightly overstated and internally inconsistent. Consider adjusting individual line items to sum to 155 (e.g. oil absorbed at 15 kcal instead of 35 kcal, or flour at 65 kcal)."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the 'Where the calories come from' section, adjust the line-item calories so they sum to 155 rather than 175. The simplest fix: drop oil absorbed from 35 kcal to 15 kcal (4g of absorbed oil at ~9 kcal/g is about 36 kcal, but absorption in a griddle-cooked pancake is realistically 1-2g, so 15 kcal is more defensible). Or reduce flour to 65 kcal and coconut to 50 kcal to better reflect the stated weights. Either way, the three rows must add up to the headline number of 155.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"kuih-putu","name":"Kuih Putu","category":"snacks","serving":"2 pieces","servingGrams":60,"calories":230,"protein":2,"carbs":42,"fat":5,"metaDescription":"Kuih Putu is about 230 calories for 2 pieces. Steamed rice flour, gula melaka centre, coconut outside. Here is what you are actually eating.","summary":"Kuih Putu is a traditional steamed kuih made from rice flour, filled with melted gula melaka palm sugar, and rolled in grated coconut, sold fresh off the steamer at pasar malam and morning markets across Malaysia.","answer":"Two pieces of Kuih Putu (about 60g) comes in at roughly 230 calories, with 42g carbs, 5g fat, and barely any protein to speak of. The gula melaka filling is doing the most work here, contributing around 77 calories on its own. This is a ballpark estimate, not a lab result, so treat it as a solid guide rather than gospel.","realTalk":["Kuih Putu is one of those snacks that feels innocent because it is small and steamed, not fried. If you have never had it, picture a soft cylinder or disc of steamed rice flour with a molten palm sugar centre and grated coconut coating the outside. 230 calories for two pieces is not bad for a kuih, but the catch is that nobody really stops at two. Grab four and you are already at 460 calories from something you barely noticed eating. The gula melaka is the main calorie driver at around 77 kcal per serving, and the coconut adds another 46. Fair to note: calorie data for traditional kuih is thin, so this is a genuine estimate based on the known components, not a precise lab figure.","What actually changes the count is how generous the vendor is with filling and coconut. A stall that loads up the gula melaka and piles on extra coconut can easily push a serving to 270 calories or more. Putu piring, the bigger disc-shaped version, runs around 260 calories because the mold holds more dough and filling. If you are eating this as a snack, two pieces is fine. The problem is buying a whole bag of six or eight at the pasar malam and finishing them on the drive home. That is 700 plus calories gone before dinner, and your brain did not even register it as a meal."],"lighter":["Stick to two pieces, not the whole packet. It sounds obvious but this is genuinely where most people go wrong with kuih.","Go for putu bambu over putu piring if you have a choice. The bamboo tube version is smaller and more consistent at around 230 calories versus 260 for the disc.","Skip the extra coconut topping if the vendor asks. That generous handful adds 20 to 40 calories and you honestly will not miss it.","Eat it as a standalone snack with plain water or teh o kosong, not paired with other kuih. It is easy to collect three or four different kuih at the market and suddenly you have had a full meal in finger food form.","Eat it fresh off the steamer when it is piping hot. You slow down naturally and actually taste it, instead of powering through a cold bag of them."],"variants":[{"name":"Putu piring (disc-shaped, larger)","calories":260,"note":"Larger disc mold holds slightly more dough and filling; heavier gula melaka centre"},{"name":"Putu bambu (bamboo tube, standard)","calories":230,"note":"Standard hawker version; compact cylindrical shape, consistent size"},{"name":"Extra coconut topping","calories":270,"note":"Generous extra grated coconut added on top adds roughly 20-40 kcal to any variant"}],"components":[{"name":"Rice flour (steamed dough shell)","grams":25,"calories":92},{"name":"Gula Melaka filling (palm sugar)","grams":20,"calories":77},{"name":"Grated coconut (topping/coating)","grams":13,"calories":46},{"name":"Salt and trace moisture loss","grams":2,"calories":15}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Kuih Putu?","a":"About 230 calories for 2 pieces (60g), which is the standard hawker serving. This covers the rice flour shell, gula melaka filling, and grated coconut coating. It is an estimate since lab data for traditional kuih is limited, but it is a solid ballpark."},{"q":"What exactly is Kuih Putu?","a":"It is a Malaysian steamed kuih made from rice flour dough, shaped into cylinders (putu bambu) or discs (putu piring), with a centre of gula melaka palm sugar that melts when steamed. The outside is coated in grated coconut. It is sold fresh and hot, typically at morning markets and pasar malam."},{"q":"Is Kuih Putu healthy?","a":"It is not a health food, but it is also not deep-fried, so it is better than a lot of snacks. The problem is it is almost entirely simple carbs and sugar with almost no protein. Two pieces is a reasonable treat. A whole bag of six is basically dessert pretending to be a snack."}],"related":["onde-onde","kuih-seri-muka","cendol"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The ingredient breakdown row labelled 'Salt and trace moisture loss (2g) — 15 kcal' is factually incorrect. Salt contributes zero calories, and moisture loss is not a calorie source. This appears to be a rounding catch-all row, but labelling it this way is misleading. It should either be removed (and the other rows adjusted to sum to 230) or relabelled honestly as a rounding remainder."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Remove the 'Salt and trace moisture loss — 15 kcal' row from the breakdown list. Adjust the rice flour row from 92 kcal to 107 kcal (or redistribute the 15 kcal across the other components) so the rows still sum to 230 kcal. Alternatively, relabel the row as 'Rounding / minor ingredients' with a note that it is not a real calorie-contributing ingredient.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"kuih-bom","name":"Kuih Bom","category":"snacks","serving":"1 piece","servingGrams":55,"calories":210,"protein":3,"carbs":30,"fat":9,"metaDescription":"Kuih Bom packs around 210 calories per piece. That sesame-coated deep-fried dough ball is tastier than it looks on paper, but the calories add up fast.","summary":"Kuih Bom is a deep-fried glutinous rice dough ball coated in sesame seeds and filled with sweet gula melaka grated coconut, a classic Malaysian kuih that is dangerously easy to eat more than one of.","answer":"One Kuih Bom is around 210 calories for a 55g piece. That comes from the glutinous rice dough mixed with coconut milk (110 cal), the sweet gula melaka coconut filling (65 cal), sesame seed coating (23 cal), and the frying oil the dough soaks up (42 cal). It is a small ball but it is not a light snack. Worth knowing before you grab three off the kuih tray.","realTalk":["Kuih Bom is that round, golden, sesame-coated ball you see at kuih stalls and pasar malam. The dough is glutinous rice flour mixed with coconut milk, deep fried until puffed up, with a soft sweet coconut filling inside. At 210 calories per piece, it is not catastrophic if you eat one. The problem is nobody eats one. The calories come from three places: carbs from the glutinous flour, fat from the coconut milk in the dough plus the frying oil absorbed, and sugar from the gula melaka filling. Fair warning: these numbers are a ballpark estimate because detailed lab data for kuih bom specifically is thin, so treat it as a reasonable guide rather than a precise count.","What changes the number most is how much oil the dough absorbs during frying. Freshly fried pieces that have been properly drained will be on the lower end. Ones sitting in a tray soaked in oil, or fried at too low a temperature, absorb more fat and could push past 230 calories easily. The filling matters too: the chocolate variant in modern kuih shops runs about 230 calories versus the classic coconut version at 210. The sweet potato dough version (keledek) drops to around 185 calories because less glutinous flour is used. Two pieces of the classic version is already 420 calories, which is a full meal's worth of calories for some people. One is fine as a snack. Two is a decision you should make consciously lah."],"lighter":["Stop at one. Seriously. One piece is a proper snack at 210 calories. Two pieces is 420 calories and at that point you could have had a real meal.","Look for the sweet potato dough version if you see it. At around 185 calories it saves you 25 calories per piece and the texture is actually pretty good.","Eat it fresh off the fryer rather than from a tray that has been sitting. Fresh ones have had less time to absorb excess oil.","Pair it with plain water or unsweetened tea, not teh tarik or sirap. Adding a 150-calorie drink to a 210-calorie kuih turns a snack into a 360-calorie side quest.","Skip the chocolate filling variant. The extra sugar and fat in the chocolate version pushes it to around 230 calories for the same size piece, which is not worth it when the coconut filling is honestly better anyway."],"variants":[{"name":"Sweet potato dough (keledek) version","calories":185,"note":"Dough made with mashed sweet potato replacing some glutinous flour; lower fat, slightly lower calories"},{"name":"Standard glutinous rice dough with grated coconut filling","calories":210,"note":"Classic version; sesame-coated, deep fried, filled with gula melaka and grated coconut"},{"name":"Chocolate filling variant","calories":230,"note":"Chocolate filling adds extra sugar and fat versus coconut filling; sold in some modern kuih shops"}],"components":[{"name":"Glutinous rice dough (flour + coconut milk)","grams":25,"calories":110},{"name":"Sweet grated coconut filling (gula melaka + coconut)","grams":18,"calories":65},{"name":"Sesame seed coating","grams":4,"calories":23},{"name":"Frying oil absorbed","grams":5,"calories":42}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Kuih Bom?","a":"One Kuih Bom is around 210 calories for a standard 55g piece. This covers the glutinous rice dough, sweet coconut filling, sesame coating, and absorbed frying oil."},{"q":"What exactly is Kuih Bom?","a":"It is a Malaysian deep-fried kuih made from glutinous rice flour dough mixed with coconut milk, rolled in sesame seeds, then fried until it puffs up. Inside is a sweet filling of grated coconut cooked with gula melaka. Some modern versions use chocolate filling instead. You will find it at kuih stalls, pasar malam, and traditional bakeries."},{"q":"Is Kuih Bom healthy?","a":"It is a deep-fried sweet snack, so no, it is not something you eat for nutrition. At 210 calories per piece with 9g of fat and 30g of carbs, it is a treat. One piece occasionally is fine. The issue is portion control because they are small and easy to eat several at a time without noticing."}],"related":["onde-onde","kuih-keria","pisang-goreng"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The headline says 210 kcal but the component breakdown in the answer box sums to 240 kcal (110 + 65 + 23 + 42). One of these numbers is wrong. Either the components need to add to 210, or the headline should read 240. Fix the breakdown so the parts sum to the stated total.","factualConcerns":["Component calorie breakdown sums to 240 kcal (110 dough + 65 filling + 23 sesame + 42 oil) but the stated total is 210 kcal. The arithmetic is off by 30 calories, creating an internal contradiction."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Adjust the component figures so they sum to 210. For example: glutinous rice dough 100 kcal, sweet coconut filling 60 kcal, sesame coating 20 kcal, frying oil 30 kcal = 210. Or, if 240 is the more accurate total, update the headline, answer box, table, FAQ, and all prose references from 210 to 240.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"kuih-siput","name":"Kuih Siput","category":"snacks","serving":"1 small handful (~6-8 pieces)","servingGrams":25,"calories":120,"protein":2,"carbs":13,"fat":6,"metaDescription":"Kuih siput has about 120 calories per small handful (25g). These crunchy fried Raya snacks are easy to demolish by the bowl, so let's talk numbers.","summary":"Kuih siput is a crunchy, savoury fried snack shaped like a snail shell, made from spiced flour dough and popular during Raya as a cookie you grab by the handful.","answer":"One small handful of kuih siput, about 6 to 8 pieces or 25g, comes in at roughly 120 calories, with 13g carbs, 6g fat, and 2g protein. The calories are mostly coming from the fried dough itself. It is not a heavy snack per piece, but that is the whole problem lah, because nobody stops at one handful.","realTalk":["Kuih siput is that classic Raya cookie you find in every rumah terbuka, sitting in a big toples next to the muruku and the cornflake cookies. The name means snail in Malay because of the coiled shape. It is deep fried spiced dough, so the calorie drivers are pretty straightforward: flour base giving you the carbs, and absorbed frying oil pushing the fat up. At 120 calories for 25g, it sounds manageable until you realise a proper makan session at a Raya open house means you are mindlessly grabbing these while talking and laughing. Easy to put away 100 to 150g without noticing, which is 480 to 720 calories from snacks alone. Fair warning: these numbers are a ballpark estimate because there is not much hard data on kuih siput specifically, and every recipe is a bit different in how much butter and oil goes in.","What actually changes the count is the recipe and how long they are left to drain. More butter in the dough means more calories before it even hits the oil. Poorly drained pieces can carry noticeably more absorbed fat, easily pushing a 25g serving from 120 to 140 or 150 calories. The spice blend itself, usually curry powder or cumin and sesame, adds almost nothing calorie-wise. If you are trying to manage your intake at a Raya gathering, portion it out upfront: put 6 to 8 pieces on your plate and then step away from the toples. Do not eat standing next to the container or you will lose count entirely."],"lighter":["Portion it out before you eat. Six to eight pieces on a plate and that is your serving. Do not snack straight from the jar.","Alternate kuih siput with zero or low calorie options at the table, like plain air and conversation, so you are not reaching out of boredom.","If you are making them at home, try baking instead of frying. You cut out the absorbed oil which is 36 calories of the 120 right there.","Drain fried kuih siput on paper towels properly before storing. Better draining means less residual oil in each piece.","Be honest about the context. If you are going to three open houses in a day, this is where the calories pile up, not from one bowl but from three."],"variants":[],"components":[{"name":"Wheat and tapioca flour (dough base)","grams":14,"calories":50},{"name":"Butter or margarine (in dough)","grams":3,"calories":22},{"name":"Coconut milk (in dough)","grams":3,"calories":8},{"name":"Frying oil absorbed","grams":4,"calories":36}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Kuih Siput?","a":"About 120 calories per small handful of 6 to 8 pieces, which is around 25g. That works out to roughly 480 calories per 100g."},{"q":"What exactly is kuih siput?","a":"It is a crunchy deep fried savoury snack made from spiced dough, shaped like a snail shell. It is especially popular during Raya as a festive cookie and you will find it in almost every Malaysian home during the celebration."},{"q":"Is kuih siput considered a healthy snack?","a":"Not really, bro. It is deep fried dough, so it is pretty calorie dense for its size. The main issue is that it is so easy to overeat. In small controlled portions it is fine as an occasional treat, but mindlessly snacking on it at open houses will add up fast."}],"related":["karipap","kuih-ros","pisang-goreng"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The calorie breakdown lists 'Wheat and tapioca flour' as the dough base. Tapioca flour is not a standard or universal ingredient in kuih siput. Most traditional recipes use wheat flour only, sometimes with a small amount of rice flour. Listing tapioca flour as a base ingredient may mislead readers about what they are eating."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Change \"Wheat and tapioca flour (dough base)\" to \"Wheat flour (dough base)\" in the calorie breakdown list. If a small amount of rice flour is used in the specific recipe being referenced, \"Wheat and rice flour\" would also be acceptable.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"amplang","name":"Amplang","category":"snacks","serving":"1 small handful (about 25g)","servingGrams":25,"calories":135,"protein":2,"carbs":14,"fat":8,"metaDescription":"Amplang is a Sabah fish cracker made from mackerel and sago flour. One handful (25g) is around 135 calories, and yes, half the damage is frying oil.","summary":"Amplang is a puffy, deep-fried fish cracker from Sabah and Sarawak, made with mackerel or shrimp mixed into a sago flour dough and fried until light and crunchy.","answer":"One small handful of amplang, about 25g, comes in at around 135 calories. That's 14g carbs, 8g fat, and 2g protein. Not a disaster for a snack, but the catch is that 25g disappears in about 30 seconds flat, and most people eat two or three handfuls without thinking twice.","realTalk":["Amplang is one of those Sabahan snacks that sounds innocent because it has fish in it, but bro, the fish is almost a side note. The real calorie driver here is the sago starch base and the frying oil absorbed during cooking. Out of 135 calories per 25g handful, roughly 65 of them are straight from oil. The fish content is only about 5g per serving, giving you a measly 15 calories of actual protein. If you see it being sold at Kota Kinabalu airport or a pasar malam stall, it's genuinely delicious and worth trying, especially the ikan tenggiri (Spanish mackerel) version. Just be honest with yourself about the portion. These numbers are a ballpark estimate by the way, because proper lab data for amplang is thin and most packages round off or differ by brand.","What actually moves the maths is how much you eat in one sitting. A small 25g handful is 135 calories but a typical 100g snacking session (which is easy to hit while watching TV) is around 540 calories, roughly the same as a full plate of nasi goreng. The udang (shrimp) variant runs slightly higher at about 140 calories per 25g. There is no real lower-calorie way to prepare amplang since the deep frying is baked into the product itself. Your best move is to portion it out before you start eating, put the bag away, and pair it with something filling like a cup of black coffee or tea rather than snacking from an open packet."],"lighter":["Pour a measured 25g into a bowl and seal the bag immediately. You will eat less without thinking about it.","Buy the smaller travel packs instead of the big souvenir bags. Built-in portion control without any willpower needed.","Pair it with plain kopi-o or teh-o so you have something in your hand between crackers and slow down the pace.","If you want the fish flavour with less oil, otak-otak or ikan bakar gives you actual fish protein and far fewer calories per 100g.","Treat it as a once-in-a-while thing when you are actually at the pasar or airport, not a pantry staple to graze on daily."],"variants":[{"name":"Amplang Ikan (fish/mackerel)","calories":135,"note":"Standard version; made with ikan tenggiri (Spanish mackerel), slightly firmer texture"},{"name":"Amplang Udang (shrimp)","calories":140,"note":"Shrimp variant; similar macro profile, marginally higher sodium; Peladang udang label shows same 560 kcal/100g as ikan version"}],"components":[{"name":"Tapioca/sago starch (puffed cracker base)","grams":12,"calories":50},{"name":"Frying oil absorbed (deep-fried)","grams":7,"calories":65},{"name":"Fish fillet (mackerel or shrimp)","grams":5,"calories":15},{"name":"Seasonings (sugar, salt, garlic, MSG)","grams":1,"calories":5}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Amplang?","a":"One small handful of amplang, around 25g, is approximately 135 calories. Scale that up and 100g is about 540 calories, most of it coming from the sago starch base and absorbed frying oil."},{"q":"What exactly is amplang?","a":"Amplang is a deep-fried fish cracker from Sabah and Sarawak in East Malaysia. It is made by mixing ground fish (usually ikan tenggiri, which is Spanish mackerel, or shrimp) with sago or tapioca flour and seasonings, then deep frying until puffy and crunchy. You will commonly find it sold at Sabah airports and pasar malam stalls as a regional snack or souvenir."},{"q":"Is amplang a healthy snack?","a":"It is not a terrible snack in small amounts but it is not a healthy one either. The fish content per serving is low, around 5g, so the protein benefit is minimal. Most of the calories come from starch and frying oil. For the same calorie spend you could eat a hard-boiled egg and feel fuller for longer."}],"related":["keropok-lekor","pisang-goreng","keropok-keping"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The page repeatedly describes amplang as being from both 'Sabah and Sarawak'. Amplang is primarily a Sabah specialty (Kota Belud origin, strongly associated with Sabah souvenirs and KK airport). It is not a recognised Sarawak product in the same way. The Sarawak attribution is an overclaim and could mislead readers."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Remove 'and Sarawak' from the hero summary, FAQ schema answer for 'What exactly is amplang?', and the FAQ visible body text. Change to 'Amplang is a deep-fried fish cracker from Sabah, East Malaysia' throughout.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"kuih-kapit","name":"Kuih Kapit (Love Letters)","category":"snacks","serving":"3 pieces","servingGrams":25,"calories":120,"protein":2,"carbs":18,"fat":4,"metaDescription":"Kuih Kapit (Love Letters) has about 120 calories per 3 pieces (25g). The danger is nobody stops at 3. Here is the full calorie breakdown.","summary":"Kuih Kapit is a thin, crispy folded wafer made from egg, coconut milk and sugar, pressed in a hot iron mould and eaten by the stack during Chinese New Year and beyond.","answer":"3 pieces of Kuih Kapit (about 25g) comes in at roughly 120 calories, with 18g carbs, 4g fat and 2g protein. Fair warning though, these numbers are a ballpark estimate since detailed lab data for this snack is pretty thin. What is not a ballpark is how fast you go through half a tin without realising it.","realTalk":["Kuih Kapit, also called Love Letters or kuih sepit, is that thin crispy folded wafer your aunty makes in bulk for Chinese New Year using a special hinged iron mould pressed over a flame. The batter is simple: flour, egg, coconut milk and sugar. Each piece is light, maybe 8g, and at 40 calories a wafer it sounds harmless. The problem is nobody eats just 3. You sit in front of the tin while watching TV and suddenly you have eaten 15 pieces, which is 600 calories of snack you barely registered. The calories come mainly from the carbs (flour and sugar) plus the fat from coconut milk. It is genuinely delicious and there is nothing wrong with it, just know what you are working with.","The maths change fast with quantity. Stick to 3 pieces (25g, 120 calories) as an actual conscious serving rather than a background snack and it is totally fine as a treat. The sesame seed variant nudges up to about 135 calories for the same 3 pieces because of the added fat from the seeds. If you are making these at home, the coconut milk is the biggest lever: swapping thick coconut milk for a lighter version can shave 10 to 15 calories per serving without killing the texture. The honest answer on all these numbers though is treat them as a reasonable estimate, not gospel, because hard nutritional data for traditional kuih like this is scarce and recipes vary quite a bit between households and commercial makers."],"lighter":["Portion it out before you sit down. Put 3 to 5 pieces in a small bowl and put the tin away. Eating straight from the tin is how you accidentally finish 300 calories.","If you are making it at home, use a slightly thinner batter and less sugar. You can cut sugar by 20% and most people cannot tell the difference once the wafer is crispy.","Pair it with unsweetened tea or plain coffee so you are not doubling up on sugar from the drink side.","Go for plain original over pandan or sesame variants. The difference is small but plain keeps you at 120 calories per 3 pieces instead of the 135 calories for sesame.","Treat it as a proper dessert moment, not background snacking. Eat it slowly, actually taste it. You will eat fewer pieces and enjoy it more."],"variants":[{"name":"Plain (original)","calories":120,"note":"Standard egg, coconut milk, sugar batter; 3 pieces ~25g"},{"name":"Pandan flavoured","calories":120,"note":"Same base batter with pandan extract; negligible calorie difference"},{"name":"Sesame seed variant","calories":135,"note":"Added sesame seeds on surface add a small amount of fat and calories"}],"components":[{"name":"Rice flour and wheat flour (batter base)","grams":10,"calories":38},{"name":"Sugar","grams":7,"calories":27},{"name":"Coconut milk (thick)","grams":5,"calories":33},{"name":"Egg","grams":3,"calories":22}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Kuih Kapit (Love Letters)?","a":"About 120 calories per 3 pieces (25g). That works out to roughly 40 calories per individual wafer. These are estimates as hard nutritional data for traditional kuih is limited, but this is a reasonable ballpark for the standard egg, coconut milk and sugar batter."},{"q":"What exactly is Kuih Kapit?","a":"Kuih Kapit is a thin, crispy wafer made by pressing a batter of flour, egg, coconut milk and sugar between a hot hinged iron mould. The result is a delicate folded or rolled wafer. It is a Chinese New Year staple in Malaysia and Singapore, though you can find it year-round in snack shops. Also known as kuih sepit or Love Letters."},{"q":"Is Kuih Kapit healthy?","a":"It is a sugary, starchy snack so it is not something you build meals around, but a few pieces as a treat is perfectly fine. The issue is it is very easy to mindlessly eat a lot of them because each piece feels so light. If you are tracking calories, count your pieces before you start eating, not after."}],"related":["kuih-bahulu","kuih-bangkit","kuih-ros"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The ingredient breakdown lists 'Rice flour and wheat flour' as the batter base, implying both are standard components. Traditional kuih kapit uses rice flour (and sometimes tapioca starch); wheat flour is not a typical ingredient and is absent from most household and commercial recipes. Listing wheat flour alongside rice flour as the default base is mildly misleading."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the ingredient breakdown row, change \"Rice flour and wheat flour (batter base)\" to \"Rice flour (batter base)\" to reflect the standard recipe. The calorie figure for that row (38 kcal for 10g of flour) does not need to change.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"umai","name":"Umai","category":"lauk","serving":"1 serving (hawker/restaurant portion)","servingGrams":160,"calories":200,"protein":25,"carbs":6,"fat":8,"metaDescription":"Umai is Sarawak's raw fish salad and one serving is only about 200 calories. High protein, almost no carbs. Seriously good diet food.","summary":"Umai is a Sarawak Melanau dish of thin-sliced raw fish tossed in lime juice, shallots, chili and salt, eaten cold and fresh.","answer":"One hawker serving of umai (about 160g) comes in at around 200 calories, with 25g protein and only 6g carbs. The bulk of those calories is from the raw fish itself, mostly ikan tenggiri (Spanish mackerel). The condiments, lime, shallots, chili, barely register. As raw fish dishes go, this is genuinely one of the leanest things you can order.","realTalk":["Umai is basically Sarawak's version of ceviche, bro. Melanau people have been eating raw fish this way for generations: thin slices of fresh tenggiri or terubok, dressed with calamansi, sliced shallots, chili, and salt. No cooking, no oil, no coconut milk. At 200 calories for a full serving, it is one of the rare hawker dishes where you can eat the whole plate and not regret it later. The 25g of protein is solid too, roughly the same as a medium chicken breast. Fair warning though, these numbers are a genuine ballpark estimate because there is almost no hard nutritional data published for umai specifically, so take the figures as a useful guide rather than gospel.","What actually moves the calorie count is the fish you pick. Tenggiri lands at about 200 calories a serving, which is your standard order. Swap to umai terubok (the shad-family fish, fattier and oilier) and you jump to around 260 calories for the same portion size. Go the other way with umai udang (prawns) and you drop all the way down to about 115 calories since prawns are much leaner than any fish. The condiments change almost nothing. One thing to watch: some stalls add a splash of chili oil or mix in a tiny bit of sugar to balance the acidity, which can quietly add 30 to 50 calories. Ask if you want to keep it clean."],"lighter":["Order umai udang instead of the fish version and you cut down to about 115 calories, same dish, same experience.","Eat it as a main, not a side, since at 200 calories and 25g protein it holds up fine on its own without needing rice.","Skip any version that comes with a coconut milk dressing or sweet sauce on the side, those add calories fast.","Ask the stall to go easy on any added chili oil or sugar syrup if they use it to balance the lime.","Choose tenggiri over terubok if you want the leaner fish option, terubok is the fattier fish and runs about 60 calories higher per serving."],"variants":[{"name":"Umai udang (prawn)","calories":115,"note":"Prawns are much leaner than fish; ~130g raw shrimp plus same condiments"},{"name":"Umai ikan tenggiri (Spanish mackerel)","calories":200,"note":"Standard version; lean semi-fatty saltwater fish, most common hawker choice"},{"name":"Umai ikan terubok (toli shad/Chinese herring)","calories":260,"note":"Terubok is an oily shad-family fish, significantly fattier than tenggiri; higher calorie"}],"components":[{"name":"Raw ikan tenggiri (Spanish mackerel), ~130g","grams":130,"calories":181},{"name":"Sliced shallots, ~30g","grams":30,"calories":12},{"name":"Red chili, ~15g","grams":15,"calories":6},{"name":"Calamansi/lime juice, ~20ml","grams":20,"calories":5}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Umai?","a":"A standard hawker serving of umai (about 160g) is around 200 calories with 25g protein and 6g carbs. Umai udang is much lower at about 115 calories, while umai terubok is higher at around 260 calories due to the fattier fish."},{"q":"What exactly is umai and where does it come from?","a":"Umai is a raw fish salad from Sarawak, traditionally made by the Melanau coastal community. Fresh fish is sliced thin and mixed with calamansi lime juice, sliced shallots, chili and salt. The acid from the lime cures the fish slightly, similar to ceviche. No cooking involved."},{"q":"Is umai actually healthy or is there a catch?","a":"Honestly, for a hawker dish it is as clean as it gets. High protein, low carbs, no added fat, no frying. The main thing to be aware of is that you are eating raw fish, so freshness matters a lot. At a legit stall that sources their fish daily, it is fine. The calorie count is also a ballpark estimate since data for this dish is limited."}],"related":["hinava","sarawak-laksa","ikan-bakar"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The variants table labels umai terubok as \"toli shad/Chinese herring\". Terubok (Tenualosa toli) is correctly called toli shad, but \"Chinese herring\" is not its standard English name and may confuse readers. Minor point."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the variants table Notes column for umai terubok, change \"toli shad/Chinese herring\" to just \"toli shad\" to avoid the inaccurate common-name label.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"midin","name":"Midin","category":"veg","serving":"1 plate (hawker/kopitiam, stir-fried belacan style)","servingGrams":160,"calories":140,"protein":5,"carbs":8,"fat":10,"metaDescription":"Midin stir-fried belacan style is about 140 calories a plate. This Sarawak jungle fern is one of the lightest veg dishes you can order at a kopitiam.","summary":"Midin is a wild jungle fern from Sarawak, stir-fried fast over high heat with belacan, garlic and chilli until just-tender and a little crunchy at the tips.","answer":"A plate of midin masak belacan at a Sarawak hawker or kopitiam comes in at about 140 calories for roughly 160g. The fern itself is barely 44 calories; the cooking oil is doing most of the work at 133 calories for 1.5 tbsp. Protein is modest at 5g, carbs 8g, fat 10g. As veg dishes go, this is genuinely one of the lighter things you can order.","realTalk":["Okay first, if you have never heard of midin, let me put you on to it. It is a wild jungle fern that grows in Sarawak, and it is only really available there and at certain specialty restaurants in KL. The hawker throws it in a screaming-hot wok with belacan, garlic, chilli and a splash of oil, and it comes out with this satisfying crunch at the curly tips. At around 140 calories a plate, it is legitimately a low-calorie dish and something you can eat a lot of without wrecking your deficit. Fair warning though: the numbers here are a ballpark estimate because hard nutritional data for midin is almost nonexistent. We are working from the components, not a lab test, so treat it as a rough guide.","What actually changes the maths is the oil. The cooking oil alone accounts for 133 of those 140 calories; the fern is basically free. A hawker who is heavy-handed with the oil could easily push you from 140 to 180 or 200 calories for the same plate. If you are eating masak lemak style with coconut milk, that version runs about 380 calories because the santan contributes an extra 250 to 300 kcal on its own. The garlic-only version (masak bawang putih) saves you a little, coming in around 120 calories. If you are ordering this at a warung, ask for less oil (kurang minyak) and stick to the belacan or garlic versions rather than the lemak."],"lighter":["Order masak bawang putih instead of belacan style to shave off about 20 calories, or ask for kurang minyak whichever version you get.","Avoid midin masak lemak unless you have the calories to spare, the coconut milk alone adds 250 to 300 kcal and takes the dish to around 380 calories.","Pair it with a plain protein like steamed fish or grilled chicken rather than a saucy lauk, so your overall meal stays light.","If you are cooking at home, 1 tbsp of oil instead of 1.5 tbsp saves you around 45 calories and the texture difference is barely noticeable.","Use it as a high-volume filler alongside your rice, the bulk helps you feel full without adding much to your calorie count."],"variants":[{"name":"Midin masak bawang putih (garlic only)","calories":120,"note":"Lighter on aromatics, slightly less oil than belacan version"},{"name":"Midin masak belacan (standard)","calories":140,"note":"Typical hawker version with shrimp paste, garlic, chilli"},{"name":"Midin masak lemak (with santan)","calories":380,"note":"Cooked in thick coconut milk; coconut milk contributes ~250-300 kcal per serving"}],"components":[{"name":"Midin fern (raw ~130g, cooked down)","grams":110,"calories":44},{"name":"Cooking oil (1.5 tbsp)","grams":15,"calories":133},{"name":"Belacan, garlic, chilli, shallots","grams":20,"calories":25},{"name":"Absorbed water / sauce","grams":15,"calories":0}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Midin?","a":"A standard plate of midin masak belacan from a Sarawak hawker or kopitiam is about 140 calories for 160g. The garlic-only version is slightly lighter at around 120 calories, and the masak lemak (coconut milk) version jumps to roughly 380 calories."},{"q":"What exactly is midin and where can I find it?","a":"Midin is a wild jungle fern native to Sarawak, Borneo. It is harvested from the rainforest and stir-fried with belacan, garlic or coconut milk. You will find it at almost every kopitiam and hawker centre in Kuching and Sibu, but it is hard to find outside Sarawak unless a restaurant specifically sources it."},{"q":"Is midin actually healthy or is it just another oily veg dish?","a":"The fern itself is very low calorie, around 44 calories for a cooked portion. It is the cooking oil that takes the total to 140 calories. Compare that to kangkung belacan which runs roughly similar numbers depending on oil. As long as it is not cooked masak lemak style, midin is a genuinely light and filling vegetable to add to your meal."}],"related":["kangkung-belacan","paku-pakis","sarawak-laksa"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The headline 140 kcal for 160g is plausible. However, the page's own breakdown lists 44 kcal (fern) + 133 kcal (oil) + 25 kcal (aromatics) = 202 kcal, yet the prose says \"The cooking oil alone accounts for 133 of those 140 calories; the fern is basically free.\" That is internally contradictory. Either the oil figure is overstated (1.5 tbsp at a hawker is unlikely; 1 tbsp / 10g is more realistic at ~90 kcal, which would make the total add up closer to 140-160 kcal), or the headline should be higher. The headline number itself (140 kcal) is believable for a stir-fried veg dish; the breakdown table is what breaks the math.","factualConcerns":["The calorie breakdown is internally inconsistent: the component list totals ~202 kcal (44 fern + 133 oil + 25 aromatics) but the headline and prose state 140 kcal. The prose then compounds this by saying the oil alone accounts for 133 of the 140 calories, which contradicts showing 44 kcal for the fern and 25 kcal for aromatics in the same breakdown. The oil figure of 133 kcal for 1.5 tbsp is also high for a typical hawker portion; ~1 tbsp (90 kcal) would make the totals consistent with the 140 kcal headline."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the breakdown table, reduce the oil line to \"1 tbsp (10g) / ~90 kcal\" and update the total to ~160 kcal (or adjust the headline to match the 202 kcal that the current components actually add up to). Then fix the prose line to say \"the oil contributes the bulk of these calories\" rather than claiming oil alone accounts for 133 of 140 kcal. Whichever direction is chosen, the breakdown, the headline, and the prose paragraph should all agree on one number.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"paku-pakis","name":"Paku Pakis","category":"veg","serving":"1 plate, stir-fried (goreng belacan / sambal)","servingGrams":170,"calories":180,"protein":9,"carbs":14,"fat":9,"metaDescription":"Paku pakis goreng belacan is about 180 calories per plate. One of the better veg orders you can make, honestly. Here is the full breakdown.","summary":"Pucuk paku fern shoots stir-fried with belacan or sambal, a classic veg dish across Sabah, Sarawak and the East Coast that punches way above its weight on flavour.","answer":"A standard plate of paku pakis goreng belacan comes in at around 180 calories for 170g. That gets you 9g protein, 14g carbs and 9g fat. Not bad at all for something that tastes that good with rice. The fern shoots themselves are barely 44 calories for 130g cooked, so the oil doing the stir-frying is where most of the calories actually come from.","realTalk":["Paku pakis is stir-fried fiddlehead fern shoots, called pucuk paku, and if you have never tried it, think of it like kangkung's wilder Bornean cousin. Slightly more tender, earthy, with that classic belacan smell that will make the whole table hungry. At 180 calories a plate it is genuinely one of the smarter veg dishes you can order at a warung. The fern shoots themselves only clock in at 44 calories for 130g, which is almost embarrassingly low. To be honest though, these numbers are a ballpark estimate because hard nutrition data for paku pakis is thin. Take them as a solid guide, not a lab result.","What actually moves the maths is the oil and whether santan gets involved. A hawker stall goreng will use roughly 1.5 tbsp of oil (14g, about 90 calories) and that alone accounts for half the plate total. Go kerabu style, blanched with lime and belacan and no frying, and the whole thing drops to around 90 calories. Order masak lemak and coconut milk pushes it up to 260 calories, and if there are prawns or ikan bilis in there too you are looking at 310 calories. Portion size matters too, warung plates vary, so if they pile it on generously add another 20 to 30 calories to whatever version you order."],"lighter":["Order it kerabu style if the stall offers it, blanched and tossed with lime and belacan, cuts the calories roughly in half to around 90 calories.","Skip the masak lemak version when you are watching your numbers, coconut milk adds 80 to 130 extra calories compared to the goreng belacan version.","Eat it with plain white rice rather than nasi goreng so you are not doubling up on oil at the same meal.","Ask the stall to go easy on the oil if you are ordering at a sit-down place, some will actually do it if you ask nicely.","Use it to bulk out your plate volume without adding many calories, paku pakis is genuinely filling for how low-cal it is."],"variants":[{"name":"Kerabu pucuk paku (salad, no cooking oil)","calories":90,"note":"Blanched ferns tossed with lime, belacan, chili and toasted coconut; minimal fat, very low calorie"},{"name":"Goreng belacan (stir-fried with shrimp paste)","calories":180,"note":"Standard hawker version; oil is the main calorie driver"},{"name":"Masak lemak / pucuk paku lemak (coconut milk gravy)","calories":260,"note":"Santan-based; fat climbs significantly with coconut milk; common in Negeri Sembilan and Sarawak"},{"name":"Masak lemak with udang / ikan bilis (protein added)","calories":310,"note":"Lemak version with prawns or anchovies adding protein and extra calories"}],"components":[{"name":"Pucuk paku (fern shoots, cooked)","grams":130,"calories":44},{"name":"Cooking oil (hawker stall, ~1.5 tbsp)","grams":14,"calories":90},{"name":"Belacan / sambal paste","grams":15,"calories":28},{"name":"Aromatics (garlic, shallots, chili)","grams":20,"calories":18}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Paku Pakis?","a":"A standard plate of paku pakis goreng belacan is around 180 calories for 170g. The kerabu salad version drops to about 90 calories, while masak lemak with santan climbs to 260 to 310 calories depending on what protein is added."},{"q":"What exactly is paku pakis?","a":"Paku pakis refers to fiddlehead fern shoots, called pucuk paku, that are stir-fried with belacan (shrimp paste), sambal or cooked in coconut milk gravy. It is a staple veg dish in Sabah, Sarawak and parts of the East Coast peninsula, and pretty much everyone who tries it becomes a fan."},{"q":"Is paku pakis a healthy choice when eating out?","a":"Honestly, yes, it is one of the better veg orders you can make. The fern shoots themselves are very low calorie and provide decent plant protein. The main thing to watch is whether it is fried in a lot of oil or cooked in santan, both of which push the calories up considerably. Goreng belacan style at 180 calories is a solid call."}],"related":["kangkung-belacan","midin","sayur-manis"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["9g protein per plate is on the high side for cooked fiddlehead ferns (typically 3-4g per 100g cooked), though belacan and possible ikan bilis additions make it less alarming than a hard error. Worth a second look but not clearly wrong given hawker variation."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Consider lowering the protein figure from 9g to 5-6g per plate, or adding a note that protein varies depending on whether ikan bilis or other protein is added to the stir-fry.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"butod","name":"Butod","category":"snacks","serving":"1 grub (grilled)","servingGrams":7,"calories":20,"protein":1,"carbs":0,"fat":1,"metaDescription":"Butod (sago grub) has about 20 calories per grilled grub. Sabah's most talked-about snack, raw or fried. Here's what you're actually eating.","summary":"Butod is the sago grub harvested from rotting sago palms in Sabah, eaten raw and wriggling as a delicacy or grilled and skewered at markets for the slightly less adventurous crowd.","answer":"One grilled butod grub comes in at around 20 calories for roughly 7g. Raw it's about 15 calories, deep-fried closer to 25. The calorie count is genuinely low per grub, but fair warning: these numbers are estimates because hard nutritional data on sago grubs is basically non-existent. The fat content is what drives most of the calories, not protein or carbs.","realTalk":["Butod is a Sabah thing. If you've never heard of it, it's a fat white grub that lives inside rotting sago palm trunks, and yes, people eat it alive. Locals, especially from Kadazan-Dusun and other indigenous communities, have eaten it for generations. Tourists try it on a dare. The grub has a coconut-y, creamy taste raw, and goes crispy and nutty when grilled or fried. Calorie-wise, 20 calories per grilled grub sounds almost too good to be true. And honestly, be straight with you, these are ballpark estimates because there's very little lab data on butod specifically. What we know is the macros are mostly fat with a bit of protein and basically zero carbs.","In practice, nobody sits down and eats one butod. At a market stall they come skewered in batches of 5 to 10. Ten grilled grubs puts you at around 200 calories, which is still pretty reasonable. Where it gets spicier is deep-fried, where each grub picks up a bit of oil and hits 25 calories each, so ten grubs is closer to 250 calories. The serving size is really what drives the maths here, not the cooking method. If you're eating it as a novelty snack at a Sabah night market, you're probably not having more than 5 to 10 grubs total, which is 100 to 200 calories. Honestly for a snack, that's not bad lah."],"lighter":["Stick to grilled over deep-fried. You save about 5 calories per grub, which adds up to 25 to 50 calories across a typical serving of 5 to 10.","Eat it raw if you can handle it. At 15 calories per grub it's the lowest calorie option and also how it's traditionally eaten.","Cap yourself at 5 grubs as a tasting experience rather than a full snack. That's only 100 calories grilled.","Skip any dipping sauces they might serve alongside. A sweet chili sauce can add 30 to 50 calories before you notice.","Don't add it on top of a full meal. Treat it as the snack it is, not a side dish to nasi goreng."],"variants":[{"name":"Raw (live)","calories":15,"note":"Eaten wriggling or fresh-chilled; full moisture retained, slightly lower effective calories per grub"},{"name":"Grilled / skewered","calories":20,"note":"Most common hawker preparation; crispy outside, juicy inside; fat renders slightly, moisture lost"},{"name":"Deep-fried","calories":25,"note":"Fried in oil until crispy; absorbs a small amount of cooking oil adding a few extra calories"}],"components":[{"name":"Sago grub body (protein)","grams":1,"calories":4},{"name":"Sago grub fat (lipid)","grams":1,"calories":9},{"name":"Residual moisture / trace carbs","grams":5,"calories":7}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Butod?","a":"One grilled butod grub is approximately 20 calories. Raw it's around 15 calories and deep-fried about 25 calories. These are estimates as formal nutritional data for sago grubs is limited."},{"q":"What exactly is butod and is it safe to eat?","a":"Butod is the larva of the Rhynchophorus ferrugineus beetle, harvested from sago palm trunks. It has been eaten in Sabah for generations and is perfectly safe. The raw version is eaten fresh and wriggling; grilled and fried versions are widely sold at Sabah markets and cultural festivals."},{"q":"Is butod actually a good protein source?","a":"Each grub only has about 1g of protein, so a serving of 10 grubs gives you maybe 10g of protein total. It's not a high-volume protein source by any stretch, but insects in general do have reasonably complete amino acid profiles. Think of it as a novelty snack, not a protein strategy."}],"related":["kelupis","amplang","kuih-penjaram"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The 'where the calories come from' breakdown (4 kcal protein + 9 kcal fat + 7 kcal residual moisture/trace carbs = 20 kcal) contradicts the nutrition table which shows 0g carbs. The 7 kcal mystery row is unexplained and makes the macro math inconsistent: 1g protein + 1g fat = ~13 kcal, not 20. Either the macro table should show more fat/protein, or the breakdown row should be removed."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Remove the \"Residual moisture / trace carbs\" row from the breakdown list (moisture has no calories), and adjust the fat entry to 1.7g fat = 15 kcal and protein to 1g = 4 kcal to reach ~19-20 kcal total. Alternatively, update the nutrition table to show 1g protein and 1.5g fat to better reflect the 20 kcal headline, and drop the 0-calorie moisture row entirely from the breakdown.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"abacus-seeds","name":"Abacus Seeds","category":"noodles","serving":"1 plate","servingGrams":320,"calories":520,"protein":22,"carbs":65,"fat":18,"metaDescription":"Abacus Seeds clock in at around 520 calories per plate. Here's what's actually in that chewy Hakka yam-dough dish and whether it fits your goals.","summary":"A Hakka stir-fried dish of chewy yam-tapioca dough beads tossed with minced pork, dried shrimp, mushroom and black fungus, popular in Hakka communities across Malaysia.","answer":"One plate of Abacus Seeds (around 320g) comes in at roughly 520 calories, with 22g protein, 65g carbs and 18g fat. The yam-tapioca dough beads carry most of the carb load, while the minced pork and cooking oil push the fat and protein up. Fair warning, these numbers are a ballpark estimate since hard nutritional data for this dish is basically nonexistent, but 500-plus calories for a full plate is a reasonable working figure.","realTalk":["Abacus Seeds (算盘子, suan pan zi) is a Hakka dish you'll find at Hakka restaurants and some heritage kopitiam spots across Malaysia, especially in KL, Bentong and parts of Sabah. If you've never had it, the dough beads are made from yam and tapioca starch, then hand-rolled into little round shapes that look like abacus beads, boiled and stir-fried with minced pork, dried shrimp, shiitake mushrooms and black fungus. The texture is QQ and slightly sticky, think a chewier version of mochi but savory. At 520 calories a plate it's not a diet food, but it's also not a disaster. The yam dough beads alone account for 290 of those calories, and the minced pork adds another 145. To be straight with you though, this is a medium-confidence estimate because proper lab-tested nutrition data for Abacus Seeds is basically nonexistent. Take the number as a useful guide, not gospel.","What actually moves the number is how heavy-handed the wok is with oil and how big the portion is. A generous cook who doubles the oil can easily add 80 to 100 extra calories without you noticing. Portion size is the other big lever: a smaller serve of 200g instead of 320g would drop you closer to 325 calories. The minced pork contributes real protein but also fat, so if you could swap in lean chicken mince you'd save maybe 30 to 40 calories. Ask for less oil if you're ordering at a restaurant, most places will accommodate. The dried shrimp and mushrooms are practically free at only 40 calories for 30g, so don't stress about those."],"lighter":["Ask for less oil at the wok. This dish traditionally uses a fair bit and even cutting it by half saves you around 40 to 60 calories.","Eat a smaller portion. 200g instead of 320g puts you around 325 calories and honestly still feels satisfying because the dough is dense and chewy.","Pair it with a clear soup or a plate of stir-fried sayur manis instead of ordering extra rice or another fried item.","Skip any extra sauce or gravy that some places pour over. That stuff adds calories fast and you can't easily account for it.","Share a plate between two people and bulk up with a simple vegetable dish on the side. You still get to enjoy the flavour without eating the full 520 calories yourself."],"variants":[],"components":[{"name":"Yam-tapioca dough beads (cooked)","grams":200,"calories":290},{"name":"Minced pork (stir-fried)","grams":60,"calories":145},{"name":"Dried shrimp and mushrooms","grams":30,"calories":40},{"name":"Cooking oil and aromatics","grams":30,"calories":45}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Abacus Seeds?","a":"One plate of Abacus Seeds (roughly 320g) is around 520 calories. That breaks down to about 22g protein, 65g carbs and 18g fat. This is a solid estimate but hard to pin down precisely since the dish doesn't have much published nutritional data."},{"q":"What exactly are Abacus Seeds?","a":"Abacus Seeds are a Hakka dish made from yam and tapioca starch dough that's hand-shaped into little bead-like rounds, boiled until chewy, then stir-fried with minced pork, dried shrimp, mushrooms and black fungus. They taste savory and slightly earthy from the yam, with a QQ chewy bite. It's not pork-free, so non-halal."},{"q":"Is Abacus Seeds healthy?","a":"It's a balanced enough meal with decent protein from the pork and reasonable carbs from the yam dough, but 520 calories a plate means it's not a light option. The bigger issue is the oil used in stir-frying, which you can't always control. If you're counting calories, treat it as a proper meal rather than a snack, and maybe skip the sugary drink that usually comes with it."}],"related":["hakka-mee","wat-tan-hor","char-kuey-teow"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The eyebrow category label reads \"Noodles\" but Abacus Seeds is not a noodle dish. It is a Hakka stir-fried yam-tapioca dough bead dish. Should be labelled something like \"Hakka\" or \"Rice & Noodles\" or a dedicated stir-fry category, not Noodles."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Change `<p class=\"food-hero__eyebrow\">Noodles</p>` to `<p class=\"food-hero__eyebrow\">Hakka</p>` (or whatever the correct category slug is for this dish in the database). Everything else is accurate.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"ayam-masak-arak","name":"Ayam Masak Arak (Rice Wine Chicken)","category":"lauk","serving":"1 serving (2-3 bone-in chicken pieces with wine sauce)","servingGrams":300,"calories":530,"protein":30,"carbs":6,"fat":35,"metaDescription":"Ayam Masak Arak (Rice Wine Chicken) has around 530 calories per serving. The sesame oil is what pushes the number up. Here's the full breakdown.","summary":"A Foochow-Sarawak classic from Sibu where chicken is braised in red glutinous rice wine and sesame oil, traditionally served during confinement but honestly too good to wait that long.","answer":"One serving of Ayam Masak Arak, which is 2 to 3 bone-in pieces with the wine sauce, comes in at around 530 calories, with 30g protein, 6g carbs, and 35g fat. The dish is lower carb than you'd expect but the fat is real because sesame oil alone contributes about 180 calories per serving. These are honest estimates since detailed nutritional data for this dish is sparse, so treat it as a ballpark rather than a precise lab result.","realTalk":["Ayam Masak Arak is a Foochow confinement dish from Sibu, Sarawak. If you're not from that community, you may have only seen it at Sarawakian kopitiam or at a friend's mum's place after she gave birth. It's chicken braised in ang chao, which is red glutinous rice wine, cooked down with sesame oil and ginger until the sauce is fragrant and slightly sticky. At 530 calories for a solid serving, it's not a diet dish. The two big calorie drivers are the sesame oil, about 180 calories from just 1.5 tablespoons, and the chicken thigh and drumstick fat. On the protein side, 30g is decent. Worth flagging: these numbers are a ballpark estimate because hard nutritional data for this dish basically doesn't exist, so there's genuine uncertainty of maybe plus or minus 60 to 80 calories per serving.","What actually moves the number is mostly the oil. A soup-style version with more wine broth and less sesame oil drops the count to around 380 calories, especially if it's served with mee sua noodles instead of rice. The rich confinement version with extra sesame oil and wine can hit 680 calories for the same portion size. If you're eating it at home or at a kopitiam, ask for less oil in the braising if you can. Choosing breast over thigh would cut maybe 40 to 60 calories from the chicken portion. The ang chao wine and dregs themselves only add about 75 calories from the 70g in a standard serving, so the wine isn't the villain here."],"lighter":["Ask for the soup-style version with more wine broth and less sesame oil. That alone can bring it down to around 380 calories.","Skip the mee sua noodles if they come on the side. They add calories fast and you probably don't need them if you're already eating rice.","If you're cooking at home, cut the sesame oil to 1 tablespoon instead of 1.5. You still get the flavour but lose about 60 calories from the dish.","Use chicken breast instead of thigh and drumstick. Less flavour, yes, but if you're counting calories the swap saves you 40 to 60 calories per serving.","Eat it with plain steamed rice and a pile of sayur manis or other low-calorie veg on the side. The dish pairs well with greens and you'll stay full without needing a second helping."],"variants":[{"name":"Soup-style (more wine broth, less oil)","calories":380,"note":"Served with extra red wine broth and mee sua noodles; lower oil ratio reduces calories"},{"name":"Standard braised (kopitiam/home style)","calories":530,"note":"Typical Sibu Foochow version with sesame oil and ang chao dregs, 2-3 bone-in pieces"},{"name":"Rich confinement version (extra sesame oil and wine)","calories":680,"note":"Postpartum confinement preparation uses more sesame oil and wine per serving for nourishment"}],"components":[{"name":"Chicken thigh/drumstick (bone-in, cooked, ~180g raw)","grams":180,"calories":245},{"name":"Sesame oil (~1.5 tbsp used in braising)","grams":20,"calories":180},{"name":"Red glutinous rice wine and dregs (ang chao)","grams":70,"calories":75},{"name":"Ginger and aromatics","grams":30,"calories":25}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Ayam Masak Arak (Rice Wine Chicken)?","a":"Around 530 calories per serving of 2 to 3 bone-in chicken pieces with wine sauce, roughly 300g total. That's 30g protein, 6g carbs, and 35g fat. The sesame oil is the main calorie driver at about 180 calories on its own."},{"q":"What is Ayam Masak Arak exactly?","a":"It's a Foochow Chinese dish from Sibu, Sarawak where chicken is braised in ang chao (red glutinous rice wine) with sesame oil and ginger. It's traditionally a confinement dish eaten after childbirth but you'll also find it at Sarawakian kopitiam. The sauce has a slightly sweet, winey, deeply aromatic flavour from the wine dregs and sesame oil cooked together."},{"q":"Is Ayam Masak Arak a healthy dish?","a":"It depends what you mean by healthy. The protein is solid at 30g, the carbs are low at 6g, and ginger has its merits. But 35g fat and 530 calories for a side dish means it adds up fast if you're not careful with portions. The soup-style version at around 380 calories is a lighter option if you're watching your intake."}],"related":["sarawak-laksa","kolo-mee","manok-pansoh"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The soup-style variant is listed at 380 kcal and described as including mee sua noodles, but the tips section tells readers to skip the mee sua noodles because 'they add calories fast'. These two claims contradict each other and will confuse readers trying to make the lighter choice."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the variants table, clarify that the 380 kcal soup-style figure is for the chicken and broth only, without mee sua. Or adjust the tips section to say mee sua is included in the 380 kcal estimate so readers understand the count already accounts for it. Simplest fix: remove the mee sua reference from the variant table description so the 380 kcal figure is unambiguously chicken-and-broth only, and the tip to skip mee sua makes clean sense.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"butter-prawns","name":"Butter Prawns","category":"lauk","serving":"1 restaurant portion (6-8 medium-large prawns, shell-on, with egg floss)","servingGrams":220,"calories":580,"protein":28,"carbs":14,"fat":44,"metaDescription":"Butter Prawns clock around 580 calories per restaurant portion. The butter and egg floss add up fast. Full breakdown inside.","summary":"Shell-on prawns deep-fried then wok-tossed in a rich butter, curry leaf and egg-floss sauce, which is as good as it sounds and as calorie-heavy as you'd expect.","answer":"A restaurant portion of Butter Prawns (6-8 medium-large shell-on prawns with egg floss) comes in at around 580 calories, with 28g protein, 14g carbs and 44g fat. The prawns themselves are not the problem; it's the butter, the deep-frying oil and those crispy egg-yolk strands that stack the numbers fast. Note that this is a ballpark estimate because reliable lab data for this dish is thin, so treat it as a realistic guide rather than gospel.","realTalk":["Butter Prawns is one of those dishes where the main ingredient is genuinely good for you and then the cooking method absolutely wrecks the macro profile. You get prawns, which are lean and high-protein, but they go into a deep fryer first, then get tossed in a wok with a generous amount of butter, evaporated milk, curry leaves, and egg-yolk floss fried in more butter. That egg floss, only 10g of it, contributes around 110 calories on its own. The 30g butter base adds another 215 calories. So out of 580 total calories, roughly 460 come from fat sources. If you've never had this dish, picture crispy golden prawns coated in a fragrant, slightly creamy sauce with delicate egg strands that melt on your tongue. It's genuinely one of the better things Malaysian Chinese cuisine has produced. Worth eating. Just not every day, lah.","The version you order matters a lot. A home-cooked pan-fried version with the same butter and egg floss but without deep-frying sits around 280 calories, basically half the restaurant number, because you skip the 135 calories of absorbed frying oil. The salted egg version pushes up to 640 calories because salted egg yolks replace or supplement the egg floss and those yolks are very fat-dense. Portion size is another big variable. A restaurant order is typically shared between two or three people alongside rice and vegetables, so your actual intake might be 180-200 calories per person if you split it properly. The calorie estimates here are best guesses since standardised data for this dish is genuinely hard to find, so treat the numbers as a solid ballpark rather than exact figures."],"lighter":["Share it properly. Split one restaurant portion between two people and you're looking at roughly 290 calories each, which is perfectly manageable.","Order the pan-fried version when you can, or ask at hawker stalls. Skipping the deep-fry step cuts about 135 calories straight away.","Eat it with plain steamed rice, not fried rice. The dish already has enough fat going on, you don't need more from the rice.","Go easy on the sauce at the bottom of the plate. A lot of the butter and oil pools there. You can leave it and lose maybe 50-80 calories.","Skip the salted egg upgrade. The regular egg floss version saves you around 60 calories per portion and honestly tastes just as good."],"variants":[{"name":"Home-cooked, pan-fried (no deep fry)","calories":280,"note":"Prawns pan-fried or stir-fried instead of deep-fried; less oil absorbed, same butter and egg floss; typical home recipe portion"},{"name":"Restaurant butter prawn with egg floss (deep-fried)","calories":580,"note":"Standard hawker/restaurant style: shell-on prawns deep-fried then wok-tossed in butter-curry-leaf-egg-floss sauce"},{"name":"Salted egg butter prawn","calories":640,"note":"Salted egg yolks added to or replacing egg floss; richer and saltier sauce, slightly higher calories from additional egg yolk fat"}],"components":[{"name":"Prawns (deep-fried, ~120g edible meat)","grams":160,"calories":200},{"name":"Butter (sauce base, ~30g)","grams":30,"calories":215},{"name":"Oil absorbed during deep-frying (~15ml)","grams":15,"calories":135},{"name":"Egg yolk floss (2 yolks fried in butter)","grams":10,"calories":110},{"name":"Cornstarch coating + oats/nestum + aromatics","grams":25,"calories":55}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Butter Prawns?","a":"A standard restaurant portion of Butter Prawns, which is 6-8 medium-large shell-on prawns with egg floss sauce, contains around 580 calories. A home-cooked pan-fried version is closer to 280 calories, and the salted egg variant goes up to about 640 calories."},{"q":"What exactly is Butter Prawns and what makes it so rich?","a":"Butter Prawns is a Malaysian Chinese dish where shell-on prawns are deep-fried then wok-tossed in a sauce of butter, evaporated milk and curry leaves, finished with crispy egg-yolk floss. The combination of deep-frying oil absorbed by the prawns plus the butter sauce base is what drives the calorie count so high. The egg floss alone adds around 110 calories to the dish."},{"q":"Is Butter Prawns a good source of protein or should I avoid it when dieting?","a":"The prawns themselves are solid protein at around 28g per portion, which is decent. The problem is the fat coming in at 44g, mostly from butter and frying oil. It's not a dish to avoid entirely, but it's better treated as an occasional order and shared between people rather than a solo serving. Pair it with plain rice and vegetables and you've got a reasonable meal overall."}],"related":["sambal-udang","salted-egg-crab","nestum-chicken"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The 580 kcal headline is plausible for a deep-fried shell-on butter prawn restaurant portion, but the \"Where the calories come from\" breakdown items sum to 715 kcal (200+215+135+110+55), not 580. That 135 kcal gap is visible to any reader who adds the rows. Either the headline or the breakdown needs adjusting to match.","factualConcerns":["The calorie breakdown rows sum to 715 kcal (Prawns 200 + Butter 215 + Oil 135 + Egg floss 110 + Coating 55 = 715) but the headline and nutrition table both state 580 kcal. This internal inconsistency is a factual/arithmetic error in the page."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Either reduce the breakdown items to sum to 580 kcal (e.g. cut the butter line to ~80 kcal for ~11g butter, or reduce oil absorption to ~60 kcal, and adjust egg floss to ~60 kcal for ~1.5 yolks, bringing the total in line) or raise the headline to 715 kcal and update the nutrition table and all references accordingly. The simpler fix is to adjust the breakdown rows: Prawns 200, Butter 130, Oil 80, Egg floss 85, Coating 55 = 550, then round the headline to 550 kcal. Or trim butter to ~11g (80 kcal), oil to 80 kcal absorbed, keep prawns at 200, floss at 110, coating at 55 = 525, headline ~525. The exact numbers are estimates anyway; the key is that the rows must add up to whatever the headline says.","severity":"major"}},{"dish":{"slug":"chili-crab","name":"Chili Crab","category":"lauk","serving":"1 serving, about half a medium mud crab with sauce","servingGrams":250,"calories":450,"protein":25,"carbs":20,"fat":22,"metaDescription":"Chili crab is about 450 kcal per half crab with sauce. The sweet chili gravy and wok oil do the heavy lifting, not the crab meat itself.","summary":"Mud crab wok-tossed in a sweet, spicy tomato and chili sauce thickened with egg, served with mantou or rice and absolutely covered in sauce you will not stop mopping up.","answer":"Half a medium mud crab with sauce comes in at around 450 kcal, with 25g protein, 20g carbs, and 22g fat. The crab meat itself is actually lean at about 100 kcal for 120g edible meat. The calories come mostly from the wok oil (roughly 180 kcal per portion) and the sweet chili and ketchup sauce base (about 105 kcal for 60g). So yes, you are basically paying 280 kcal just for the sauce and oil that makes the whole thing taste incredible.","realTalk":["Chili crab is a Singapore and Malaysian seafood classic, a whole mud crab cracked open and cooked in a glossy, slightly sweet, spicy tomato and chili gravy thickened with beaten egg. It looks dramatic, tastes amazing, and is genuinely a mess to eat. At 450 kcal for half a crab with sauce, it is not the worst thing in the world, but do not let the word \"crab\" fool you into thinking this is some low-calorie health food. The crab meat is lean, sure. But the sauce is built on ketchup, sweet chili sauce, and a generous pour of wok oil, and that is where most of the damage happens. Worth flagging: these numbers are a ballpark estimate because solid nutritional data for chili crab is basically non-existent. Different hawkers use different amounts of oil and sauce, so treat 450 kcal as a reasonable middle-ground, not gospel.","What actually moves the number is the oil and the sauce. Some stalls are heavy-handed with the wok oil, which alone can push your portion past 200 kcal before you even count the crab. The sweet chili and ketchup base adds another 100-plus kcal for 60g of sauce, and people mop that up. The bread is where it gets serious though: two steamed mantou add about 160 kcal, taking you to 610 kcal total. Two fried mantou, which soak up oil, push it to 650 kcal. Swap to a bowl of white rice and you are at 680 kcal. If you want to keep it lower, eat the crab plain with no bread and go easy on scooping up the gravy. The crab meat itself is genuinely high protein and not the problem here."],"lighter":["Skip the mantou entirely. Two fried mantou alone add 200 kcal on top of your 450 kcal crab, which is a big chunk for bread you are mostly using to mop sauce.","Go easy on the sauce. I know it is the best part, but 60g of that sweet chili gravy is already 105 kcal. Leave some in the pot instead of chasing every last drop.","Order steamed mantou over fried if you need the bread. Steamed is roughly 80 kcal per bun versus fried, which absorbs oil and costs more.","Share the crab. A whole mud crab with two people plus shared rice keeps your individual total way more manageable than going solo.","Pair it with a plain steamed vegetable dish, something like kailan or kangkung, instead of rice or noodles to keep the overall meal calories reasonable while still feeling full."],"variants":[{"name":"Pepper crab (black pepper version)","calories":400,"note":"Less sweet sauce, similar oil, slightly lower carbs from no sweet chili base"},{"name":"Chili crab, crab only no bread","calories":450,"note":"Half mud crab with sauce, no accompaniment"},{"name":"Chili crab with steamed mantou (2 pcs)","calories":610,"note":"Two steamed buns add roughly 160 kcal"},{"name":"Chili crab with fried mantou (2 pcs)","calories":650,"note":"Fried mantou absorb oil and add roughly 200 kcal"},{"name":"Chili crab with white rice (1 bowl)","calories":680,"note":"One bowl of rice (200g cooked) adds about 230 kcal"}],"components":[{"name":"Crab meat, cooked (approx 120g edible)","grams":120,"calories":100},{"name":"Wok oil used in cooking (per portion)","grams":20,"calories":180},{"name":"Sweet chili sauce and ketchup (sauce base)","grams":60,"calories":105},{"name":"Tomato passata, egg, aromatics (sauce body)","grams":50,"calories":55}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Chili Crab?","a":"About 450 kcal for half a medium mud crab with sauce, roughly 250g. That breaks down to 25g protein, 20g carbs, and 22g fat. Add steamed mantou and you are at 610 kcal, fried mantou brings it to 650 kcal, and a bowl of rice takes it to 680 kcal."},{"q":"What exactly is chili crab?","a":"It is a whole mud crab cooked in a wok with a thick, sweet and spicy sauce made from chili, ketchup, tomato, and egg. The sauce is glossy, slightly tangy, a bit sweet, and very orange. You eat it with your hands, crack the shell yourself, and use bread or rice to mop up the sauce. It is messy and very worth it."},{"q":"Is chili crab actually healthy?","a":"The crab meat itself is lean and high in protein, 120g of edible meat is only about 100 kcal. The problem is the sauce and wok oil, which together add close to 285 kcal per serving. So it is decent protein for the calories, but not a low-calorie meal, especially once you add bread or rice on the side."}],"related":["salted-egg-crab","kam-heong-crab","sambal-udang"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["Minor internal inconsistency: the FAQ answer for 'Is chili crab actually healthy?' states sauce and wok oil together add 'close to 285 kcal per serving', but the calorie breakdown table adds up to 340 kcal from non-crab components (180 oil + 105 sauce base + 55 aromatics/egg). The 285 figure appears to silently drop the aromatics row. Not a factual error about the dish, but the numbers do not reconcile within the page."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the third FAQ answer, change \"which together add close to 285 kcal per serving\" to \"which together add close to 340 kcal per serving\" to match the component breakdown shown on the page.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"ikan-pari-bakar","name":"Ikan Pari Bakar (Grilled Stingray)","category":"lauk","serving":"1 serving, hawker stall (stingray wing in banana leaf with sambal)","servingGrams":320,"calories":430,"protein":44,"carbs":10,"fat":22,"metaDescription":"Ikan Pari Bakar clocks around 430 calories per hawker serving. The stingray is lean, it's the sambal and grilling oil that add up. Full breakdown here.","summary":"Stingray wing grilled in banana leaf and smothered in sambal belacan, a hawker classic that is honestly one of the better high-protein options at the stall if you watch the oil.","answer":"One hawker serving of Ikan Pari Bakar (a full stingray wing in banana leaf with sambal belacan, about 320g) comes to around 430 calories, with 44g protein, 10g carbs, and 22g fat. The fish itself is surprisingly lean at only 195 calories for the whole wing portion. The sambal and the oil absorbed during grilling are doing most of the calorie work.","realTalk":["Ikan pari bakar is stingray wing grilled directly on a charcoal or gas flame, wrapped in banana leaf and heaped with sambal belacan made from dried shrimp paste, chilli, and lime. It is a hawker staple all over the peninsula, most famously in Melaka and the seafood town stalls along the coasts. The fish itself is lean and meaty, which is why the protein number (44g) is genuinely impressive for a single dish. The catch is that the calories are sitting at 430 because of 160 calories worth of cooking oil absorbed during grilling and another 75 from the sambal. Be honest: this is a solid protein source dressed up in a fairly oily sauce. These numbers are a ballpark estimate because detailed nutrition data for stingray specifically is thin, so treat the figures as a reasonable middle ground rather than lab-certified truth.","What actually changes the maths is how generous the stall is with oil and sambal. A home-style version with minimal basting and a thinner sambal layer can bring things down to around 320 calories for the same wing. On the other end, some pasar malam stalls add a margarine or extra oil baste on top, which pushes it past 550 calories. The wing size matters too: a bigger wing at 270g of flesh instead of 230g adds roughly 40 to 50 more calories from the fish alone. If you are eating this with rice, that is another 250 to 350 calories depending on how much you scoop, so plan accordingly. The good news is you can ask the stall to go light on the sambal and skip any butter baste, and you are looking at something closer to 350 calories with 44g protein, which is a pretty solid deal lah."],"lighter":["Ask the stall to go easy on the oil baste. Some places douse the whole thing before serving. Just ask, most will oblige.","Skip or halve the sambal topping. The sambal belacan adds about 75 calories and a good chunk of the sodium. A smaller scoop still gives you all the flavour.","Eat it with plain steamed rice or extra kangkung rather than nasi lemak or fried rice. The fish itself is doing the heavy lifting protein-wise.","Avoid the pasar malam versions with margarine or butter baste on top. That one extra step adds well over 100 calories to an already decent dish.","Share one wing between two people and order an extra plate of blanched vegetables. The portion is filling enough to split, and you get more variety on the table for the same calories."],"variants":[{"name":"Light sambal, less oil","calories":320,"note":"Home-style or health-conscious version with minimal oil, thinner sambal layer"},{"name":"Standard hawker serving","calories":430,"note":"Typical hawker stall portion: full wing, generous sambal belacan, some oil from grilling"},{"name":"Extra sambal + butter basted","calories":550,"note":"Some stalls add margarine or extra oil baste on top of the sambal; popular at pasar malam stalls"}],"components":[{"name":"Stingray wing flesh (cooked, ~230g)","grams":230,"calories":195},{"name":"Sambal belacan (chilli, belacan, lime)","grams":60,"calories":75},{"name":"Cooking oil absorbed during grilling","grams":18,"calories":160}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Ikan Pari Bakar (Grilled Stingray)?","a":"A standard hawker serving of Ikan Pari Bakar, one stingray wing in banana leaf with sambal belacan (around 320g), comes to about 430 calories. That is 44g protein, 10g carbs, and 22g fat. These are estimates as detailed nutrition data for stingray is limited, but they represent a reasonable middle-ground figure for a typical hawker portion."},{"q":"What exactly is Ikan Pari Bakar?","a":"Ikan pari is stingray and bakar means grilled. The wing is laid on a banana leaf, smothered in sambal belacan (a paste of dried shrimp, chilli, and lime), then grilled over charcoal or gas until the banana leaf chars at the edges and the fish is cooked through. It is a hawker classic across Malaysia, particularly popular at seafood stalls in Melaka, Klang, and coastal towns. The flesh is firm, slightly sweet, and soaks up the spicy sambal really well."},{"q":"Is Ikan Pari Bakar a healthy option for someone trying to lose weight?","a":"Honestly, it is one of the better things you can order at a hawker stall if protein is your goal. 44g protein for 430 calories is a good ratio. The fish itself is lean, and the calories mainly come from the sambal and grilling oil, both of which you can ask to be reduced. Compared to a plate of char kuey teow or curry laksa at similar or higher calories but much less protein, this comes out ahead. Just watch the rice portion you pair it with."}],"related":["ikan-bakar","sambal-sotong","asam-pedas"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["Sambal belacan is described as 'a paste of dried shrimp, chilli, and lime' in both the FAQ and the body. Belacan is fermented shrimp paste, not simply dried shrimp, and a standard sambal belacan recipe also includes shallots and often garlic. The description is a simplification but unlikely to mislead the target audience; flag as minor only."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the FAQ and body text, change \"a paste of dried shrimp, chilli, and lime\" to \"a paste of fermented shrimp paste (belacan), chilli, shallots, and lime\" for accuracy.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"nasi-kukus","name":"Nasi Kukus","category":"rice","serving":"1 plate (steamed rice + 1 piece ayam goreng berempah + sambal + sides)","servingGrams":450,"calories":700,"protein":36,"carbs":79,"fat":27,"metaDescription":"Nasi Kukus is about 700 calories per plate, steamed rice with crispy spiced fried chicken. Here is what drives the count and how to order smarter.","summary":"Steamed rice served with ayam goreng berempah and sambal, a classic at mamak stalls and East Coast hawker spots where the crispy spiced fried chicken is the whole point.","answer":"A standard plate of Nasi Kukus comes in at around 700 calories: 220g of steamed rice (285 kcal), one piece of ayam goreng berempah at 150g (300 kcal), sambal at about 55 kcal, and the usual sides of ikan bilis, cucumber and tauge adding another 50 kcal. The fried chicken alone is doing nearly half the work here, so that is where the number lives.","realTalk":["Nasi Kukus is basically steamed rice served with a piece of heavily spiced, deep-fried chicken (ayam goreng berempah), sambal, and a small pile of sides. It sounds simple but that one piece of fried chicken is pulling 300 calories on its own because it is coated in rempah, battered to some degree, and dropped in a wok of oil. The rice and sambal are pretty tame by comparison. At 700 calories for a full plate with decent protein at 36g, it is not a disaster of a meal, honestly. If you were 120kg and eating two of these plates, yes, that is a problem. But one plate fits into a sensible day without much drama. Fair warning though: calorie data for nasi kukus is thin and the numbers here are a ballpark estimate based on component weights. Different stalls use different amounts of oil and rempah, so treat 700 kcal as a reasonable middle ground, not a lab-certified figure.","The single biggest lever is the gravy. If you go kuah banjir style, where the uncle floods your rice with curry gravy, you are looking at around 1,100 calories instead of 700. That extra ladle of curry adds close to 400 kcal in one move. Ordering a second piece of ayam goreng berempah bumps you straight to 980 calories. If you want to eat lighter, skip the ikan bilis (saves you roughly 70 calories and a bit of fat from the frying), ask for less sambal, and stick to one piece of chicken. The steamed rice portion is actually quite reasonable at 220g for 285 kcal, so the rice is not your enemy here. It is the oil in the chicken and any add-on gravy that pushes the number up fast."],"lighter":["Stick to one piece of ayam goreng berempah and skip any extra chicken. Going from one to two pieces adds 280 calories.","Say no to kuah banjir. That curry gravy pour-over adds close to 400 calories in one move and you barely notice it taste-wise.","Skip the ikan bilis goreng if you are watching calories. It is fried anchovies, which adds fat you do not really need when the chicken already has plenty.","Load up on the cucumber and tauge sides instead. They add almost no calories and actually help you feel fuller.","If the stall lets you choose your chicken cut, go for a drumstick over a thigh, same weight but slightly less fat in the skin."],"variants":[{"name":"Without ikan bilis / plain sambal only","calories":630,"note":"Skip the fried anchovies, lighter sambal portion; rice and chicken stay the same"},{"name":"Standard plate (1 piece ayam, sambal, ikan bilis)","calories":700,"note":"Typical mamak or East Coast hawker serving"},{"name":"Ayam goreng berempah x2 pieces","calories":980,"note":"Two pieces of fried chicken; common if ordering extra or at kedai nasi"},{"name":"Kuah banjir (curry gravy flooded over rice)","calories":1100,"note":"Adding generous ladle of curry gravy roughly doubles the calorie count; referenced at ~1115 kcal by Malaysian sources"}],"components":[{"name":"Steamed rice (nasi kukus, ~220g cooked)","grams":220,"calories":285},{"name":"Ayam goreng berempah (spiced fried chicken, ~150g, 1 piece)","grams":150,"calories":300},{"name":"Sambal (~40g, 2-3 tbsp)","grams":40,"calories":55},{"name":"Sides: ikan bilis goreng + cucumber + tauge (~40g)","grams":40,"calories":50}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Nasi Kukus?","a":"A standard plate of Nasi Kukus is around 700 calories: 285 kcal from the steamed rice, 300 kcal from one piece of ayam goreng berempah, 55 kcal from the sambal, and about 50 kcal from the ikan bilis and vegetable sides."},{"q":"What exactly is Nasi Kukus?","a":"Nasi Kukus means steamed rice, but the dish is really about the ayam goreng berempah, which is a deep-fried chicken piece coated in a blend of spices like turmeric, lemongrass, and galangal. It is popular at mamak stalls and East Coast hawker places in Malaysia, and you will also find it commonly in Sabah. The rice is plain steamed, but the chicken is anything but."},{"q":"Is Nasi Kukus healthy for someone trying to lose weight?","a":"One plate at 700 calories with 36g of protein is actually a decent meal for weight loss, as long as you stop at one plate. The fried chicken is high in fat from the oil, so it is not the leanest protein source, but the portion is not huge. The danger is adding curry gravy (jumps to 1,100 kcal) or ordering two pieces of chicken (980 kcal). Keep it to one piece, no gravy flood, and it fits into a calorie deficit without feeling like punishment."}],"related":["nasi-lemak","nasi-kandar","nasi-goreng-kampung"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The real talk section says the chicken is 'battered to some degree', which is imprecise. Ayam goreng berempah is marinated and coated in rempah paste then deep-fried, not battered. Minor but worth tightening.","The lighter-eating tip claims a drumstick has slightly less fat in the skin than a thigh. Drumsticks and thighs have broadly similar fat content and this claim is not clearly supported. It is borderline misleading as dietary advice."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the real talk paragraph, change \"battered to some degree, and dropped in a wok of oil\" to \"coated in rempah paste and deep-fried\". In the tips list, remove or rewrite the drumstick-vs-thigh tip, or soften it to \"the difference is small either way\" rather than implying a clear fat advantage.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"daun-ubi-tumbuk","name":"Daun Ubi Tumbuk","category":"veg","serving":"1 hawker/warung side-dish serving","servingGrams":170,"calories":270,"protein":12,"carbs":9,"fat":20,"metaDescription":"Daun ubi tumbuk is around 270 calories per warung serving. The cassava leaves are basically free, but the santan does the heavy lifting calorie-wise.","summary":"Pounded young cassava leaves slow-cooked with thick coconut milk, lemongrass, and ikan bilis, a staple side dish in Sarawak and Sabah that tastes humble but hits harder than it looks.","answer":"A standard hawker or warung serving of daun ubi tumbuk (about 170g) comes in at roughly 270 calories, with 12g protein, 9g carbs, and 20g fat. The leaves themselves are almost nothing calorie-wise. It is the santan that carries the bulk of the calories, contributing around 135 calories out of the 270 on its own.","realTalk":["Daun ubi tumbuk is basically pounded cassava leaves cooked down in coconut milk with aromatics and usually ikan bilis or anchovies. If you have never had it, think of it as Sarawak and Sabah's answer to a braised greens side dish, except the gravy is rich, slightly fatty, and really good with rice. At 270 calories for a side dish, it is not exactly a free food, but it is not crazy either. The cassava leaves themselves only contribute about 30 calories. The santan is doing 135 calories of work all by itself. Be honest with yourself about how much gravy you are ladling over your rice. Fair warning on the numbers: this is a genuine ballpark estimate because there is basically no hard nutritional data published for this specific dish, and every warung makes it differently.","What actually moves the maths is simple: how much santan and how much oil. The tanpa santan version (just oil, garlic, anchovies) drops to around 120 calories for the same-sized serving, saving you 150 calories. The light coconut milk version sits at 180 calories. Go full masak lemak penuh with generous santan and maybe some udang or offal thrown in and you are looking at 400 calories from a side dish alone. The ikan bilis adds about 45 calories but also gives you a decent protein hit for the size. If you are eating this at a nasi campur counter, watch the portion size because the serving spoon will give you whatever it gives you, and the gravy tends to be generous."],"lighter":["Ask for tanpa santan or kurang santan if you are at a place that makes it fresh, you can drop from 270 to 120-180 calories easily.","Eat it with plain white rice rather than nasi lemak or nasi minyak. The dish is already fatty, no need to stack coconut rice on top.","Treat it as a condiment, not a main. Two big spoonfuls of the dish alongside a leaner protein like ikan stim or ayam bakar keeps total calories in check.","Skip extra gravy. The leaves are fine, it is the pooled santan at the bottom of the tray that inflates the number. Take the solids.","Pair it with a clear soup rather than another santan-based lauk. Your body does not know which meal the fat came from, so keep the rest of the plate lighter."],"variants":[{"name":"Tanpa santan (stir-fried with oil only)","calories":120,"note":"No coconut milk, just oil, garlic, anchovies; much lower fat"},{"name":"Santan nipis (light coconut milk)","calories":180,"note":"Diluted coconut milk gravy, common in lighter home cooking"},{"name":"Santan pekat biasa (standard hawker)","calories":270,"note":"Typical warung/kopitiam version with moderate thick coconut milk and ikan bilis"},{"name":"Masak lemak penuh (full gulai style)","calories":400,"note":"Restaurant-style with generous thick coconut milk, oil, and protein additions like udang or perut"}],"components":[{"name":"Pounded cassava leaves, cooked","grams":80,"calories":30},{"name":"Thick coconut milk (santan) absorbed","grams":45,"calories":135},{"name":"Ikan bilis / anchovies","grams":15,"calories":45},{"name":"Aromatics (shallots, lemongrass, turmeric, chili)","grams":20,"calories":20},{"name":"Cooking oil","grams":5,"calories":45}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Daun Ubi Tumbuk?","a":"A typical hawker or warung serving of daun ubi tumbuk (170g) is around 270 calories, with 12g protein, 9g carbs, and 20g fat. The coconut milk accounts for the majority of those calories."},{"q":"What exactly is daun ubi tumbuk?","a":"It is young cassava leaves that get pounded, then cooked slowly in coconut milk with lemongrass, shallots, turmeric, chili, and usually ikan bilis or salted fish. It is a classic side dish in Sarawak and Sabah, commonly eaten with rice at warung and nasi campur stalls."},{"q":"Is daun ubi tumbuk a healthy vegetable dish?","a":"The cassava leaves themselves are high in fibre and protein for a leafy green. The dish gets a solid 12g of protein partly from the ikan bilis. The catch is the thick santan, which pushes the fat to 20g and the total to 270 calories for a side dish. It is fine in context, just do not assume it is calorie-free because it is listed under vegetables."}],"related":["kangkung-belacan","sayur-manis","midin"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The 270 kcal total is believable, but the stated 12g protein is on the high side for 15g ikan bilis and 80g cooked cassava leaves. A realistic figure is closer to 7-9g. Not a blocking error given the explicit estimate caveat, but worth noting.","factualConcerns":["The variant table labels the heaviest version 'masak lemak penuh (full gulai style)'. Masak lemak is a West Malaysian (Minang) cooking style term. For a Sabah/Sarawak dish this would more naturally be called masak santan or simply gulai. Minor framing issue, does not affect the calorie numbers."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Change the protein figure from 12g to 9g in both the answer-box body text and the FAQ schema/HTML. Optionally relabel the heavy-santan variant from \"masak lemak penuh (full gulai style)\" to \"masak santan pekat / gulai style\" for regional accuracy.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"wild-boar","name":"Wild Boar (Babi Hutan)","category":"lauk","serving":"1 serving wild boar dish (meat + gravy, no rice)","servingGrams":290,"calories":460,"protein":54,"carbs":5,"fat":23,"metaDescription":"Wild boar curry (babi hutan) comes in at around 460 calories per serving, with 54g protein. Non-halal. Here is what drives those numbers.","summary":"Wild boar, or babi hutan, is a non-halal game meat popular in Sabah and Sarawak, cooked as curry, stir-fry with bamboo shoot, or smoked, and it is leaner and gamier than the farmed pork you know.","answer":"A standard serving of wild boar dish (about 290g of meat and gravy, no rice) comes to roughly 460 calories, with 54g protein, 23g fat, and only 5g carbs. That is for the curry version, which is the base estimate here. Smoked wild boar (sinalau bakas) drops to around 310 calories, while adding santan to the curry pushes it up to 540 calories.","realTalk":["Babi hutan is a Borneo thing, mainly Sabah and Sarawak, and it is worth knowing about if you have never had it. This is wild game, not farmed pork, so the meat itself is leaner and a lot gamier in flavour. At 460 calories for a curry serving, the number is solid for what you get: 54g of protein is genuinely impressive, and the carbs are almost nothing since it is just meat and spices. The fat at 23g is moderate, not alarming. Honestly this is a pretty decent dish for a dieter if you are not Muslim and you happen to be eating in Sabah or Sarawak. Fair warning though: hard nutrition data for wild boar cooked the Malaysian way is basically nonexistent, so these numbers are a ballpark estimate based on the closest data available. Do not treat them as gospel.","What really shifts the maths here is the cooking method and the gravy. Sinalau bakas, the smoked version where the meat is just salted and dried over fire, skips most of the oil entirely and comes in at about 310 calories for a similar portion. The moment you add a curry paste and cooking oil, you are looking at 108 extra calories just from the oil absorbed into the dish. Throw in coconut milk and the whole thing jumps to 540 calories. So if you are watching numbers, ask for the stir-fry with bamboo shoot (tumis babi hutan) at around 400 calories, go easy on the gravy, and skip the santan version. Pair it with rice and a full plate sits around 720 calories, which is fine for a meal as long as you account for it."],"lighter":["Order sinalau bakas where available. The smoked preparation skips the curry paste and most of the oil, cutting about 150 calories from the same amount of meat.","Skip the santan. Coconut milk adds roughly 80 calories to an already decent curry, and the dish honestly tastes great without it.","Go easy on the gravy. Most of the oil calories pool in the sauce, so scoop the meat and leave some of the liquid behind.","Ask for the bamboo shoot stir-fry version (tumis babi hutan) instead of curry. Less gravy, less oil, about 60 fewer calories.","Watch your rice portion. The meat itself is high protein and low carb, so do not cancel it out with a mountain of rice. Stick to 150g cooked rice instead of 200g and you save around 65 calories from the full plate total."],"variants":[{"name":"Sinalau bakas (smoked wild boar, plain)","calories":310,"note":"Salted and smoked only, minimal oil, ~200g serving; very lean preparation, no sauce"},{"name":"Stir-fry with bamboo shoot (tumis babi hutan)","calories":400,"note":"Light stir-fry with oil and aromatics, bamboo shoot adds bulk, less gravy than curry"},{"name":"Wild boar curry (kari babi hutan)","calories":460,"note":"Standard curry with spice paste, some oil; base estimate for this entry"},{"name":"Wild boar curry with coconut milk (santan)","calories":540,"note":"Richer curry with added santan; common in some Sarawak preparations, higher saturated fat"},{"name":"Wild boar curry + white rice (full plate)","calories":720,"note":"Above curry dish plus 200g cooked white rice (~260 kcal); typical full meal order"}],"components":[{"name":"Wild boar meat, cooked (~200g)","grams":200,"calories":320},{"name":"Cooking oil absorbed into dish","grams":12,"calories":108},{"name":"Curry paste / spices / gravy base","grams":50,"calories":50},{"name":"Bamboo shoots or vegetables","grams":50,"calories":15}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Wild Boar (Babi Hutan)?","a":"A standard serving of wild boar curry (about 290g of meat and gravy, no rice) is around 460 calories. Smoked wild boar (sinalau bakas) is closer to 310 calories, while wild boar curry with coconut milk reaches about 540 calories. Add 200g of white rice and a full plate is around 720 calories."},{"q":"What is babi hutan and is it halal?","a":"Babi hutan literally means wild forest pig. It is game meat hunted in the jungles of Sabah, Sarawak, and Brunei, and it is a staple in the indigenous communities of Borneo. It is non-halal, so this one is only for non-Muslim diners. The meat is leaner and gamier than farmed pork, and it is commonly cooked as a curry, stir-fried with bamboo shoot, or smoked over fire as sinalau bakas."},{"q":"Is wild boar good for weight loss?","a":"Actually not bad, if you choose the right preparation. At 54g of protein per serving and only 5g of carbs, the meat itself is lean and filling. The calories mainly come from cooking oil and gravy. Stick to the smoked or stir-fried versions, go easy on the sauce, and it is a genuinely high-protein meal that fits into a calorie deficit without too much fuss."}],"related":["sinalau-bakas","manok-pansoh","rendang"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The headline 460 kcal is plausible for 290g wild boar curry. However the related-foods card lists sinalau bakas (smoked wild boar, ~200g) at 560 kcal, while the main body of this same page states sinalau bakas is ~310 kcal for a similar portion. That is a ~250 kcal internal contradiction for the same dish. The 310 kcal figure is consistent with lean smoked game meat (~155 kcal/100g); 560 kcal would imply ~280 kcal/100g which is closer to fatty pork. The related-card figure looks like a cross-page data error and should match the 310 kcal stated in the body.","factualConcerns":["The page describes babi hutan as game meat from the jungles of Sabah, Sarawak, and Brunei. Including Brunei is inaccurate: wild boar hunting and eating as a culinary tradition belongs to the indigenous communities of Malaysian Borneo (Sabah and Sarawak). Brunei is a Muslim-majority sultanate where wild boar consumption is not a mainstream food tradition in the same way."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"1. In the related-foods card for sinalau bakas, change 560 kcal to 310 kcal (or align it with whatever the sinalau-bakas.html page uses as its headline number, and reconcile both pages so they agree). 2. Remove \"and Brunei\" from the babi hutan description in the FAQ and hero summary, leaving just \"Sabah and Sarawak.\"","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"sago-gula-melaka","name":"Sago Gula Melaka","category":"dessert","serving":"1 bowl (hawker/kopitiam)","servingGrams":250,"calories":370,"protein":2,"carbs":62,"fat":13,"metaDescription":"Sago Gula Melaka has around 370 calories per hawker bowl. The gula melaka syrup and thick santan are where most of it comes from, not the sago.","summary":"Chilled sago pearls drowned in fragrant palm sugar syrup and poured over with thick coconut milk, served cold and way too easy to finish in two minutes.","answer":"A hawker bowl of Sago Gula Melaka comes in at around 370 calories for a 250g serving. The sago pearls themselves are pretty light at 155 calories for 120g, but the gula melaka syrup adds another 115 calories and the thick santan poured on top contributes 125 calories. So basically, you are paying most of your calorie tab to the syrup and coconut milk, not the sago. Total carbs sit at 62g, fat at 13g, and protein at just 2g.","realTalk":["Sago Gula Melaka is one of those desserts that looks innocent because it is mostly translucent pearls in a bowl, but the two liquids poured over it are doing the heavy lifting calorie-wise. Gula melaka is real palm sugar, fragrant and genuinely delicious, and santan is full-fat coconut milk. Together they account for 240 out of the 370 calories. To be straight with you, the 370 figure here is a ballpark estimate because there is very little hard published data specifically for this dish, and portion sizes vary a lot between hawkers. A light version with minimal santan might be closer to 260 calories, a generous Nyonya-style pour can push to 460. If you have never had it before, think of it as the Malaysian answer to a pudding dessert, chilled, slightly sweet, with that distinct smoky-caramel note from the palm sugar.","What actually moves the numbers is how heavy-handed the hawker is with the syrup and santan. A kopitiam that free-pours thick coconut milk adds way more than one where you get a thin drizzle. If you ask for less santan, you realistically knock off around 80 to 110 calories just from that one change, dropping the bowl to roughly 260 to 290 calories. The sago itself is almost entirely starch with negligible protein or fat, so there is no meaningful way to swap it out and still eat the same dish. Where you can control things: portion size matters more than anything else here. A small half-bowl at 125g would put you around 185 calories, which is perfectly reasonable as a dessert after a proper meal."],"lighter":["Ask for less santan or santan on the side. That single move saves you 80 to 110 calories without touching the gula melaka flavour.","If the place lets you choose your syrup amount, go half. The gula melaka is potent enough that half the syrup still gives you that full palm sugar hit.","Treat it as dessert, not a snack on top of dessert. A bowl after a protein-forward meal is fine. Two bowls because it is light-looking is not.","Order the small size if available. A 125g portion is roughly 185 calories, which is a very different number from the standard 250g hawker bowl.","Pair it with a zero-calorie drink like plain water or unsweetened iced Chinese tea instead of teh tarik, so the dessert stays the indulgence and does not stack onto an already heavy drink order."],"variants":[{"name":"Light / less santan","calories":260,"note":"Minimal coconut milk, closer to HealthHub SG published figure; sometimes served at health-conscious cafes"},{"name":"Standard hawker bowl","calories":370,"note":"Typical kopitiam or pasar malam serving with normal gula melaka syrup and a proper pour of thick santan"},{"name":"Rich version (extra santan + gula melaka)","calories":460,"note":"Restaurant or Nyonya-style with extra thick santan and generous palm sugar syrup; closer to recipe-based calculations"}],"components":[{"name":"Cooked sago pearls (120g)","grams":120,"calories":155},{"name":"Gula melaka syrup (palm sugar dissolved, ~30g sugar equiv.)","grams":55,"calories":115},{"name":"Thick coconut milk / santan (60ml)","grams":60,"calories":125}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Sago Gula Melaka?","a":"A standard hawker bowl of Sago Gula Melaka (250g) is around 370 calories. That breaks down to 155 calories from the sago pearls, 115 from the gula melaka syrup, and 125 from the thick coconut milk. Lighter versions with less santan can be as low as 260 calories, while a rich Nyonya-style serve can hit 460."},{"q":"What exactly is Sago Gula Melaka?","a":"It is a chilled Malaysian dessert made from cooked sago pearls, which are small translucent starch balls, served with gula melaka syrup made from palm sugar and poured over with thick coconut milk. You get it at kopitiams, pasar malams, and Nyonya restaurants. It is sweet, fragrant, and usually served cold."},{"q":"Is Sago Gula Melaka a healthy dessert?","a":"Honestly, it is not a diet food, but it is not the worst dessert either. At 370 calories per bowl it is lighter than most cakes or fried desserts, but it is almost entirely carbs and fat with barely any protein. If you ask for less coconut milk you can bring it down to around 260 calories, which is reasonable as an occasional treat."}],"related":["cendol","bubur-cha-cha","ais-kacang"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The headline 370 kcal is plausible for a 250g hawker bowl, but the three listed components (155 + 115 + 125) sum to 395 kcal, not 370. The internal breakdown does not add up to the stated total.","factualConcerns":["The calorie component breakdown (155 kcal sago + 115 kcal gula melaka syrup + 125 kcal santan) sums to 395 kcal, but the stated total throughout the page is 370 kcal. Either the total or one of the component figures needs correcting to be internally consistent."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Either adjust the total to 395 kcal (and update the answer box, nutrition table per-100g figure from 148 to 158 kcal, and the meta description), or reduce one component figure so the parts add to 370 kcal (e.g. reduce santan to 100 kcal, giving 155 + 115 + 100 = 370). The simplest fix that keeps the headline round number intact is to change the santan line from 125 to 100 kcal.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"air-barli","name":"Air Barli","category":"drinks","serving":"1 glass (hawker/kopitiam)","servingGrams":300,"calories":100,"protein":1,"carbs":24,"fat":0,"metaDescription":"Air barli from a kopitiam glass is about 100 calories, almost all from sugar. Here's what you're actually drinking and how sweet matters.","summary":"Air barli is a Malaysian barley drink made by boiling pearl barley with rock sugar until the water turns slightly milky and sweet, often served cold at kopitiams and hawker stalls.","answer":"A standard 300ml kopitiam glass of air barli comes in at about 100 calories. Most of that is from the dissolved rock sugar, around 48 calories from roughly 2.5 teaspoons of sweetener. The pearl barley itself contributes maybe 42 calories of starch that leaches into the water during boiling. So yeah, this is basically flavoured sugar water with a faint grain taste. Not terrible in the grand scheme of Malaysian drinks, but the calories are almost entirely carbs (24g), zero fat, barely any protein.","realTalk":["Air barli is one of those drinks you grab at the kopitiam when you want something that feels healthy, and honestly it's not wrong to think that way. Compared to teh tarik or Milo ais, 100 calories for a full glass is pretty decent. The drink is just boiled pearl barley water sweetened with rock sugar, sometimes with a squeeze of lemon if you order barli limau. The barley grain barely shows up calorie-wise since most of it stays solid and you're drinking the water, not eating the grain. To be upfront: these numbers are a ballpark estimate because solid nutrition data for kopitiam air barli is pretty thin, and sweetness levels vary from stall to stall.","What actually moves the calorie count here is simple: sugar. A lightly sweetened glass sits around 100 calories, but a generous auntie who pre-sweetens a whole pot heavily can push you to 130 calories without you knowing. Barli kosong with no sugar at all drops to around 30 calories, basically just hot grain water. Barli limau adds maybe 10 calories from the lemon and sometimes a touch more sugar to balance the sourness, landing around 110 calories. If you order frequently, asking for kosong or kurang manis is an easy way to shave 50 to 70 calories per glass across a week without feeling like you're giving up anything."],"lighter":["Order barli kosong or kurang manis. No sugar or less sugar drops it from 100 calories down to 30 to 60, and the drink still tastes like barley water.","Barli limau kosong is a solid option. The lemon gives it enough flavour that you don't miss the sweetness as much.","Skip the refill at the self-serve stalls. That second glass is another 100 calories you probably didn't plan for.","Drink it hot. Hot drinks tend to get sipped slower, so you drink less overall.","If you're making it at home, use stevia or skip the sweetener entirely. The barley starch gives a faint natural sweetness on its own."],"variants":[{"name":"Barli kosong (plain, no sugar)","calories":30,"note":"Boiled pearl barley water with no sweetener; mostly water with trace starch"},{"name":"Air barli standard (hawker)","calories":100,"note":"Typical kopitiam glass with rock sugar, moderately sweet, 300ml"},{"name":"Barli limau (lemon barley)","calories":110,"note":"Same as standard with a squeeze of lemon juice; negligible calorie difference, sometimes slightly more sugar added to balance tartness"},{"name":"Barli manis (extra sweet)","calories":130,"note":"Hawker version with generous rock sugar; common at some stalls that pre-sweeten a large pot heavily"}],"components":[{"name":"Added rock sugar / sugar (approx 2.5 tsp dissolved)","grams":12,"calories":48},{"name":"Pearl barley soluble starch in liquid","grams":15,"calories":42},{"name":"Water (base of drink)","grams":273,"calories":10}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Air Barli?","a":"A standard kopitiam glass of air barli (300ml) is around 100 calories. The sugar is the main source, roughly 48 calories from about 2.5 teaspoons of dissolved rock sugar. Order it kosong and it drops to about 30 calories."},{"q":"What is air barli exactly?","a":"Air barli is pearl barley boiled in water with rock sugar until the liquid turns slightly cloudy and sweet. You drink the liquid, not the grain. It's a common kopitiam and hawker stall drink served cold or hot, sometimes with lemon (barli limau). It's been around Malaysian coffee shops forever."},{"q":"Is air barli a healthy drink?","a":"Compared to teh tarik (around 130 to 180 calories) or Milo ais (around 180 to 200 calories), 100 calories for a full glass is actually on the lower end for kopitiam drinks. There's no fat and no real protein either, so it's essentially sweetened water. It's fine as an occasional drink. If you want it to work for a diet, ask for kosong or kurang manis."}],"related":["teh-tarik","milo-ais","cincau"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["In the 'Where the calories come from' breakdown, water (273g) is assigned 10 kcal. Water has zero calories. This appears to be a fudge to make the three rows add to 100, but labelling plain water as contributing 10 kcal is technically wrong. The 10 kcal should be folded into the barley starch row or the table total should be presented as approximately 90 kcal with an 'other trace' note.","The related-foods card for Milo Ais shows 250 kcal, but the FAQ prose on the same page states Milo ais is around 180 to 200 calories. Minor internal inconsistency that could confuse readers."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the bd-row for water, either remove the 10 kcal entry entirely (combine with the barley starch row as a trace amount) or label it as \"Other trace starch / minerals\" at 10 kcal rather than \"Water\". Also reconcile the Milo Ais related card (250 kcal) with the FAQ prose (180-200 kcal) by picking one figure and using it consistently.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"teh-o-limau","name":"Teh O Limau Ais","category":"drinks","serving":"1 glass (hawker, normal sweet)","servingGrams":250,"calories":70,"protein":0,"carbs":17,"fat":0,"metaDescription":"Teh O Limau Ais has about 70 calories per glass, almost all from sugar. Order kurang manis and you drop it to 40 cal. Here is the full breakdown.","summary":"Iced black tea with calamansi lime and sugar, no milk, making it one of the lighter drink options at any mamak or kopitiam.","answer":"A standard glass of Teh O Limau Ais is around 70 calories, almost entirely from the sugar the hawker adds. The black tea itself contributes basically nothing (about 2 calories), and the lime juice adds maybe 8. So the whole drink is basically just sweetened lime tea over ice. Order kurang manis and you are down to about 40 calories. These are ballpark estimates as hard data for this specific drink is thin, but the numbers are in the right range.","realTalk":["Teh O Limau Ais is iced black tea with calamansi lime and sugar, no condensed milk involved. That is exactly why it is lighter than teh tarik, which can clock 130 to 180 calories from condensed milk alone. At 70 calories for a standard glass, it is honestly one of the better drink choices at a mamak. The problem is the word 'standard' does different things in different shops. Some hawkers have a heavy hand with the sugar, and a manis-heavy pour can push you to 100 calories easily. These numbers are estimates, bro, there is no lab testing happening here, so treat them as a ballpark.","What actually moves the calorie count is purely the sugar. The tea and lime are basically free calories. Two and a half teaspoons of sugar gets you to 70 calories. Four teaspoons lands at 100. One teaspoon (kurang manis) puts you at about 40. If you are tracking, just say kurang manis every single time and you shave off 30 calories per glass. Over a day of two or three drinks, that adds up. Nothing else changes here, no oil, no protein, no noodle swap to worry about."],"lighter":["Order kurang manis every time, drops you from 70 to about 40 calories per glass.","If you want zero sugar, ask for teh o limau kosong. Pure tea and lime, basically zero calories.","Stick to one glass per meal rather than getting free refills at those all-you-can-drink spots.","Compare to teh tarik at 130 to 180 calories or milo ais at 150 plus, and teh o limau ais is already the smart pick lah.","Squeeze extra lime yourself if the stall has fresh limes on the table. More lime flavour means you notice the sweetness less even with less sugar."],"variants":[{"name":"Kurang manis (less sugar)","calories":40,"note":"About 1 tsp sugar, common order for calorie-conscious; roughly half the carbs"},{"name":"Teh O Limau Ais (standard)","calories":70,"note":"Typical hawker pour, ~2-2.5 tsp sugar, no milk"},{"name":"Teh O Limau Ais manis (extra sweet)","calories":100,"note":"4+ tsp sugar, some hawkers default to this sweetness level"}],"components":[{"name":"Sugar (2.5 tsp added by hawker)","grams":10,"calories":40},{"name":"Brewed black tea (Boh/Ceylon, ~200ml)","grams":200,"calories":2},{"name":"Calamansi or lime juice (2-3 limes, ~25ml)","grams":25,"calories":8},{"name":"Ice (melts into drink)","grams":15,"calories":0}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Teh O Limau Ais?","a":"About 70 calories for a standard hawker glass at normal sweetness. Kurang manis brings it down to around 40 calories, and extra sweet can push it to 100. Almost all the calories come from sugar."},{"q":"What exactly is Teh O Limau Ais?","a":"It is iced black tea with calamansi lime juice and sugar, no milk. The 'O' means black (no milk) and 'limau' is lime. You will find it at pretty much every mamak, kopitiam, and hawker centre in Malaysia."},{"q":"Is Teh O Limau Ais a healthy drink choice?","a":"Compared to most Malaysian drinks it is one of the better options. No condensed milk, no Milo powder, no coconut milk. The only real calories come from sugar, so just say kurang manis and you have a 40-calorie drink that tastes good and keeps you hydrated. Not bad at all."}],"related":["teh-tarik","milo-ais","cincau"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The headline is 70 kcal but the ingredient breakdown rows sum to only 50 kcal (Sugar 40 + Tea 2 + Lime 8 + Ice 0). The prose then claims \"Two and a half teaspoons of sugar gets you to 70 calories\" which is wrong: 2.5 tsp sugar is roughly 10g = 40 kcal, not 70. Either the headline should be ~50 kcal, or the sugar weight in the breakdown should be raised to ~17g to match 70 kcal. 70 kcal is not implausible for this drink but the internal numbers do not agree with each other.","factualConcerns":["The ingredient breakdown rows sum to 50 kcal (40+2+8+0) but the headline and answer box state 70 kcal. The prose compounds this by saying '2.5 tsp of sugar gets you to 70 calories' when 2.5 tsp is ~10g = 40 kcal, not 70 kcal.","The 'How to eat it lighter' tips section says 'milo ais at 150 plus' but the related-foods card on the same page shows Milo Ais at 250 kcal. The two numbers on the same page contradict each other."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Two options: (A) Change the headline to 50 kcal and update the answer box, meta description, and sugar-statement in the prose to match the breakdown. (B) Keep 70 kcal but change the breakdown sugar row to ~17g / 68 kcal and update the prose to say roughly 4 tsp. Also change \"milo ais at 150 plus\" in the tips list to \"milo ais at 250 plus\" to match the related card.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"air-mata-kucing","name":"Air Mata Kucing","category":"drinks","serving":"1 glass (hawker/kopitiam, iced)","servingGrams":300,"calories":180,"protein":1,"carbs":44,"fat":0,"metaDescription":"Air Mata Kucing has around 180 calories a glass at the hawker stall, mostly from rock sugar. Here is what you are actually drinking.","summary":"Air Mata Kucing is a classic Chinese herbal cooling drink made by boiling dried longan, monk fruit (luo han guo), and rock sugar together, then serving it iced.","answer":"A standard glass of Air Mata Kucing at your typical hawker stall or kopitiam clocks in at around 180 calories for a 300ml iced serving. Almost all of that comes from rock sugar: 40g dissolved into the drink alone contributes 154 calories. The dried longan bits you eat at the bottom add another 17. The monk fruit and winter melon that give it that herbal edge? Basically zero. So yeah, it tastes light and refreshing but calorie-wise it drinks more like a soft drink than plain water.","realTalk":["Air Mata Kucing, which literally means \"cat's eye tears\" (named after the dried longan, not actual cats, relax), is a traditional Chinese cooling drink you find all over Malaysia. It is boiled from dried longan, monk fruit, sometimes winter melon strips, and a very generous hand with the rock sugar. The hawker version runs at about 180 calories per glass, and the honest truth is that is a ballpark estimate because hard nutritional data for this drink is basically nonexistent. Nobody has lab-tested the Petaling Street auntie's recipe. That said, the math is simple: rock sugar is still sugar, at roughly 4 calories per gram, and most stalls are not shy with it.","What actually swings the number is how heavy the sugar hand is. The home-cooked version using just 25g of rock sugar across a 1500ml pot works out to about 80 calories per glass, less than half the hawker version. The monk-fruit-only version drops to around 50 calories but good luck finding that at a stall. Bottled versions like Yeos or F&N sit around 120 calories because commercial production controls the sugar level. The ice dilutes the drink somewhat at the hawker stall but does not change the calorie count in any meaningful way since the sugar is already dissolved in the base liquid."],"lighter":["Ask for less sugar (kurang manis) if the stall lets you customise. Some will, some won't.","Make it at home. Use 20 to 25g of rock sugar per 1500ml of water instead of the 80 to 100g a typical hawker batch uses. Your glass drops from 180 to around 80 calories.","Go for the bottled version (Yeos, F&N) on the go. Around 120 calories and the sweetness is at least consistent.","Drink it slow and eat the longan bits at the bottom. That way you feel like you got something out of the glass instead of just chugging sugar water.","Compare it to your other drink options at the stall. At 180 calories it is better than a can of Coke (139 calories for 330ml) by not much, but it is way worse than plain iced barley or teh o kosong."],"variants":[{"name":"Home-cooked (less sugar)","calories":80,"note":"Made with 25g rock sugar per 1500ml, 6 servings; much lighter than hawker version"},{"name":"Hawker stall, iced (standard)","calories":180,"note":"Petaling Street style, heavily sweetened with rock sugar; ice dilutes somewhat"},{"name":"Bottled / packaged (can or packet)","calories":120,"note":"Commercial versions like Yeos or F&N; sweetness controlled but still sugary"},{"name":"No rock sugar (monk fruit only)","calories":50,"note":"Sweetened only by monk fruit and natural longan sugars; rare at hawker stalls"}],"components":[{"name":"Rock sugar (dissolved in drink)","grams":40,"calories":154},{"name":"Dried longan pulp (eaten)","grams":18,"calories":17},{"name":"Winter melon (dissolved solids)","grams":30,"calories":4},{"name":"Monk fruit (luo han guo, negligible)","grams":2,"calories":5}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Air Mata Kucing?","a":"A standard hawker or kopitiam glass (about 300ml, iced) has around 180 calories. The vast majority comes from dissolved rock sugar, not the longan or monk fruit. Home-cooked versions can be as low as 80 calories if you go easy on the sugar."},{"q":"What exactly is Air Mata Kucing?","a":"It is a traditional Chinese herbal cooling drink made by simmering dried longan (the fruit), monk fruit (luo han guo), sometimes winter melon, and rock sugar in water, then serving it chilled or iced. The name means cat's eye tears in Malay, a reference to the small dried longan fruit. You find it at Chinese herbal drink stalls, kopitiam, and pasar malam all over Malaysia."},{"q":"Is Air Mata Kucing a healthy drink?","a":"It is not a bad choice compared to a teh tarik or sirap bandung, but it is not plain water either. The monk fruit has some antioxidant properties and the drink has zero fat, but 44g of carbs from sugar in one glass is real. If you are watching your calories, the home-cooked low-sugar version or a monk-fruit-only version is genuinely decent. The hawker stall version, though, is basically sweetened herbal water."}],"related":["teh-tarik","cincau","air-barli"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The tips section says Air Mata Kucing at 180 cal is 'better than a can of Coke (139 calories for 330ml) by not much.' The Coke figure of 139 kcal for 330ml is correct, but that makes AMK worse than Coke calorically, not better. The framing is misleading and should be corrected."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the tips list, change \"At 180 calories it is better than a can of Coke (139 calories for 330ml) by not much\" to something like \"At 180 calories it is actually worse than a can of Coke (139 calories for 330ml), which is a bit of a reality check for something that feels so light and herbal.\"","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"ais-krim","name":"Ais Krim (Local Ice Cream)","category":"dessert","serving":"1 stick (potong)","servingGrams":65,"calories":80,"protein":1,"carbs":14,"fat":3,"metaDescription":"Ais krim potong is about 80 calories per stick. Durian flavour hits 110 cal, red bean stays at 70. Here is the full breakdown.","summary":"Ais krim potong is the OG Malaysian ice cream on a stick, made with coconut milk and sugar, sold in flavours like red bean, corn, coconut, and durian.","answer":"One stick of ais krim potong is about 80 calories for a standard 65g bar. That is for your typical corn or coconut flavour. Red bean comes in lower at around 70 calories, while durian can push up to 110 calories per stick because durian puree is genuinely calorie-dense. The base is mostly sugar syrup and santan, so the calories are almost entirely carbs and fat, with barely any protein.","realTalk":["Ais krim potong is the frozen treat you grew up eating from a styrofoam box at the school canteen or from a guy on a bicycle. It is a block of flavoured ice cream frozen on a stick, made with coconut milk, sugar syrup, and whatever filling goes in it, red bean, corn, pandan, or durian. At 80 calories a stick, it is actually one of the more reasonable desserts you can have. The calories come mainly from sugar (46 cal from sweeteners alone) and santan (21 cal). Durian is the only one you need to watch, at 110 cal per stick, because durian puree adds real density.","The math changes almost entirely by flavour choice. Red bean potong sits at 70 cal and the red bean filling actually adds a bit of fibre. Corn is 80 cal. Coconut is 85 cal, slightly richer santan base. Durian is 110 cal. So if you swap your durian stick for a red bean one you save 40 calories, which is real. Portion size also matters because some artisan or pasar malam versions are bigger than the standard 65g bar. A 90g homemade-style potong could easily be 120 cal. Stick to the individually wrapped commercial ones if you want predictable numbers."],"lighter":["Pick red bean flavour over durian. That swap alone cuts about 40 calories per stick.","Eat one stick, not two. Sounds obvious but pasar malam sellers sometimes push you to buy three for some deal.","Skip the big slab versions sold at dessert shops. Those are usually 150 cal or more per serving.","Buy the individually wrapped commercial bars like King's Potong where the calorie count is printed on the label.","Eat it as a standalone treat, not after a full meal. At 80 cal it is a fine dessert on its own."],"variants":[{"name":"Red bean (kacang merah)","calories":70,"note":"Lowest sugar, red bean filling adds fibre; F&N King's entry 64-70 cal"},{"name":"Corn (jagung)","calories":80,"note":"Sweet corn adds natural sugar; about 77 cal per 60g bar"},{"name":"Coconut (kelapa)","calories":85,"note":"Slightly richer coconut milk base; roughly 80-90 cal per stick"},{"name":"Durian","calories":110,"note":"Durian puree is calorie-dense; King's Potong Durian 100-120 cal per stick"}],"components":[{"name":"Sugar and glucose syrup","grams":12,"calories":46},{"name":"Coconut milk (santan)","grams":10,"calories":21},{"name":"Starch, milk powder, fillings (red bean/corn/pandan)","grams":8,"calories":28}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Ais Krim (Local Ice Cream)?","a":"One standard stick of ais krim potong is about 80 calories (65g). Red bean flavour is around 70 cal, corn is 80 cal, coconut is 85 cal, and durian is the highest at around 110 cal per stick."},{"q":"What exactly is ais krim potong?","a":"It is a traditional Malaysian ice cream frozen in a rectangular block on a wooden stick. The base is coconut milk (santan) and sugar, with fillings like red bean, sweet corn, pandan, or durian. You will find it at school canteens, pasar malam, and convenience stores."},{"q":"Is ais krim potong healthy compared to other desserts?","a":"For a Malaysian dessert, it is actually on the lighter end. At 70 to 110 calories per stick it beats a bowl of cendol (around 250 cal) or ais kacang (200 plus cal) by a wide margin. The downside is it has almost no protein, so it is a treat, not a meal."}],"related":["cendol","ais-kacang","bubur-cha-cha"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["FAQ says cendol is 'around 250 cal' and ais kacang is '200 plus cal', but the related-foods cards on the same page show cendol at 330 kcal and ais kacang at 350 kcal. The internal numbers contradict each other. The comparison dessert figures in the FAQ should match the related cards."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the third FAQ answer, change \"around 250 cal\" to \"around 330 cal\" and \"200 plus cal\" to \"around 350 cal\" to match the related-cards data on the same page.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"cempedak-goreng","name":"Cempedak Goreng","category":"snacks","serving":"1 piece","servingGrams":75,"calories":185,"protein":2,"carbs":26,"fat":8,"metaDescription":"Cempedak Goreng is about 185 calories per piece. Richer than pisang goreng, the batter and frying oil do most of the damage. Here's the full breakdown.","summary":"Cempedak Goreng is deep-fried battered cempedak fruit, like pisang goreng's richer cousin with a stronger, sweeter flavour and more indulgent bite.","answer":"One piece of Cempedak Goreng (about 75g) comes in at around 185 calories, with 26g carbs, 8g fat, and basically 2g protein. The fruit itself is only about 52 calories. The batter adds another 55 and the oil absorbed during frying pushes it over the line with 135 calories on its own. So yeah, it's the cooking method doing most of the damage, not the fruit.","realTalk":["Cempedak is a cousin of jackfruit, sweeter and with a stronger smell, and when you fry it in batter it becomes one of those roadside snacks that's genuinely hard to stop at one. At 185 calories a piece, it's not outrageous on its own, but nobody buys just one. Three pieces and you're at 555 calories for what is essentially a snack. Worth knowing: these numbers are a ballpark estimate because solid nutrition data for cempedak goreng is thin. We've broken it down by components (fruit pulp, batter, absorbed oil) and it lands around 185 per piece, but the real number shifts depending on how thick the batter is and how long it drains after frying.","The biggest calorie driver is the oil absorbed during deep frying, clocking in at 135 calories out of 185. Batter thickness matters a lot too. A thin, airy batter absorbs less oil than a thick doughy coat. If you're buying from a stall, pieces vary wildly in size. A small piece might be 60g and around 145 calories. A big fat one could be 100g and closer to 250. If you're tracking, weigh it or just count 185 and move on. There's no lighter version of this dish really, because the whole point is the fry. Pair it with black coffee instead of a sugary drink and at least you're not doubling down on the damage."],"lighter":["Eat one piece and actually stop. One is 185 calories, three is a small meal.","Let it drain on paper towel for a minute before eating. You'd be surprised how much oil comes off.","Buy the smaller pieces at the stall. Size varies a lot and a smaller piece can save you 40 to 60 calories.","Skip the sugary drinks alongside it. Teh tarik adds another 100 plus calories on top.","Eat it as a planned snack, not an impulse add-on to a full meal."],"variants":[],"components":[{"name":"Cempedak fruit pulp (fresh, ~45g)","grams":45,"calories":52},{"name":"Batter coating (rice flour + plain flour mix, ~15g dry)","grams":15,"calories":55},{"name":"Oil absorbed during deep frying","grams":15,"calories":135}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Cempedak Goreng?","a":"One piece of Cempedak Goreng is about 185 calories (75g serving). The fruit itself is only around 52 calories, but the batter adds 55 and the absorbed frying oil contributes another 135, so the cooking method is really what drives the number."},{"q":"What is cempedak goreng?","a":"Cempedak is a tropical fruit closely related to jackfruit, but sweeter and with a stronger aroma. Cempedak Goreng is the fruit coated in a rice flour and plain flour batter and deep fried, same concept as pisang goreng. You'll find it at roadside stalls and pasar malam."},{"q":"Is cempedak goreng healthy?","a":"Not really, but it's not a disaster if you eat it in moderation. The fruit itself has some natural sugars and a bit of fibre. The problem is frying it in batter adds a lot of fat and calories. At 185 calories a piece with only 2g of protein, it's a snack that fills you up on carbs and fat, not much else."}],"related":["pisang-goreng","karipap","cucur-udang"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The headline 185 kcal per 75g piece is plausible, but the component breakdown is internally inconsistent: 52 (fruit) + 55 (batter) + 135 (oil) = 242, not 185. The oil figure of 135 kcal (implying ~15g absorbed oil) is also implausibly high for a snack this size; typical absorbed oil for a deep-fried piece this weight is 35-70 kcal (4-8g oil). Either the total should be ~242 or the oil figure should be reduced to ~78 kcal to make the math reach 185.","factualConcerns":["The calorie component breakdown does not add up: 52 kcal (fruit) + 55 kcal (batter) + 135 kcal (oil) = 242 kcal, but the stated total is 185 kcal. This inconsistency appears in the answer box prose and is repeated verbatim in FAQ 1. The oil absorption figure of 135 kcal (equivalent to roughly 15g absorbed oil per piece) is also unrealistically high for a 75g deep-fried snack."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Fix the breakdown so the components sum to 185: keep fruit at 52 kcal, keep batter at 55 kcal, and reduce the absorbed oil figure from 135 kcal to approximately 78 kcal (about 8-9g oil). Update the prose in the answer box and both FAQ instances to reflect the corrected oil figure. Alternatively, if 242 is the intended total, update the headline and table to 240 kcal and adjust the per-100g figure accordingly.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"ubi-kayu","name":"Ubi Kayu","category":"snacks","serving":"1 serving ubi kayu rebus (boiled cassava with grated coconut and palm sugar syrup)","servingGrams":205,"calories":310,"protein":2,"carbs":57,"fat":9,"metaDescription":"Ubi kayu rebus comes in at around 310 calories for a standard hawker serving. The fried version hits 450. Here is the full breakdown.","summary":"Boiled or fried cassava, a classic Malaysian hawker snack that looks humble but carries more calories than most people expect once the coconut and gula melaka show up.","answer":"A standard serving of ubi kayu rebus, which is boiled cassava topped with fresh grated coconut and a drizzle of palm sugar syrup, comes in at around 310 calories for roughly 205g. Strip it down to plain boiled cassava with no toppings and you are looking at 185 calories. Order the goreng version instead and you are up at 450 calories for a 150g portion of deep fried cassava fingers. The carbs are doing most of the work here at 57g for the rebus serving, so treat it as a proper snack or a small side, not a freebie.","realTalk":["Ubi kayu is just cassava, a starchy root crop that gets boiled until soft and served with grated coconut and gula melaka syrup, or cut into fingers and deep fried until golden. You see it at Ramadan bazaars, pasar malam stalls, and old school kopitiams. It is not a diet food and nobody is pretending it is. The 310 calories in the rebus version come mostly from the cassava itself (185 cal) plus the grated coconut adding another 88 cal, which surprises people because coconut sounds innocent but it is about 60% fat by weight. Fair warning: these are ballpark estimates because there is almost no published lab data for Malaysian hawker-style ubi kayu, so treat the numbers as a reasonable guide rather than gospel.","What actually moves the numbers is the toppings. Plain boiled cassava with nothing on it is 185 calories and genuinely not that heavy for a snack. The moment grated coconut and gula melaka syrup land on top, you add 125 calories and the fat jumps to 9g. If you get the goreng version, oil absorption during deep frying pushes the total to 450 calories and fat to around 22g for just 150g of food. So if you want ubi kayu at a stall, ask for rebus and go easy on the syrup drizzle. That alone saves you 140 to 260 calories compared to the fried version."],"lighter":["Ask for rebus instead of goreng and you cut at least 140 calories straight away, no willpower required.","Request less gula melaka syrup or skip it entirely. The syrup alone adds sweetness and calories you can live without.","Go easy on the grated coconut portion. Ask the uncle for half and you save roughly 40 to 44 calories without losing the flavour entirely.","Treat it as a snack between meals rather than eating it on top of a full meal. At 310 cal for the full rebus serving, it is not a side dish, it is basically a snack meal on its own.","Share a portion. Hawker servings are often generous and splitting it with someone keeps your intake reasonable without skipping it altogether."],"variants":[{"name":"Ubi kayu rebus plain (no coconut or syrup)","calories":185,"note":"Just boiled cassava ~165g, no toppings"},{"name":"Ubi kayu rebus with coconut and gula melaka","calories":310,"note":"Standard hawker serving with grated coconut and palm sugar syrup drizzle"},{"name":"Ubi kayu goreng (deep fried fingers, 150g portion)","calories":450,"note":"Deep fried cassava fingers, golden and crispy; oil absorption raises fat to ~22g"}],"components":[{"name":"Boiled cassava (165g)","grams":165,"calories":185},{"name":"Fresh grated coconut (25g)","grams":25,"calories":88},{"name":"Palm sugar syrup / gula melaka (15g)","grams":15,"calories":35}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Ubi Kayu?","a":"A standard serving of ubi kayu rebus with grated coconut and gula melaka syrup is around 310 calories for about 205g. Plain boiled cassava with no toppings is 185 calories. The deep fried goreng version hits roughly 450 calories for a 150g portion."},{"q":"What exactly is ubi kayu?","a":"Ubi kayu is the Malay name for cassava, a starchy root crop. In Malaysian hawker settings it is usually served one of two ways: boiled soft and topped with fresh grated coconut and palm sugar syrup (rebus), or cut into thick fingers and deep fried until crispy (goreng). Both versions are common at pasar malam and bazaar Ramadan stalls."},{"q":"Is ubi kayu healthy?","a":"It depends on the version and the portion. Plain boiled cassava is not terrible as an occasional snack, it gives you carbs and a bit of fibre. The issue is the toppings: grated coconut adds significant fat and the gula melaka syrup piles on sugar. The fried version is a different story altogether at 450 calories for 150g. Fine as a treat, just do not eat a full plate every day and call it a light snack."}],"related":["pisang-goreng","kuih-seri-muka","onde-onde"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The body text claims fresh grated coconut is 'about 60% fat by weight'. Fresh grated coconut (with its natural moisture content) is around 30-35% fat by weight. The 60% figure applies to dried or desiccated coconut. This is factually wrong, though it does not affect the calorie numbers which are calculated correctly."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"In the \"The real talk\" section, change \"it is about 60% fat by weight\" to \"it is around 33% fat by weight once you account for the moisture in fresh coconut\" (or simply \"fresh grated coconut is surprisingly high in fat\").","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"coconut-shake","name":"Coconut Shake","category":"drinks","serving":"1 cup (Melaka-style, ~400 ml)","servingGrams":380,"calories":270,"protein":4,"carbs":30,"fat":15,"metaDescription":"Coconut shake calories: a Melaka-style cup with ice cream comes in at about 270 kcal. Not your innocent coconut water, bro. Here is the breakdown.","summary":"A Melaka street-food classic, blended from coconut water, scraped young coconut flesh and a scoop of vanilla ice cream, served over ice.","answer":"One cup of Melaka-style coconut shake is about 270 calories for roughly 380g of drink. That scoop of vanilla ice cream is doing most of the damage at 165 kcal on its own, with the coconut flesh adding another 68 kcal and the coconut water contributing just 30 kcal. So it is refreshing, sure, but it is nowhere near as light as plain coconut water. These are ballpark estimates since hard lab data for this specific drink is pretty thin.","realTalk":["If you have been to Jonker Street in Melaka, you have seen the stalls lining up the coconut shells and you know the drill: coconut water, scraped young coconut flesh, one scoop of vanilla ice cream, all blended together with ice. It tastes like something your body deserves after walking around in 34-degree heat. The problem is that scoop of ice cream, which accounts for more than half the calories at 165 out of 270 kcal. The coconut flesh adds another 68 kcal on top. So you are essentially having a dessert that pretends to be a cold drink. For most people having it as a treat on a Melaka day trip, that is totally fine. Just do not convince yourself it is the same as sipping plain coconut water on a hot day, because it is not even close.","The maths here are pretty simple: the ice cream is the variable that matters most. Skip the ice cream and you drop to around 60 calories, just the coconut water and flesh blended with ice. Ask for an extra scoop and you are looking at roughly 370 kcal, more than some people's full meals. Some stalls are more generous than others with the flesh, so the actual calorie count could swing 20 to 30 kcal either way. To be straight with you, these numbers are a solid estimate based on the components, not a lab-verified figure, because reliable data for this specific drink is genuinely hard to find. The core lesson: the drink itself is fine, the ice cream is a choice."],"lighter":["Ask them to skip the ice cream or use half a scoop. You go from 270 kcal down to around 60 to 100 kcal just like that.","Do not upsize. A larger cup almost always means more ice cream and more coconut flesh, not just more water.","Order plain air kelapa (coconut water straight) from a different stall if you just want hydration. That is 30 to 40 kcal and actually more refreshing.","Share one cup with someone. Two people, 135 kcal each, everybody happy.","Have it after a long walk around Jonker Street so at least you have burned some of those calories first before you drink it."],"variants":[{"name":"Plain coconut water only (no ice cream)","calories":60,"note":"Just the coconut water and flesh blended with ice, no ice cream, a much lighter drink"},{"name":"Standard Melaka coconut shake","calories":270,"note":"Coconut water, scraped young coconut flesh, one scoop vanilla ice cream, blended with ice"},{"name":"Extra scoop of ice cream","calories":370,"note":"Some stalls offer a double-scoop version; adds roughly 100 kcal per extra scoop"}],"components":[{"name":"Vanilla ice cream (1 scoop, ~80g)","grams":80,"calories":165},{"name":"Young coconut flesh (scraped, ~70g)","grams":70,"calories":68},{"name":"Coconut water (~150ml)","grams":150,"calories":30},{"name":"Ice (melted, minimal calories)","grams":80,"calories":7}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Coconut Shake?","a":"A standard Melaka-style coconut shake with one scoop of vanilla ice cream is around 270 calories per cup (about 380g). Without the ice cream, it drops to roughly 60 calories."},{"q":"What exactly is a coconut shake?","a":"It is a Melaka specialty: young coconut water and scraped coconut flesh blended with ice and a scoop of vanilla ice cream. The result is a cold, creamy drink that sits somewhere between a smoothie and a dessert. You see it everywhere along Jonker Street."},{"q":"Is coconut shake healthy?","a":"The coconut water and flesh parts are decent, but that scoop of ice cream adds 165 kcal and a fair bit of fat. As an occasional treat on a trip to Melaka, it is fine. As a daily drink thinking it is just coconut water, not great for the calorie budget."}],"related":["teh-tarik","cendol","air-kelapa"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"The headline 270 kcal for a standard cup is plausible. However the variants table claims an extra scoop of ice cream adds only ~100 kcal (270 to 370 kcal), and the prose repeats \"roughly 100 kcal per extra scoop\". If one 80g scoop of vanilla ice cream accounts for 165 kcal, a second scoop of similar size should add closer to 150-165 kcal, putting a double-scoop cup at around 420-435 kcal, not 370. The 100 kcal figure for an extra scoop is roughly half what it should be.","factualConcerns":["Extra scoop of ice cream is stated to add roughly 100 kcal (bringing total from 270 to 370 kcal), but one 80g scoop is already costed at 165 kcal in the breakdown table. A second scoop of similar size would add ~150-165 kcal, making the double-scoop total closer to 420-435 kcal. The 100 kcal and 370 kcal figures are both too low.","The ice row in the breakdown shows 7 kcal for 80g of ice. Plain ice has essentially zero calories. The 7 kcal is unexplained and likely an artefact of the generation process. Trivially small but inaccurate."],"voiceIssues":["No lah, bro, or other Malaysian flavour anywhere in the prose. The CLAUDE.md voice expects 2-3 per post. The writing is good and non-preachy, but it reads slightly more like a careful food writer than a guy at the mamak. Not a hard fail, just noticeably restrained.","The tips section item 'Have it after a long walk around Jonker Street so at least you have burned some of those calories first before you drink it' is the closest to mamak voice but the rest of the tips are generic."],"fixSuggestion":"In the variants table, change the extra-scoop row from 370 kcal to approximately 435 kcal and update the notes from \"adds roughly 100 kcal per extra scoop\" to \"adds roughly 165 kcal per extra scoop\". In the prose (second paragraph of \"The real talk\"), change \"you are looking at roughly 370 kcal\" to \"roughly 435 kcal\". Fix the ice row to 0 kcal. Optionally drop in one bro or lah in the real-talk section to hit the voice target.","severity":"minor"}},{"dish":{"slug":"kaya-toast","name":"Kaya Toast","category":"mamak","serving":"1 set, 2 slices","servingGrams":105,"calories":370,"protein":6,"carbs":44,"fat":19,"metaDescription":"Kaya toast comes in at 370 calories per set. That cold butter slab is doing most of the work. Here's what's really in your kopitiam breakfast.","summary":"Charcoal-toasted white bread slathered with kaya and a thick cold butter slab, served as part of the classic kopitiam breakfast set with soft-boiled eggs and kopi.","answer":"One standard kopitiam kaya toast set (2 slices, about 105g total) comes in at 370 calories. The bread itself is only 160 kcal, the kaya adds 75 kcal, but that thick cold butter slab alone is 130 kcal. So yeah, the toast isn't the problem. The butter is.","realTalk":["Kaya toast is a kopitiam classic. Charcoal-toasted white bread, a generous spread of kaya (that's coconut and pandan jam, very sweet, very Malaysian), and a thick slab of cold butter sandwiched in the middle. At 370 calories for two slices, it's not a light breakfast, but it's also not a disaster on its own. The issue is that 130 of those calories come purely from that butter slab, and the kaya adds another 75 kcal mostly from sugar. The bread is almost the innocent party here.","What actually changes the number: skip the butter and you drop to 235 kcal, a full 135 kcal gone just like that. Go the Ya Kun chain route with 4 thin butter layers instead of one thick kopitiam slab, and you're looking at around 460 kcal for what is essentially the same dish. The kaya portion matters too. A light spread versus a generous slather can swing 20 to 30 kcal easily. And remember, most people pair this with teh tarik or kopi susu, which adds another 80 to 150 kcal on top. The full breakfast set with soft-boiled eggs is probably 500 to 550 kcal total once you count everything."],"lighter":["Ask for kaya only, no butter. You go from 370 down to 235 kcal and the toast is still tasty lah.","Go easy on the kaya spread. A light layer versus a thick one saves you 20 to 30 kcal without ruining the experience.","Swap the kopi susu for teh O kosong (plain black tea, zero calories). That alone saves 80 to 120 kcal from your morning set.","Have one slice instead of two if you're eating it alongside proper food. Half the bread, half the calories from that component.","Pair it with two soft-boiled eggs, which are around 140 kcal for both, rather than a full nasi lemak or curry puff on the side. Keep the rest of the set light and the kaya toast is totally fine."],"variants":[{"name":"Kaya only (no butter)","calories":235,"note":"Some kopitiam offer kaya-only option; removes ~130 kcal from butter"},{"name":"Standard kopitiam (1 thick butter slab)","calories":370,"note":"Typical charcoal toast with one cold butter slab and generous kaya spread"},{"name":"Ya Kun style (4 thin butter slabs)","calories":460,"note":"Chain-style with 4 thin butter layers; Ventrickle TikTok measured 458.9 kcal"},{"name":"Kaya peanut butter toast","calories":420,"note":"Replaces plain butter with peanut butter plus kaya; adds protein, similar or slightly higher calories"}],"components":[{"name":"Charcoal-toasted white bread (2 slices, ~60g)","grams":60,"calories":160},{"name":"Kaya (coconut pandan jam, ~25g)","grams":25,"calories":75},{"name":"Cold butter slab (~18g, kopitiam thick cut)","grams":18,"calories":130}],"faq":[{"q":"How many calories in Kaya Toast?","a":"A standard kopitiam kaya toast set (2 slices with kaya and one cold butter slab, about 105g) is 370 calories. Skip the butter and it drops to 235 kcal. Ya Kun style with 4 thin butter layers runs about 460 kcal."},{"q":"What exactly is kaya toast?","a":"Kaya is a jam made from coconut milk, eggs, and sugar, flavoured with pandan. It's very Malaysian and very good. Kopitiam kaya toast is charcoal-toasted white bread with a thick cold butter slab and a spread of kaya, usually served as a breakfast set with soft-boiled eggs and either kopi or teh tarik."},{"q":"Is kaya toast a healthy breakfast?","a":"It's not a health food, but it's not as bad as some people think, as long as you're not drowning it in butter. At 370 kcal for the toast alone (not counting the drink), it fits into a reasonable breakfast if you keep the rest of your day sensible. The main issue is the butter and the sugar in the kaya, plus whatever you're drinking alongside it."}],"related":["roti-canai","french-toast","teh-tarik"]},"review":{"calorieConcern":"","factualConcerns":["The eyebrow category label reads 'Mamak & Bread' but kaya toast is a kopitiam dish, not a mamak dish. Mamak stalls serve roti canai, mee goreng, teh tarik etc. Kaya toast belongs to the kopitiam/coffee-shop category. Small but a real misclassification that a Malaysian reader would notice."],"voiceIssues":[],"fixSuggestion":"Change the eyebrow label from \"Mamak & Bread\" to \"Kopitiam & Bread\" (or just \"Kopitiam Breakfast\") to accurately reflect that kaya toast is a kopitiam staple, not a mamak item.","severity":"minor"}}];

const CATEGORY_KEYS = ["rice","noodles","mamak","soup","lauk","veg","snacks","dessert","drinks"];

const SCHEMA = {
  type: "object", additionalProperties: false,
  required: ["slug","name","category","serving","servingGrams","calories","protein","carbs","fat","metaDescription","summary","answer","realTalk","lighter","components","faq","related"],
  properties: {
    slug:{type:"string"}, name:{type:"string"}, category:{type:"string",enum:CATEGORY_KEYS},
    serving:{type:"string"}, servingGrams:{type:"integer"},
    calories:{type:"integer"}, protein:{type:"integer"}, carbs:{type:"integer"}, fat:{type:"integer"},
    metaDescription:{type:"string",maxLength:160}, summary:{type:"string"}, answer:{type:"string"},
    realTalk:{type:"array",minItems:2,maxItems:2,items:{type:"string"}},
    lighter:{type:"array",minItems:3,maxItems:5,items:{type:"string"}},
    components:{type:"array",minItems:2,maxItems:5,items:{type:"object",additionalProperties:false,required:["name","grams","calories"],properties:{name:{type:"string"},grams:{type:"integer"},calories:{type:"integer"}}}},
    variants:{type:"array",minItems:0,maxItems:8,items:{type:"object",additionalProperties:false,required:["name","calories","note"],properties:{name:{type:"string"},calories:{type:"integer"},note:{type:"string"}}}},
    faq:{type:"array",minItems:3,maxItems:3,items:{type:"object",additionalProperties:false,required:["q","a"],properties:{q:{type:"string"},a:{type:"string"}}}},
    related:{type:"array",minItems:3,maxItems:3,items:{type:"string"}}
  }
};

const VOICE = "Mamak-friend voice: casual, blunt, warm, specific real numbers, occasional lah/bro/kan used sparingly. NOT a health blog or fitness influencer. ABSOLUTE HARD RULE: NEVER use an em dash or en dash anywhere; use a comma or full stop or the word and. Banned: fuel your body, clean eating, eat clean, wellness journey, nutrient-dense, the word delve, generic openers, and starting a paragraph with Look,.";

phase("Repair")
const fixed = await parallel(FLAGGED.map(item => () => {
  const prompt = [
    "You are repairing ONE page in a Malaysian food calorie database. A reviewer flagged specific issues. Fix ONLY what is needed, keep everything else intact, and return the COMPLETE corrected dish object.",
    "",
    VOICE,
    "",
    "CURRENT DISH OBJECT (JSON):",
    JSON.stringify(item.dish, null, 2),
    "",
    "REVIEWER FINDINGS:",
    "- calorie concern: " + (item.review.calorieConcern || "(none)"),
    "- factual concerns: " + JSON.stringify(item.review.factualConcerns),
    "- voice issues: " + JSON.stringify(item.review.voiceIssues),
    "- suggested fix: " + (item.review.fixSuggestion || "(none)"),
    "",
    "REPAIR RULES:",
    "1. KEEP the headline calories figure as-is (reviewer judged it plausible). Do NOT change calories, protein, carbs, fat, serving, servingGrams, slug, name, or category.",
    "2. The components breakdown MUST sum to within 5 kcal of the headline calories. Adjust component figures so they add up. If you change a component number that the prose mentions, update that sentence so they never contradict.",
    "3. Fix every factual concern the reviewer raised. Correct misleading claims.",
    "4. Fix every voice issue: remove banned phrases, remove any em or en dash, warm up flat writing, fix typos.",
    "5. Do not invent new variants or related slugs. Keep the existing related and variants unless a fix specifically requires it.",
    "6. Keep realTalk to exactly 2 paragraphs, faq to exactly 3 items, related to exactly 3 slugs.",
    "",
    "Return the full corrected dish object with every required field."
  ].join("\n");
  return agent(prompt, { label: "fix:" + item.dish.slug, phase: "Repair", model: "sonnet", schema: SCHEMA });
}));

return fixed.filter(Boolean);
